      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>McDOUGALLS SELF-RAISING FLOUR YORKSHIRE PUDDINGSIOU</head>
        <p>McDOUGALLS SELF-RAISING FLOUR YORKSHIRE PUDDINGSIOU</p>
        <p>bad remained in the service long enough to see many of his ideas realized. There were two matters of urgent administration which he sincerely hoped the Board would not hesitate to carry out at the earliest opportunity. A great responsibility rested upon the Board for the unsatisfactory conditions of things that obtained with reference to the mental cases. It was one of the blemishes on the Union that they were not up-to-date in that respect. Ile had, however, been glad to find an eagerness during the past few years to pioneer changes in that direction and be had no doubt that if the proposals already under consideration became the policy of the Board they would have one of the finest systems of mental classification that it was possible to find in any Union. He also hoped the Board would soon take in band the treatment of phthisis. It was a deplorable fact that the number of such cases had alarmingly increased, particularly in the town of Warrington, during the past few years. With that humanity which bad always been a feature of its policy, be was sure the Board would not hesitate longer than was absolutely necessary before providing suitable accommodation for these cases. They could take it as a sincere expression of his inmost thoughts when he said he Was leaving a staff that was 'second to none in the kingdom. He did not know one officer at whom he could point the finger of doubt. To-day the institution was held in very high repute by the ratepayers outside. They had abundant evidence of that in the fact that the hospital was never free from congestion, and their relieving officers would tell them that people would readily come to the institution for treatment whereas years ago they used to fight shy of it. In conclusion Mr. Awbery wished them all well and tad that whatever might betide, his wife and be would never look at those beautiful presents without feeling the great kindness wh.ch had been shown to them. (Applause). Mr BOTT.MILEY then made the presentation to Dr Taylor. He said all the eflicers who had contributed to the gift were well aware of the great care and atten- tion the doctor had bestowed on their dear friend Mr Awbery during his serious illness, and not only that, but they were well assured that had it been any other officer who had been placed in siruilao circumstances he would have received equal care at Dr Taylor's hands. They were delighted to know that Dr. Taylor ' had decided to live with Mr. Awbery and they knew that in his hands Mr. Awbrey would be quite safe. During the two years Dr. Taylor had been at Warring- ton the Guardians had orpreciated his services very , highly. He had not only done the work in connection with the institution in a highly efficient manner but had proved a friend to all. (Applause). Dr. TA vLon, who was taken by surprise, said he would always treasure the gift as a memento of their kind feelings towards him. He had spent a very happy couple of years at Warrington. It had been sunshine as far as his professional career was concerned, but there were a few clouds, lie referred to the sickness among the nursing staff and,following closely upon that outbreak, the serious illness of his • truest friend, Mr. Awbery. Ile hoped Mr. Awbery would have many happy days, and that he (the speaker) would have the pleasure, in some part, of attecding him and restoring him completely to health. (Applause). A vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Mather and Mr. Bottomley for naking the presentation, on the motion of Mr. AWBERY, seconded by Mr. ToDD. Refreshments were then served, the catering being in the bands of Mrs. Atkinson. Dancing was afterwards resumed and continued until 2 a.m.</p>
        <p>The whole of the arrangements for the function were in the hands of Mr. IV. C. Brcoks.</p>
        <p>„..g.,,v,1 ~,,,,,„,:,,,,;,,,,, ~„,„,,,,,;;,,,,,„,,,,,,0vi,.,,,,,, „,;;;35,,,,,f6,,.,,,,,,, ~.,,,,,,,,,,,,,..,,,iOl , ,0.„,n,;,1,,,i,,, , ~,,,,;,,,,,,,17/ via1.,,,,,,itui,5,, -,,;,,,,„(;c7),, v„,, „,,,,,44, , ,‘ &lt;, ~,,..,,f, "N„..,„,,,, 1 ~.., ~ 7,..1.,,,,:,,,46 .41,1 w. , k ..1, ~,,,li; „,,,,1,.,z;, „„...,..,,,,,,,i,4 c ~ , , ~,,,,,,it. i.1t.....x44 v ~,,, ..., , ,5,4; ,m,..5,c,” , , , ~ ~,:...,*,.,r3.,,ik,,i, otz.iair ‘4...,f1p i1.,.)10.444014,4„, 7 , , 'Pc/ ..14,74,,,, ,w.,,,, .. , .. ~ , • • .1 .; ~ ,Niklaier</p>
        <p>MR. EDWARD TERRY. The famous actor, Mr. Edward Terry, died at his residence at Barnes last iteek after a</p>
        <p>painful illness. Mr. Terry, who was sixty- eight years of age, made his first stage appeararce at Christchurch in 1863. Four years later he found himself in London. Li 1887, he built Terry's Theatre in the Strand. There he produced "Sweet Lavender," with which he is said to have made £50,000, and in which he appeared in the part of Dick Phenyl of indelible memory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WORKHOUSE MASTER'S 1 FAREWELL. PRESENTATIONS FROM GUARDIANS AND OFFICIALS. MR. AWBERY AND WORKHOUSE REFORMS.</head>
        <p>WORKHOUSE MASTER'S 1 FAREWELL. PRESENTATIONS FROM GUARDIANS AND OFFICIALS. MR. AWBERY AND WORKHOUSE REFORMS.</p>
        <p>The dining hall at the Warrington Workhouse has often been the scene of interesting gatherings, but never has it presented a more pleasing spectacle than on Tuesday evening. The occasion was a farewell social given to the Guardians, officers and friends by Mr. and Mrs. J. F. Awbery who will shortly relinquish their appointments as master and matron cf the institution and take up their residence in Switzerland. The fact that their retirement has been necessitated by Mr. Awbery'' long illness has given great regret to their many friends and the rarting function naturally had its sad side. However, everything possible was done to make the evening a pleasantly memorable one. The dining hall was transformed into a place of beauty by an artistic scheme of decoration and shortly after eight o'clock dancing was in full swing, to music supplied by Mr. G. Lomas's band. Many of the dancers were in fancy dress and considerable taste and originality had been shown in the representation of a variety of characters. Some of the more striking portrayals were :—Lady Esmond, Miss Broadbent; Mattador, Mr J Constable; Evening Star, Nurse Ratcliffe; Chinaman, Mr Jones : huzzar (in mess uniform), Mr Clifton ; Giana, Mrs. Constable ; Convict 90, Mr. McHugh. The guests numbered about 110. Mr. C. Wells and Mr. W. C. Brooks were efficient M.C's. For non-dancers whist was the chief attrac- tion. During the intervals songs were given by Messrs C Bylance,F Clare and W McHugh (humorist). The event of the evening was a series of presenta- tions to Mr and Mrs Awbery and Dr Taylor, resident assistant medical officer, who has resigned in order to accompany Mr Awbery as his medical atiendant. G Mather (chairman) on behalf of the Board of Guardians presented Mr and Mrs Awbery with a handsome silver tea tray bearing the following inscrip- tion :—" Presented to Mr and Mrs Awbery by the members of the Warriegton Board of Guardians as a token of es'eem and respect on resigning their posi- tions as master and matron of the Warrington Union Workhouse. April oth, 1012." Mr A Bottomley (clerk) banded Mr and Mrs Awbery a marble time piece, two bronzes and a silver kettle—the gifts if the cfficers- and also presented Dr Taylor with a gold chain, as a mark of esteem from his fellow-officials The members of the Board in attendance at the presentaticn were :—Mr. G. Mather (chairman), Mrs. Collingwood, Mrs. Simpson, Miss Brcadbent, Messrs E L Bent, A.. 1, Jolley. W C Brooks, J A Rushworth, G A Allen, J A Hughes, W Furness, R Lee, and W Flanagan. Amongst others present were : Mr A Bottomley (clerk), Rev W and Mrs Bracecarnp, Rev M Howley, Mr M Nicholls (Chief Constable), Mr J Lister, Mr H U Todd (master of Stone Wollhouse, Staffs, and ' formerly assistant master at Warringtor;, and Mr and Mrs T J Lee. Mr. MATHER, in making the first presentation, said it was the most pleasant duty he had ever performed in the course of his public career. When s public body presented to two of its servants a token of respect and esteem, it was indeed one of the highest honours that any officials could receive. They all deeply regretted that they were losing the services of Mr. and 24;5. Awbery—(hear, het.r)—and the present would serve to remind the recipients of the high opinion which the Guardians bad formed of their administration of the institution and of their devotion to the wcrk.(Applause) Mr. MATHER then handed the tray to Mr. Awbery. Mr A J JOLLEY said that with him the feelings of pain rather than pleasure predominated on that occasion but still there was a somewhat melancholy pleasure in being allowed to take a small part in the proceedings. It was not merely a question, as far es he was person- ally concerned, of parting with the two best officers they had ever had in the Warrington Union ; it was at least the partial loss of two warm personal friend!. (Applause.) The surroundings In which they were gathered were cf a particu- larly pleasing charccter in every respect but one—which was really the main one—that it was in some measure a formal parting with Mr and Mrs Awbery who were not merely personal friends of his own but, he could say without exaggeration, friends of every officer connected with the Union and of e-e,y member of the Board of Guardians—(applause)—and what Was even more essential, though they might not always have been regarded as such, they bad been the true friends of every inmate of that great he titution (Applause). lbet west the noblest testimony that could LI tendered to them in closing what had been a long -and honourable career of public service. (Applause). There were very few public officials in charge of such institutions of whom it could be truthfully said that they carried with them the respect and esteem of allwith whom they came into contact but it could, without any exaggeration, be raid of Mr. and Mrs. Awbery. The circumstances under which they were gathered had their sad side but amongst their pleasant features was the fact, which they all joyfully recognized, that their dear friend Mr. Awbery was showing real , signs of marked improvement. (Applause.) Tie was Lure he was voicing the sentiments of everyone in that room when be expressed the hope that Mr. Awbery might be spared to enjoy long years of happiness, although those years would not be spent iu their immediate vicinity. ifer was sure they would be yams of usefulness, like those who had gore before, for thera had been no trtce of selfishness in Mr. Avvbery's work. Ever since he came to Warrington he bad been Eteadily winning his way into the regard not only of those who bad been brought into direct personal contact with brim but of people outside, because good, honest, faith- ful work such as had been done by Mr. and Mrs. Awbery could not for lorg remain hidden. It bore its fruits and one of the fruits was that Mr. and Mrs. Awbery would carry with them when they left Warr:ngton the sincere and well-deserved respect of a very large pro- portion of the inhabitants of Warrington who had not the honour and pleasure of knowing them personally. (Applause). Mr BOTTOMLEY then made the presentation on behalf of the officers. He said Mr. and Mrs. Awbery bad been connected with Poor Law work for nearly twenty years and bad ueen master and matron of the Warring- ton Workhouse for nine years. Outside their official duties, however, Mr. and Mrs. Awbery bad been veri closely connected with Pour Law administration in England and Wales, and Mr. Awbery had held the highest position in the Poor Law wend that could be held by a master of a Workhouse —that of president of the Masters' Association of Great Britain which included the master of every Workhorse in England and Wales and superin- tendents and other head officials of all institutions governed by the Local Government Board and under the Poor Law. His severance from Poor Law work would be one of the hardest things that could over fall to his lot. Apart from his duty it had been his pleasure and hobby. When people were well-circum- stanced, the only pleasure they could get in life was to devote their time to that which they most appreciated. To Mr. and Mrs. Awbery the best and pleasantest duties they had ever had to perform were in connection with Poor Law work. Their severance from it would be a great wrench to them and it would be keenly felt by their fellow officials. (Applause). Mr AWBERY, who was greeted with warm applause, then acknowledged the gifts. lie said his fir-t thanks were due to Almighty God that He had spared his life to enable him at least to participate in what gleams of happiness one could glean from such a farewell party as that. It seldom fell to the lot of any official, no matter what circumstances might bring about his severance from the work, that the Board which he had served should spontaneously undertake to present him with a token of esteem. Therefore when a public body did take such a step, it emphasised the depth and sincerity of the feelings which they entertained f3r him. He had not alwa)a seen eye to eye with various members of the Board. They bad had their differences and, who was there among them that possessed any grit, ability or determination and would acciniese in every- thing that everybody said to him ? When they had had their encounters in the Board room, he could honestly say that those with whom he had found him- self at variance had admired him for having the courage of his convictions and not hesitating to give utterance to theni, even though it might at the time have lost him caste. The work of the Poor Law was a very great work and only those who were intimately con- nected with it know how difficult it was to fulfil the duties pertaining to public offices without treading upon the corns of some people. (Laughter.) They had to deal with a class of people without parallel elsewhere. They were the derelicts of society, and frequently they were a source of annoyance to society, coming to the Workhouse as a last r.sort. They bad to be handled and their various dispositions and tempers regulated. After all, they were dealing with human flesh and blood, and how to handle such people with the best results was one of the greatest social problems that men or women could set their hands to. (Hear, bear) In many ways be had tried to do his level best in Warrington. As one step, he ban always attempted to identify himself with the outside world. In Poor Law work they could become too institutional, and for one to take part in social life and to associate with various organizations that tended to elevate and uplift society, helped to knock off excresences. He had found such recreation a great help to him, for it had helped him to look at things through different glasses. When be looked round the Institution he saw a great many things he had bad the privilege of sub- mitting to the Guardians for their adoption and he</p>
        <p>LINSISED COMPOUND for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles. 90., Is. lid. Of Chemists.—Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>COLLIERY DISASTER.</head>
        <p>COLLIERY DISASTER.</p>
        <p>Three Miners Killed at Collins Green.</p>
        <p>Six men were buried on Tuesday'morning under a fall of roof at the Conics Green Colliery, near St. Helens and three of them were killed. Thomas Corcoran, of 12, North-street, Earlestown, who had charge of the work, Thomas Astley, and John Pogue were got out badly bruised. The three men who were killed were Bernard M'Adam (31), married, Ilindley-row, Burton- wood ; Michael Oars, single (24), of Derbyshire Hill- road, St. Helens ; and Patrick Stone (32), a married man with three children, of 73, Legh-street, Earlestown. The accident at the Collins Greaa Colliery is regarded by the management as a direct consequence of the strike. The Collins Green and Bold Collieries, which are situated between St Helens and Earlestown, were subject to a great amount of interference during the strike. The roadways and working places of a mine require continual attention, and, in spite of the hest efforts of the management, the sides and roofs collapse very often, with sudden and terrible results. In many of the St. Helens pits no opposition was offered to the employment of a sufficient number of daywagemen to keep the roads in fairly good order. Many pickets were on duty at Bold and Collins Green, and the repeated violent scenes made it impossible to send men down the pit to keep the roadways all right. Conse- quently, when the men descended to work last week- end, many falls and obstructions were found. In the potato seam of Collins Green pit a big fall was found to have occurred.</p>
        <p>The gang of six men began work en Monday night on this fall. The work is exceedingly dangerous and it is carried out under the supervision of one of the working officials of the pit. The amount of light that Can be used to examine the hole in the roof is very limited. The men are obliged to work under the open- ing in the roof, and they are constantly en the strain listening for any movement whicb will indicate that another fall AM take place. In spite of their utmost skill and watchfulness it frequently happens that tons of roof will fall with practically no warning. This was what occurred about 4.50 a.m. on Tuesday. The whole of the six men were buried, and some time elapsed before one of them could struggle loose from the fall awl Eumrrou help. it was found that many tons of roof had fallen, and that the place wag in an exceedingly dangerous state and difficult to ?eel with. The rescue party worked as hard as possible to recover the bodies of the three men under the fall, but at noon there were still two of them embedded in the fallen roof.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CHANCELLOR'S ENGINEER SON.</head>
        <p>CHANCELLOR'S ENGINEER SON.</p>
        <p>The " Daily Telegraph " publishes the following from a correspondent :—Mr. Lloyd George's engineer son is now on the staff Of the Port of London Authority. It has been suggested that there was some patronage in respect of his appointment, but this is untrue. Mr. George, jun , entered for the advertised engineering vacancy in the ordinary way, his name being recorded as Mr. T. L. George. In the ordinary course he was interviewed by one of the high officials connected with the department, and he gave that official his qualifications, which were that he was a graduate of Cambridge, who had taken special engineering certificates, and that he had been at work on the Pearson Works at Dover. He impressed the official as being a very capable young man who knew his hu,inese well, but as he had no qualifications as a member of the Institute of Civil Engineers he was told that he could be given an appointment, but only at a moderate salary. He expressed his willingness to accept this, as such an appointment would enable him to live with " his people in London." Then only did it trans- pire that he was the son of the Chancellor of the Exchequer.</p>
        <p>FOUND. The secret of happiness. It is health. So bear in mind that Steedman's Powders correct the slight stomach troubles which make children so fretful, and their parents depressed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>THOUGHTS ON THE STRIKE</head>
        <p>THOUGHTS ON THE STRIKE</p>
        <p>APPEALS TO FORCE LEAD TO BARBARISM.</p>
        <p>By C. E. Parker.</p>
        <p>Whether the expectations of the workers in connection with the national coal strike have been fulfilled depends entirely upon the expectations. These were of a varied character. One of the extreme leaders of the miners in South Wales said " If a strike takes place the bloodshed of the French Revolution will be a mere fleabite," and again "I am convinced that if a coal strike takes place Monarchy rule in Britain will cease." It was to be "a revolutionary war to a finish," it was to " stagger humanity." Why there was to be bloodshed and revolution was not made clear, and the advantage of staggering humanity was left to the imagination. By some it was expected that the stoppage of the coal supplies would, in a fort- night, bring the country, and especially what is called the capitalist class, to their knees. Property has shown that in comparison with labour it is almost invulnerable. The strike has hit all classes, but the labouring classes most. It has hit capital all round, but not severely, while it has probably hit capital engaged in coal mining least, because the price of coal being most affected, those who held stocks have cleared at enormous profits, and there is the prospect of higher prices in the future. No doubt many a dividend will be lessened or for a time disappear as a result of the strike, but in most cases this is only a question of temporary inconvenience, whereas to many workers it has been, and will for a consider- able time continue to be, a question of priva- tion. Had not public opinion through the Government intervened, there is little doubt the men would have been beaten. That is if the masters had utilized to the full their staying power. The point of exhaustion would have been reached sooner by the men than the masters.</p>
        <p>Strikes are, like other forms of war, stupid. They do not settle the question in dispute any more than war does. A war determines which army is best equipped and led. A strike may settle which side can longest stand the strain ; this and only this. A good straight fight between two antagonists who desire to settle the question who is the stronger and better fighter, has something primitively noble about it—it is worth a fight because it settles the issue. But if two countries fight about territory, the result in no way settles to which the territory belongs nor does an industrial fight settle the rights about wages. Such conflicts are therefore not sane. It is much easier to discern symptoms of insanity than to prescribe effec- tive remedies. One thing is apparent, public opinion will, as it has done in this case, exercise in the future a larger part in the settlement of industrial disputes. The Government will have to perform other functions than merely those of keeping the ring, seeing fair play, and letting the com- batants fight to a finish. The fact that strikes have become national, as in the rail- way and coal strikes, has, apart from any- thing else, made national intervention a necessity. This raises many new problems which will have to be faced with wisdom as well as boldness. The functions of Government may be comparatively easy when it is restricted to bringing the parties together and giving advice. But when, as in the coal strike, an impasse is rEached, and a coercive measure rendered necessary, the difficulties increase. In the coal strike both parties have, after a good deal of protest,accepted the Parliamentary plan. But what if either party had stood out and refused to be ruled by the Act? If the masters had stood out we should have the somewhat risky experi7 ment of the Government trying to work the mines. While if the men had stood out they would have to appear before the bar of public opinion and put forward their claim to be fed, housed, and clothed by the result of the work of others. The claim would have to be a very strong one before it would be acceded to, and they would be met by the old affirma- tion that if they did not work neither should they eat. The future may be left to take case of itself. But I notice one of the miners' leaders is already preparing the minds of certain classes of workers for another gigantic strike in June. This is not to be of miners alone, but of miners, transport workers, railwaymen, and others, and they are to bold up the nation." Now what, in prosaic terms, does the holding up of the nation mean? It means an attempt to starve the nation for the purpose of making a section of employers yield to demands not yet formulated by a section of the workers. And who will be the chief sufferers through this holding up business? Will it not be those who have the least reserves and, as in the coal strike, the employers will be little hit ? In the recent coal strike it was those who had smallest cellars, and the lightest purses, that felt the pinch most and will feel it the longest. The purpose of this holding up of the nation is not made clear, but whatever the object be, it is a crime against theicommunity in which the poor will suffer most. The attempts to starve the nation by certain groups of workers mean war against their own kind ; it is a counsel worthy of hell itself, and every man who loves his country and his kind should set his face against it. The sound common sense or Stephen Walsh, M.P., has, happily, made itself felt on this question in the counsels of the miners. Speaking a few days ago he said : But he did ask them not t) be led away by the horrible doctrines which were being preached at the moment. He was referring to the dcctrine called Syndicalism, by which people were going to bring the nation to its knees, and the people who were talking about bringing out transport workers and causing stagnation to the nation itself. How could the nation be destroyed unless they were destroyed with it? How could they bring the nation to its knees without poor little children and helpless women being involved in the general destruction? That was not trade unionism. That was anarchy of the vilest and most disgraceful type, and he would fight that doctrine as long as he lived.</p>
        <p>But our protest against this incitement to national industrial "hold ups "throws us back upon the unsolved problem: Is the present relative reward of land, capita', and labour equitable, and is it to remain constant ? Strikes are like a boomerang, that often recoils and hits hardest those who throw it, but without them how are adjustments to be made? It is difficult to give any answer that will be generally considered satisfactory. But one answer I suggest is an insistent appeal to reason. Sure national progress is conditional upon the !deliberate inculcation and adoption in the minds of all concerned, of accurate intellectual conceptions controlled by moral considerations. I am aware that from such ideas those who prefer staggeringhumanity" turn away in impatience and disgust, and prefer trying a short cut to barbarism by some sort of revolution. But I confidently</p>
        <p>hold that force is a very ineffective remedy, if it remedies at all.</p>
        <p>As far as the recent coal strike was in any way a win for the men this was won because of the intellectual and moral support which was given by the nation to the principle of the minimum wage. Mr. Asquith and his Cabinet rightly interpreted the national verdict when he laid it down that the coal owners must give, and the miners receive, the minimum wage. The miners wanted a rider to this verdict and desired to insist that their own schedule figures should be inserted or, at any rate, minimum figures. But the nation, through its representatives wisely declined to go further, and while agreeing to the justice of the minimum it felt incap- able through Parliament of grappling with the complicated considerations which had to be considered in fixing a minimum to the minimums. The ss. or 2s. might or might not be just, but inasmuch as the minimum was bound to affect the price of coal to many who would be right glad to have a regular ss. a day without the possibility, as in the case of the miners, of it rising to ten or possibly fifteen shillings a day, Parliament wisely declined to insert figures, and left these to be fixed by commissioners with expert local knowledge.</p>
