      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>LOCAL WONDERS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WONDERS.</p>
        <p>[By The Cynic 1</p>
        <p>I was quietly feeding the choice collection of rain_ bow trout in the park, when I suddenly became aware of the elfish presence of the Manikin. The official mascot of the order of the Tub was unusually hilarious. He was well-nigh ready to burst with pride, which was so ill-concealed, that his dwarfish figure seemed more than usually grotesque. 'l've made a discovery," he squeaked.</p>
        <p>I was curious to know what the nature of the discovery might be, but deemed it best to let him tell his own story.</p>
        <p>"You see," he pursued," our great and renowned party has resolved to go in for education." . _</p>
        <p>An ejaculation- o; surprise escaped me, as I pictured to myself some of our town's wiseacres perusing elementary treatises upon economics and tariff reform in particular, but my musings were cut short by the enthusiastic flow of eloquence from my friend. " When the Dumping Exhibition was ended, some of us thought it would be a good idea if we improved our minds, so we have mastered the alphabet, and now I have just got through the spelling book, which con- cludes with a wonderful story."</p>
        <p>My amazement grew as I listened. but I confess that what followed was a matter of still greater surprise.</p>
        <p>" The story begins by naming some fellow or other called Diddle ' and proceeds to narrate how a certain frisky cow became so lively that it actually jumped right over the moon." I tried to stop him and explain that this was merely a piece of imagination 'expres3ed in nursery rhyme, but he would not bear of it. "This is the advantage of education," he declared airily, " I might have died without such knowledge ever becoming mine had I not commenced to read. What a marvellous feat ! I should not wonder if that were not a cow from Walton, they have some wonderful animals there." " Great Scot," I replied, "do you expect me to swallow this yarn of yours " ? " Yes, certainly, I have just read it, and it must be true."</p>
        <p>I wanted some word to express my pent up feelings, but nothing occurred to my mind except a very familiar term which I at once fired-off. Looking the dwarf full in the face, I blurted : " Jehoshaphat "</p>
        <p>- " Listen," I said, "I was passing the place where the new bridge is being built over the Mersey, when one of the men fell off the platform into, or nearly into the water. Just as he was about to alight upon the surface of the river he saw that it was murky and wet, so he returned to the platform." . .</p>
        <p>The Manikin's eyes opened wide with astonishment, as be solemnly assured me that no mortal could do that without some assistance.</p>
        <p>I repressed my mirth, and assured him that this work- man bad accomplised the feat by his own unaided efforts. _ __</p>
        <p>The elf thought for a moment thcn spitefully spat out the rejoinder—" Jehoshaphat " !</p>
        <p>He then rambled on to another subject. " The strike is over at last, and our party is not sorry. Some people who conduct a javenile school opened a soup kitchen to feed folk who were starving. So our people got mad about it and sent to London for the official artificer to come here and do as they told him. So he and some more blacksmiths went atearing rcund and saying : "This is Radical trickery ; we must stop it ; such corruption is intoleralle, it is a dodge to catch votes. These schemes are exploiting poverty for political ends.', When the inhabitants generally had considered these awful allegations they said: " Yes, but what must we do for poor women and children who are in want of food ? " So at last it was decided that a town's effort should be made to cope with the situation."</p>
        <p>" Yes," I cchoed, " but has not one of the Tub Club commenced a soupery on his pub premises, and have not many children gone there who were not supposed to enter licensed places ? And is it not a fact that several subscriptions have come into the fund through a political club, instead of the usual way ? " " Yes," replied the dwarf," but this is to show our interest in the poor, and has nothing to do wi Lb political schemes." He looked hardly at me to ascertain the effect of these words, but hopped away in a hurry when I exclaimed " Jehosbaphat."</p>
        <p>The gale which spoilt the Bank Holiday of excursion- ists on Monday was responsible for loss of life in several places. Dr. J. Dixon Mann, Professor of Forensic Medicine at the University of Manchester, and hon. physician at the Salford Royal Hospital, died at his home in Man- chester on Saturday.</p>
