        <p>my.0,0-+ Atom mumfirszt,m,*o +.oo4ij ' PRACTIcAL POULTRY FARMING. M lam:.limaom-,m-Kam-m-oltnEmmico,gio</p>
        <p>[BY REDCAP].</p>
        <p>HOUDANS.</p>
        <p>Houdans are one of our oldest breeds of poultry, and one of our best. They are non-sitters, very hardy, gracd layers of large white-shelled eggs, and exce'lent table birds. t f all the non-sitting varieties, the Houdan easily " tops " the list as an all-round breed. From a purely utility point of view this breed of fowls plays a very prominent part. Whatever variety you cross the Houdan with, some valuable utility point is gained as a result. If you care to scan an earlier column on cross-breeding, there you will find that Houdans are coupled with other breeds oftener than any other variety. If they have a bad point at all, it is, that where sufficient shelter is not provided (for bad weather), their crests become somewhat troublesome, but if provided with good shelter there is not much trouble. The crest also is a favourite harbouring place for vermin, and a frequent examination of the crest should be made. If any insects are found, give the roots of the crest a good dusting with Keating's insect powder. I mention this brand of powder, because have used it with excellent results.</p>
        <p>Jm The FEMALE 11017 DAN. The above picture is a very fide representation of the flouclan, and seeing that the actual colour of these fowls is black and white, we are able in the two colours of this paper, to view our subject of illustra- tion with a good idea of what they should be like.</p>
