      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>AS AN EXIIIBITICN FOWL.</head>
        <p>AS AN EXIIIBITICN FOWL.</p>
        <p>As well as being a good utility fowl, the Houdan is a very profitable breed to take up for exhibition pur- poses. They are what can be termed a made breed —that is a breed of fowls which has long since been perfected, from an exhibition point of view. With this variety, the amateur has a fair chance of coming to the front in the poultry fancy. The best birds are not just in a few hands, and prices for good specimens are not abnormal. If any of my readers are induced—through reading these notes—to take up exhibition breeding of Houdans, then all I hope is, that they breed one as good as that illustrated in this column. If so, then you will not be far from gaining a first and special prize. Meanwhile, good luck to the Houdan and those who breed this grand all-round fowl.</p>
        <p>POULTRY FOR EXHIBITION.</p>
        <p>Of all outdoor hobbies Ido not know of any more interesting, or more profitable, than that of breeding exhibition poultry. And providing a right start is made, a fancier with any real grit rarely gives up. There are a few essential points, which first of all should be thoroughly understood, before embarking into the field of exhibition breeding. It is commonly thought that this branch of poultry farming can only be enjoyed, by those who happen to have a long purse. This however is not the case. Of course it is much easier to get to the top, and much quicker tco, when one can afford to engage the services of a practical fancier as manager. But after all, victory is much sweeter, when it comes as the result of personal enter- prise, rather than as the result of being able to purchase one's victories. For a prize so won, merely brings satisfaction ; Whereas, when means are limited and " doggedness " has to do it, this surely brings, i.ot merely satisfaction, but well eamed reward. Therefore do not be disheartened. If you have a love for the fancy but are withholding because you think you cannot afford to compete with the 'moneyed' men, take courage and make a start. But not yet ; I have more to tell.. Just let me eay, however, before proceeding, that if you were to take from the shows the working man fanciers' entries, the classes would very often be sparingly filled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>MAKING A START.</head>
        <p>MAKING A START.</p>
        <p>First of all pet to know the variety yru like best But you say : How am Ito do this? You might say again, for instance, I have seen a pen of Silver Campines, and they were beautiful lam sure I should like this variety better than any other. No one would suggest the possibility of your ' first love ' being correct. But then, on the other hand, if you saw a collection of all the different breeds respectively classified—as in a good show- then your original choice may give place to some other variety. Then again your accommodation and surroundings may not be congenial or adaptable to the variety of your choice, so that you would be forced to make a choice accordingly. Don't be in a hurty ! Because once you have made the right choice, depend upon it you'll "stick." That is, if you are a fancier. But if you make hapazard selections and are afterwards dissatisfied, then the probability is that you would give the whole business up in disgust. The first point then, is making a right choice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>TIIE BEST WAY.</head>
        <p>TIIE BEST WAY.</p>
        <p>Now for the best way in making the selection. Before advising you to pay a visit to a good show, it would be more advisable to read a little. And read only that class of poultry literature which deals with fancy poultry. For instatce, you should first of all take in a weekly journal dealing with the subject. If you want to know the best one to take, drop me a line, and I will gladly tell you. Then again you should read something in book form. And for a start I knuw of no better and surer guide than the "Feathered World" Year Book. I make no apology for specifying this pubUcation, because it contains just what a novice requires, namely, good advice and some very helpful illustrations. It is possible that from this bock von could make ycur choice, so well depicted are the different varieties After doing so you could purchase a standard work on the variety selected. These latter are the work ( f those who have generally spent a lifetime's study with the breed of fowls written upon. The weekly journal is especially IlelpfuLit keeps you up-to-date. I have taken one fancy paper for fifteen years, and even so I find something new every week. Very well then. Our second point is that you should gat a real good theoretical training. Now then, we are off to the show</p>
        <p>POELTRT EXHIBITIONS.</p>
        <p>To the older han'di:,-;p;ii.r-y—e;iiihition is the place where victories are fought for, and where a season's work comes up for decision at the hands a the critical judge. Here, old fanciers meet, taking either victory or defeat vi,ith all good grace, such as is known only to • fanciers of real sporting spirit. Here also the novitiate try conclu- sions with the more matured hands, and here also is the place where future fanciers are born. (It was in the avenues of a poultry show where the writer first fell in love with a Black Rose-combed Bantam, and a male bird to boot.) It is in the latter category that I place my novice readers. For these lines are specially penned for those who desire to know. Let us assume then, that during yrur theoretical training you have decided on the variety you wish to take up. Your first business then should be to find out the classes containing these. Have a good look at them. Com- pare the winning birds with those that have lost, and if possible get into touch with a breeder of this variety and ask him to tell you something about the specimens penned. You don't care to ! Let me just tell you,</p>
      </div>
