      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>TARIFF REFORMER: Look here, Johnny! You let me get on yaw. back, and then I can help you to carry that load!</head>
        <p>TARIFF REFORMER: Look here, Johnny! You let me get on yaw. back, and then I can help you to carry that load!</p>
        <p>ton led by two goals four tries(l6 points)to Salford nil. The third quarter raw Salford much more aggressive, and as a result of clever play by John and May, the ball was taken to the other end for the first time. May nearly got through and Daintith bad to kick dead to save. Loose play gave Warrington the advantage again, and a reckless pass by W. Thomas gave Jordan a chance which he snapped up smsnly and scored from in position for Rena ick to goal again. Before the change of ends smart work by the home baers gave Clare his third try and raised the score to 24-0. Salford failed to put up anything like a show in their last effort with the %it'd, but on the other hand Warrington combined in brilliant fashion. Beating off an invasion for whi,:h John and Ritchie were respon- sible, the Warrington backs were seen to advantage and Dickersm running strongly down the open eluded Mesley and Lovelock in quick succession and then threw wide to Renwick who promptly served Mclntyre, the last redolent bowling over cook and scoring behind the posts. Renwick improved. A mistake by Launce let in Skelhorn for the closing try of the day, Renwick bringing the season to a close with his fifth goal. I Final : Warrington, five goals, eight tries (34 points) ; Salford, nil. . •</p>
        <p>League during the last ten months have been confined to propaganda work in connection with general Liberal legislation and especially the Insurance Act. A series of out-door meetings on our usual pitch at Bridge Foot at which we have had the services of several now well- known speakers, who have popularised our Bridge Foot meetings, has we believe, done much to explain the Liberal standpoint and show the fallacies and misrepresentation of our opponents We have just concluded a series of five successful meetings in various parts of the town with a view of explaining the details and working of the Insurance Act from a non-party view. Much information has been elicited by the public from questions put to the various speake-s and we believe, that much has been done to counteract the misrepresentations of the Act sedulously promulgated from the opposite camp, 1 the necessarily complicat.d nature of the Act lending itself to all kinds of misrepresentation. In connection with this work we have had the valuable services of Councillor Kendall, J.P., Councillor Middleton, of Oldham, and Mr Garside. The labour unrest throughout the country has called for the active interference of the Government resulting, in the case of the miners, of a Minimum Wage Bill. The whole country has been watching with anxiety the efforts of Mr Asquith and his colleagues to avert a National disaster and, though we have had the utmost confidence in these efforts for peace and are thankful that an amicable and honourable arrangement is in view, we must recognise the fact that the whole question of the remuneration of labour is still unsettled and that we as a labour organition shall have to do our part in bring- ing about a peaceful and permanent solution of the vital question.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF CONIPOER OF " ASTHORE." The death is announced at the age of LO, of Mr Henry Trot6re, the well-known composer. Mr Trot6re, who was of Irish descent, was a self- taught musician, and owed his eminence among present- day song writers to sheer bard work combined with a naturally optimistic temperament. His most successful songs were "In Old Medrid" and " Asthore." which have found their way into every corner of the earth where English people are to be found. As a writer of musical comedy Mr Trotilre also met with success, and in that sphere of work he created something of a record by writing and scoring a two-act piece, • The Skirt Dancer," in six weeks.</p>
