      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>Results of Saturday's Matches.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P Leeds 9 4 16 Dewsbury 0 0 0 Hunslet 1 414 Rochdale H. ... 2 1 7 HudderEfield Keighley Widnes ') 3 13 Warrington __ .. .-. .. _ .. .. . _ _ _</p>
        <p>Bradford Northern 5 6 28 Swinton</p>
        <p>Coventry Wakefield Trinity 3 621 Hull K. R. ...... 2 1 7 Batley Oldham , 0 3 9 Salford Broughton It 3 5 21 St. Helens , 1 1 5 LANCASHIRE COMBINATION</p>
        <p>Pemberton Oldham A — 8 Salrord A 1 1 5</p>
        <p>Broughton Ran A— 6 St. Helens A 1 1 5 Warrington A ...... 6 Widnes A</p>
        <p>ORDINARY MATCH.</p>
        <p>Wigan A</p>
        <p>Widnes v. Warrington. This return match was played at Widnes on Saturday, before 2,000 spectators in fine, but windy weathtr. Both teams were well represented, as follows : Widnes—Back, Jones; threequirters,Swinton, ' Lally and Laws • half lacks, Noon and O'Garra ; for- wards, Aspey, Cosgrove, Higgins, Johnson, O'Garra and Lloyd. Warrington—Back, Tilley ; threecoarters, Mclntyre, Renwick, Dickenson and Clare ; half backs, Daintith and Jordan ; forwards, Thomas, Tranter, Arnold, P Boardman and Critchley. _ _</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr May, St Helens. The home tYam had the wind in the opening half but Warrington were early prominent through Renwick and Clare who made a fine burst on the left wing. The Widnes foi wards rushed the game to the other end where Swinton snapped a pass intended for Renwick end scored easily. Tayfor kicked a goal. Taylor and Laws both came near scoring but Clare and Mclntyre tackled finely on the verge of tl•e line. Eventually Renwick raised the seige with a great run to centre where he fed Clare, and the wing man ran in brilliantly at the corner, no goal following. Half-time, Widnes 5 points, Warrington, 3 points. The Wirepullers had a good chance on resum- ing with the wind, but the forwards did not keep up the pace wet, and Widnes got the upper hand. Lloyd eventually increased the score for Widnes with a try, while soon after O'Garra scored behind the posts for Jones to goal. Warrington now showed more resolution and Renwick beat a host of opponents before being grassed in the borne quarter. From the scrum Warrington passed across and Mclntyre was only just kept out at the corner. Another chance went wrong through Dickenson dropping a pass, and Widnes rushed the game to safer quarters. Final : Widnes' 2 goals 3 tries (13 points) ; Warrington, 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>MONDAY'S MATCHES.</head>
        <p>MONDAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. C. T. P. Warrington Widnes Bradford NVigan Oldham 1 5 23 Coventry St. Helens Leeds 1 311 York Halifax Huddersfield Rochdale II 3 521 Barrow 1 1 5 Hull Kingston R. 12 13 63 Bromley Batley - - q 416 Keighley —0 1 3 _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ Warrington Finish Strongly. Easter Monday saw some remarkable football when VS'arrington finished their League programme with a match at Wilderspool with Salford. A hurricane of wind blew down the ground, the conditions being so severe that the teams agreed to play four "twenties." Salford had a good side in the field notwithstanding the clob difficulties, and Warrington made one or two changes in the team which played at Widnes' the mort important being the playing of Jolley at, full back. Only about 3,010 people assembled. Teams: Waning- ton : Jolley, back ; Mclntyre, Renwick, Dickenson, Clare, three•quarters ; Daintith, Jord3n, half-backs; Arnold, Cheaters, Thomas, Skelhorue, Tranter, Critchley, forwards. Salford : Launce, back ' • Cook, Thoma,., Lovelock. Mesley, three-quarters; John. May, half-bucks; E Thomas, Rees, Thom, Bevan, Ritchie, Currie, forwards. Referee, Mr J C Lumley, Leeds. Warrington bad the first spell with the gale behind them, and the Reds were immediately penned in their own quarter, relief punting being quite impossible. Early on Tranter rushed through and after smart exchanges with Mclntyre the forward scored im good position for Renwick to goal. Lovelock eased the pressure with a smart run to centre, but Renwick broke through the next minute at a great pace and threw wide to Clare, who took the ball well and scored far out. Chesters was soon over with a third try after a forward rush. Salford made a spasmodic effort but could not cross the centre line and Tranter paved the way for Clare to score again, Renwick goaling, so that at the end of the first quarter Warrington had the good lead of 16 points. It was now balford's turn to have the wind at their backs, but it never could be said that they showed any idea of coping with the elerc ents or wiping off may of the deficit. Mesley and Cook occasionally made smart sprints, but Warrington's tackling was always safe. On three occasions the visitors had shots at goal only to see the ball travel wide of the posts. Lofty kicks beat the Warrington backs and went out of bounds repeatedly, whilst on oae occasion Rees appeared certain to score until Jolley interceded and saved the situation with a plucky tackle. At half-time Warring-</p>
        <p>Chapter 7</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>PROGRESSIVE LABOUR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>PROGRESSIVE LABOUR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting was held in the Lecture Room at the Liberal Club, Mr E Wynne Jones presiding. There was a very good attendance.—The Secretary (Mr. J. Morton) presented his annual report, after which the officers and committee for the ensuing year were elected as follows :—President, Mr E Wynne Jones ; vice- presidents, Messrs George Walker, J Elwell, jun., M Llewellyn, Mr Horace Fear ; treasurer, Mr S Wright, J.P. ; secretary, Mr J Morton. A strong Executive Committee consisting of 22 of the most active members of the League was elected. It was arranged to com- mence an active out-door campaign early in May. The speakers at the first meeting will be Mr A H Crosfield and Mr Gordon Harding, M.P., of Rochdale. The annual report was as follows :—The period that has elapsed since our last annual meeting at the end of May last has witnessed events in industrial and political spheres which have been remarkable. The efforts of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Skel</head>
        <p>Skel</p>
        <p>NOT QUITE SUCH AN ASS!</p>
        <p>Some Folks Use</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha simply because it makes clothes whiter and a better colour than any other soap. Others use Fels-Naptha because it saves so much work and bother. Others use it because it makes the clothes last so much longer.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha 39 Wilson street London E C</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. Offices Scheme Again Discussed.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. Offices Scheme Again Discussed.</p>
        <p>from some of the finest medical men in the country. Mr LEE asked why the Board needed two doctors? Would not one resident medical min, at a higher salary, be sufficient?</p>
        <p>Th-e. CITAIRMA:s7 : That has been discussed before you became a Guardian. The main reason is that if you appoint a medical officer here, if he keeps within certain limits of conduct, you oannot get without him. Mr LEE : We should have a resident doctor here and be should be in charge, and not an outside doctor. The CHAIRMAN : All that has been d.s listed bsfore. _ VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>Mr JOLLEY moved that the Board accord its warmest thanks to Mr. Mather for his services in the chair during the past year. lie said that during the time Mr. Mather had occupied the position his relations with all the members had been of the most cordial character. Although on one or two occasions, as happened in the best regulatPd families, and even in ecclesiastical families—(laughter)—there had been a little friction, no-one could have dealt with such differences more admirably than Mr. Mather. Ile bad a large share of the saving grace of humonr which many times got a man out of a difficulty when talents of another kind might only exaggerate or exasperate the difficulties. Mr. ALFRED BROOKS seconded and congratulated Mr Mather on the way in which be had surmounted the various difficulties which had confronted him. He hoped he would shortly be promoted to the higher sphere of Chairman of the Rural District Council. Several menthera spoke in support of the vote which was heartily passed. Mr. MATHER, in responding, said be had the satis- faction of looking back on his two years of office as a period in which improvements had been effected in the Workhouse and in other dapartments of the Board's work.</p>
        <p>fi-,iote of thanks was also passed to Mr Bout for his services in the vice-chair. The CHAIRMAN proposed a vote of thanks to the officials. They had all, from the Clerk to the humblest worker, done their duty well and the work seemed tc&gt; go on automatically. There was not one hitch of any comequence during the whole of the year, each officer having displayed tact, ability end judgment. Mr. BENT monde. Mr. JOLLEY, in supporting, said they might con- gratulate themselves that the wishes of the Guardians were carried out in a very admirable manner and that the best interests of the ratepayers were faithfully served by the officials from the Clerk downwards. The vote was heartily agreed to. The CLERK, in respcnding, said that, taking the Board as a whole, the most amicable relations bad existed with the officials. They all did their utmost to please the Board, but they were not infallible. Still, they did their best. Mr. AAN RIMY also returned thanks. He said that as that was the last occasion that be would appear in the Board room—</p>
        <p>The CHAIRM&amp;N : No, you must come and see us sometimes. Mr AWDERY said he wished to thank the Board for the exceeding and unexpected kindness he had received from them individually and collectively. It was a great severance for him to leave Warrington but he was fortified by the fact that he carried with him the esteem and goodwill of every Guardian. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>RESIDENT MEDICAL OFFICER APPOINTED</head>
        <p>RESIDENT MEDICAL OFFICER APPOINTED</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Beard of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, Mr G Mather presiding. There were also present : Mrs Simpson, Miss Broadbent, Rev A M Mitchell, Messrs E L Bent, A 3 Jones, A Brooks, \V C Brooks, It Lee, W Houghton, J A Ruihworth, 3 A Hughes, G A Allen, W Flanagan, T Ititehie,A Bottomley (clerk),R W Peacock (deputy cierk), and J F Awbery (master). TIIE MASTER'S FAREWELL.</p>
        <p>Permission was granted lo the master and matron to give a farewell entertainment to the inmates on Friday evening.</p>
        <p>NEW OFFICES SCR. ME DEFERRED. TUE ACUTE PRESSURE OF THE RATES.</p>
        <p>Mr.JO-CLEY said be found him-self iica-,iffil-crit-lty. He bad been acting as Chairman of the Offices Sub- commi: tee and be had to submit a resolution which was not unanimous. For once in his life he found himself unable to come into line with his friends Messrs Alfred Brooks and W Furness. The recommendation was " That the proposal to lease premises in King-street be abandoned and that new premises, in accordance with plans now submitted, be erected." He wished to be scrupulously fair in the matter and leave the gentle- men who advocated the proposal, from which be dissented, to state the grounds which had infleenced them in arriving at their decision. The plans for the new offices had been prepared by Messrs. Owen and very admirably served the putposes for which they were intended. A building erected on those lines would be useful and conv,nient and would in many directions facilitate their Union administration. The question which the Board would have to consider before apnroving or disapproving of the zesolution, was largely a financial one. Mr FURNESS suggested that the question of erecting new premises should be deferred until the next meeting and then the Board could be placed in posse:sion of all the facts. Mr JOLLEY : We have all the facts here. Continu-</p>
        <p>log he said that since the meeting at which the Board expressed definite approval of leasing the promises in King-street, the Rural District Council, which it was expected would use part of the premises, had decided that whatever happened it would have nothing to do, as a public body, with the King-street scheme. The main ground for dropping the proposal to go on with the leasing and alteration of the King-street offices, how- ever, was the fact that a very considerable outlay would be necessary to adapt those premises for the purpose of Union off ces, including Btard and com- mittee rooms. The expenditure was estimated at £1,500 and as any loan which might be obtained would have to be repaid in a short period, the immediate charge on the rates would be a heavy one and would involve a substantial addition for one or two half-years at least. The new schema now presented would involve an expenditure of £5,100 on land and buildings, and in addition there would he the cost of furnishing. The major:ty of the committee, however, thought it would be truly economical and in the best interests of the Union to adopt that scheme. There would be no diffi- culty in getting the loan spread over thirty years and he estimated that, taking account of the saving in rent, the total additional expenditure involved by the new scheme would be about £250 a year for a few years. Giving his reasons for objecting to the proposal, Mr. Jolley said he quite recognized that additional accommodation was urgently needed for Union and registration purposes, but his opinion was that it could be most cheaply obtained by renting suitable offices in the town, without incurr- ing any capital outlay at this stage. It would, of course, mean continuicg the Board and committee meetings at the Workhouse and the Rural District Council arrangements would be as at present. He believed that by renting instead of purchasing or building premises, there would be a saving of over £2OO a year. In recent months he had had strikingly impressed upon him the fact that there were a large number of ratepayers who felt very acutely the pressure of the rates at the level to which they had risen, not merely in Warrington but in other parts of the Union area. It was a fact that the burden was a real and heavy one and he personally felt more strongly now than ever before the extreme undesirability of doing anything that would add to the poundage rate in the Union. An annual rate of Is. 2d. had served for some years for purely Poor Law purposes and he was of opinion that, with the strictest care and attention, it would be possible to keep within that limit if they avoided anything in the nature of capital expenditure which was not absolutely neces- sary. They could not hope to reduce their expenditure, so far as it was expenditure devoted directly to the relief of the poor. Their system of working in that Union had been so satisfactory that he did not think there was any likelihood of any important diminution in their outlay in regard to out-relief or the expendi- ture in the Workhouse. The probability was rather that their expenditure under the latter head would increase. The tendency of recent legislation and the natural growth of the population tended to raise the actual numbers, but as a set off against that the Union was growing in wealth as well as in population, and gradually the yield of one penny in the pound was rising, so that the normal increase in rateable value would be sufficient to meet the gradual growth of expenditure in connection with the relief of the poordt was his deliberate opinion that the provision of additional accommodation for mental cases, which the Board had recently decided upon, could be made without compelling them to increase the cell upon the rates but the margin would be so fine that if they embarked upon further capital expenditure they would probably have to increase the rates. Mr. A. Beootes, in seconding the resolution, said Mr Jolley had told them that by renting offices they would save over £2OO a year, as compared with a building scheme. They were, however, told that they were going to save a lot when they first viewed the offices in King-street, hut it turned out that they would have to incur almost the same capital expenditure on alterations that they would have to incur if they adopted the scheme for entirely new buildings. Mr Jolley left out one important factor— that whatever offices they took, there would have to be a considerable outlay to fit them for their require- ments, and that outlay would have to come out of the current rate. The new scheme was not merely in the air ;it was not hypothetical. The offices could be built on ground of which they knew at a coat which Mr Owen guaranteed—and they knew that in the past his guarantee had always been an over-estimate—would not exceed £5,100, with an extra £560 for furniture. That expen- diture, spread over a term of thirty years, would be more truly economical and in the interests of the rate- payers. It would place less burdens upon them in the immediate future and would, as time went on, be an asset to the Board. Mr W C BROOKS failed to see why the sub-committee had gone out of their way to instruct the architects to prepare plans. He understood that they were appointed to deal with the furnishing of the premises. Mr JoLLEv : The Board gave us authority at the last meeting. The Rev A M MITCHELL said he was delighted to know that the mad scheme for the leasing of offices in King-street had been abandoned once and for all. Why it should have been desired to take them to such a gloomy edifice he did not know. As a member of the Rural District Council he had entered his protest against it. The place seemed more suitable for rats and ghosts and such like creatures. (Laughter). They should have something bright and cheerful— The CII AIP,M AN That scheme is abandoned. The Rev A M MITCHELL : But the scheme for rent- ing other premises is not abandoned. The CHAIRMAN Well, go on with that. The Rev A M MITCHELL : The same foolish sugges- tion might be made again. Continuing he said that with regard to the new scheme, whatever buildings were provided ought to be worthy of the town. War- rington, notwithstanding all its smoke and numerous odours—(laughter)—was a great and important town and the public buildings ought to be in harmony with its greatness. He would advocate the centralization of all public offices. It would be much better to have the offices in one fine block of buildings, worthy of the town, where people coming to do business in Warring- ton might save their time by passing from one set of offices to another. He suggested that it would be well to defer the matter for a little time with the object of bringing forward something more definite and comprehensive. He doubted whether the amount it was at present proposed to expend would provide anything worthy of the town. They would probably have a small, insignificant building and it would be a mistake to proceed with work of that kind. If the Board bad not decided to build a new kitchen at the Workhouse—which he con- tended was not necessary—they would have had money available for the new offices. Mr. JOLLEY, referring to Mr. Mitchell's persistent opposition to the kitchen scheme, said it was unfortun- ate that he should throw about adjectives of au offensive character with reference to proposals which had been carefully considered and which were designed to increase the comfort of the people in the Workhouse to add to the efficiency of the institution and to pro- mote economy in its working. Miss BROADBENT said she understood the provisios of new offices was very urgent as the present building was unsafe. The resolution in favour of new offices was defeated by 11 votes to 2. NEW RR,IDENT MEDICAL OFFICER. -</p>
        <p>_ Thera was only one application for the position of resident assistant medical officer, in succession to Dr. Taylor. The candidate—Dr. Wm. Megasv, of Belfast— appeared before the Board and was unanimously appointed. He has had considerable experience in the Royal Victoria Hospital, Belfast, where he resided for some months as a pupil. He has attended clinics and classes at the Belfalt fever hospital, district lunatic asylum, maternity hospital, and the Balfast hospital for sick children. The CHAIRMAN said Dr. ilega-v bad testimonials</p>
        <p>`COAGITLIIsTE,' Transparent Cement for Glass, China fr.o. 6d. and Is., post free.—Advt.</p>
        <p>I</p>
