        <p>Matrimony.</p>
        <p>At last the hopes THE RECKONING, and prayers of the nation for an end of the coal strike are answered. All over the country the pits are workmg again, and although in several districts sections of the miners seem to remain irreconcilable it is only a matter of time now before the industry will be in full swing. The men's leaders hope it may be so before many days are past, and there is hope of an early return to normal conditions in the tumbling down of the price of coal. Now that the strife is over the c ombatants and the onlookers may spend an illuminating hour in making the reckoning. Counting the cost is not always a pleasant task, but it is invariably a profitable one. Many estimates have been made of the loss to the country which the great strike has entailed. On the one hand we have the ,pessimist loading up figures which, if they were reliable, might well shock even the most confirmed smiler upon misfortune. On the other hand there are the Board of Trade returns for March, which go to show that British trade has withstood the shock of what must be counted the greatest industrial conflict on record almost without a tremor. Notwithstanding that during every day of the month reviewed the greatest of our industries was at a stand- still, and that many others were wholly or partially paralysed, both the total imports and the total exports show large increases over the corresponding month of last year.</p>
        <p>In short, the general overseas trade of the nation was astonishingly maintained. Of course, neither the alarmist arithmetic of the pessimist nor the very welcome record of the Board of Trade carries us quite close to the real truth of the matter. The scare figures must be regarded with scepticism and the trade returns as' something much less than a true measure. It is just possible that the stoppage of the fuel supply may show its results more unkindly, on the April returns, but there is no reason to suppose that our overseas trade will be hardly hit at all. That, however, is not by any means the whole story. Home trade has received a drastic check by the heavy losses it has inflicted upon the working classes. The miners themselves have lost in wages close on £10,000,000, and a loss scarcely smaller must have been sustained by the hundreds of thousands of persons in other trades who were thrown out of employment or placed on short time with a reduction of wages. It is difficult to estimate in exact figures the loss which all this must entail lupon the nation. The wage-earners are, of 'course, the first and most heavily-dealt-with sufferers, but there are many thousands out- side their ranks who are feeling the effects of the dread time through which we have passed Despite all the optimism of overseas trade returns, therefore, one cannot but count the reckoning which starts with a sum of £20,000,000 a heavy one. If the workers of the country realise the extent to which they foot the bill, there will probably be pause before the threats of more national strikes at present in the air are carried into effect. We think the great fight which the miners have made will have been worth while if the establishment of the minimum wage which has been secured leads to some basis upon which permanent peace in that sphere of labour may be hoped for. That, however, does not lead us to look with any favour upon the wild proposals for piling upon it other strikes of similar magnitude with a similar end in view. The time must come, of course when other industries will claim what the miners have obtained, but they will claim it in the wrong way if they follow the mode of blind revolt which is being preached to them in certain quarters at the moment. We can- not imagine any circumstances under which another great strike this summer would be justified. The recent strife strained the industrial machine of the nation almost to breaking-point, and it will be many months before the final effects of it pass. To put upon it now a further strain would certainly be to court chaos and disaster. It cannot at this moment be too firmly impressed upon those who are loud in their advocacy of a general" hold up" of industry that such a policy, whatever its aims, is the very last likely to gain the sympathy and support of the public opinion which had not a little to do with the miners' victory. The disposition to look upon a general strike as the first way of remedy for unsatisfactory conditions is the most alarming tendency of the times to the truest friends of labour.</p>
        <p>Self government for HOME RULE Ireland has been coming,</p>
        <p>coming,—and now we can almost say that it has come ! Mr. Asquith on Thursday introduced the third Home Rule Bill to a House of Commons crowded in every part, but by no means as excited as were the members on those earlier historic occasions when a Liberal Govern- ment attempted to find a panacea for Irish grievances within the covers of a Home Rule Bill. The feeling in the House seemed to indicatea widespread conviction that this time Home Rule was going through. Even the Opposition Leaders have confessed as much, though they have generally contradicted themselves in the next breath. Of course, the Opposition are opposed to the Bill. To them it is a party measure, but they have little to say against its provisions that was not said 26 years ago. The same bad old assertions and arguments are trotted out— as for instance, the fatuous allegation by Lord Charles Beresfori that Ireland under Home Rule must, in the event of war, be regarded as a potentially hostile country.</p>
        <p>Could anything indicate more clearly how small an advance has been made in thought and political perception by the Tory party during the last quarter of a century? The dispassionate atmosphere in which the Home Rule question is to-day discussed all over the country—and even in the House of Commons, except among the irreconcileables of the Beresford and Carson type—proves that the people of England have progressed considerably, and learned much from the lessons of South Africa. This is the age of enlightenment, and to a great extent the old hates and fears have been displaced by a calm appreciation of Ireland's great needs and just demands. "Home Rule has won by waiting," to quote a daily contempor- ary, and there is little fear that "the third time " will fail to pay in full for all its unfortunate legislative prede- cessors.</p>
        <p>Mr. ASQUITH'S Bill is packed full of the wisdom gained by experience and tempered by the moderation which recognises that even a small minority has claims to consideration. The supremacy of the Imperial Parliament remains, and revenue is still. to be collected by Imperial officers. As in both the previous Bills, but with some notable changes, the Irish Parliament is debarred from legislating 1, on a number of subjects—either subjects of Imperial concern like the Crown or the Navy, or those matters of religious controversy upon which Unionists profess to fear the oppresSion of the Protestant minority. As in the 1883 Bill, there are still to be Irish Members at Westminster, but in greatly reduced numbers.</p>
        <p>The details of the Irish Parliament and Executive will be noted with interest. What we like least (and we think our dislike will be widely shared among Liberals) is the nominated Senate. Nominated Second Chambers have been tried often enough in the Colonies, and have always proved incompatible with democracy In all probability this provision will be the object of the widest-spread opposition, and some modificatiOn of the idea may be necessary before the Bill goes through. The rest of the Constitution is plain sailing and within the defined sphere the supremacy of the Imperial over the Irish Government will be the same as its supremacy over the Government of a self-governing Dominion. The crux of the financial adjustments is to be found in the fact that Ireland, under the Union, after being overtaxed for three- quarters of a century, has been reduced to a point where she cannot pay her way. In the present year,as things stand, she will not contribute a penny to Imperial purposes, while the other parts of the United Kingdom are mulcted of about £1,500,000 for Irish purposes. Under the Union this is bound, as the PREMIER said, to get worse and worse ; because an Ireland which has no financial responsibility has no motive for economy. The proposal of the Bill is to make her financially responsible for herself on terms that she shall get what she would have got in the starting-year, if she had remained under the Union, plus an extra gift of £500,000 to diminish annually till it reaches £200,000. That is to be her Govern- ment's income from the Imperial Exchequer, provided it leaves that Exchequer's taxes where they are, and imposes n one of its own Within those limits it could, of course, only make a surplus or avoid a deficit in one way—by economising on administration. Considerable freedom is granted to an Irish Government which shows sufficient financial strength and stability, though the main con- trol of taxation and customs is retained by the Imperial Parliament. Altogether in finance, as elsewhere, we believe the Bill gives an Irish Parliament a real opportunity, and we have the liveliest anticipations of seeing it pass into law during the lifetime of the present Government.</p>
        <p>We are glad to note</p>
        <p>THE TEACHERS that the Warrington AND THE Certificated Class HEADMASTERSHIP. Teachers' Association has taken official notice of the situation brought about by the appointment of a gentleman from outside, for the second time, to a new elementary school. The teachers have a very strong case, which is stated with conspicuous moderation in a letter from their bon. secretary printed on our last page. The enquiries which have been made prove how little "open" competition for,'headships there is. The great majority of Education Authorities either promote their own men without any advertisement of the vacancies, or give a strong local preference. It is only the poor posts relatively that are advertised, and the conclusion seems 'obvious that the Warrington Com- mittee in throwing open their vacancies are not only ignoring the just claims of local men of proved capacity, but they are going the wrong way to obtain the " best " men for the positions. The "best" men are kept at home by the best authorities. Thei War- rington teachers seem to be between the devil and the deep blue sea, with little prospect of escape.</p>
        <p>THE DISTRESS. MORE FUNDS NEEDED.</p>
        <p>Last evening, the Mayor's fund for the relief of Distress in</p>
        <p>Warrington had reached the total of £1,065. This is satisfactory as far as it goes, though the response has not been so wide- spread as the great emergency demands. The individual subscriptions have been large but they have been drawn from a compara- tively limited section of the townspeople. The fact that the strike is now over does not make the need any less urgent—in fact a substantial augmentation of the relief fund is imperative if the acute distress which unquestionably prevails is to be adequately dealt with. Already expendi- ture to the amount of £7OO has been incurred and the outlay is at the rate of £6O a day. As far as can be ascertained, it will be necessary to continue relief for another fort- night and the additional amount required will not fall far short of £l,OOO. The authorities are finding out that men who have been fed on soup for four q•five weeks are beginning to suffer for want of a more sustaining diet and will not be in a fit condition to resume heavy manual labour when the works re-open. The funds at present received, however, do not admit of variety in the food supplied. Even when work is resumed, some families have been reduced to such a helpless state of poverty that rent is in arrears, articles of furniture have been pawned, and they will be in serious financial difficulties for at least a fortnight after wages begin to come in again. This week large numbers who have been honestly striving to avoid accepting charity have been compelled to yield to atern necessity and apply to the Central Relief Offices for aid. At the present time there are 900 families on the fund and that number does not wholly represent all who are receiving food at the works' soup kitchens and the Bewsey Adult School. The Education authority is now providing meals for between 1,200 and 1,300 children daily but that expen- diture will, of course, be a charge on the rates. Happily there is every hope that the larger works will in a few days be in full swing, but that does not justify an attitude of complacency or indifference towards present necessities. On the contrary the position at the moment is</p>
        <p>critical and those who have been withholdlug, their subscriptions until fully persuaded 17 the genuineness of the cry for help not hesitate longer, but at once contribuw their quota to the town's effort to meet a/1 [1 unprecedented situation.</p>
        <p>There are some feature: THE TRIUMPH OF of the Budget, willicr FREE TRADE owing to various o'gi FINANCE. tractions, have 11° received the attention, which they deserve. The mere fact that once more, since the historic Finance which the Lords rejected as "the end Of a things," it has been found unnecessary el impose any fresh taxation, while powerilf Protectionist countries have to meet Wed; necessities every year by a loan, gives eV:O to Protectitmists to" think furiously." jr;ff Trade finance, over which the 15,r",,, Reformers made merry as 'bankrupt," I,n proved itself to be not only solvent oaf exceedingly flourishing. The Budget 13 1909, which proved the latent streng7„ of Free Trade finance, was attacked e`ci another side as injurious to credit le ruinous to trade. Since its introduction vs has boomed as it never boomed before, alflo who'can swallow the impossible creed OA, trade that booms when credit is destroYeu, or even impaired? In estimating the proso pects of next year's yielA, the experts whom the Treasury is forced to rely,and tic! do the work of cold calculation in a fril6", atmosphere into which the disturbil,l! elements of fiscal controversy cannot ro allowed to enter, base their anticipations good year for us to a great extent upon ro information that our best customers are etso flourishing; but our best customers are trade rivals. The Tariff Reform theerde assumes that we cannot do a good Ole, unless our trade rivals are doing a bad 01111 Truly, Tariff Reform thought would WO some " fine confused feeding."</p>
