        <p>• * * * dies on page 7 giving the history ehools, from notes by. the late Mr. have aroused considerable interest. londent wishes us to bring the ) to date by mentioning that in the Mr. John Cann was appointed 3ter, which appointment he still • Can is highly respected not only but also in Warrington, and was Years the local secretary of the ,t Fund in connection with the</p>
        <p>National Union of Teachers. He is this week one of the delegates to the N.U.T. Con- ference, being held at Hull. He is President of the Orford Old Boys Re-Union. Orford was one of the first schools in the country where girls were taught cookery. Mrs. Cann, the superintendent of the local cookery classes, being the teacher. * * * The Home Office has issued, for the information of shopkeepers and shop assistants, a Memorandum explaining in plain language the law relating to shops which is now contained in the Shops Act, 1912. This Act, which received the Royal Assent last week, consolidates without change of substance the provisions of previous Shops Acts from the year of 1892 down to and including the Shops Act of last year. The Memorandum in particular deals fully with the new and important provisions which were contained in the Act of last year, and which come into force on May Ist next. The Home Office has also issued the form of notice which on and after Ist May shop- keepers will be required to post up in their shops stating the day or days on which their assistants are to be given a half-holiday.</p>
