        <head>LOCAL PARS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARS.</p>
        <p>Rink Hockey. An interesting hockey match between Warrington Palatine and Northwich Pavilion, played on the sink of the former team on Easter Monday, resulted in a win for the homesters by one goal to nil. Both teams played good hockey an many exciting incidents were witnessed. Local Will. Mr. John Frederick Kiernan, of Woodbank, Sandi- way, Nurthwich, and formerly of Warrington, cotton manufacturer, who died January Ist List, intestate, aged 36 years, left estate of the gross value of .f.,24,688, with net Fersonalty .C 12,376. Musical Successes. - •</p>
        <p>At the March examinations in pianoforte playing held by Trinity College of Music, London, Miss Marie Hawley the twelve-year-old daughter of Mr. Felix Hawley, Knutsford-road, Warrington passed with honours in the Junior division, obtaining 91 marks out of 100. This is very creditable performance and speaks well for the as)ilities of Miss Hawley arid her teacher Mr. William Cooke, F.R.C.0., the organist of Lymm Parish Church. Union of Clerks. The monthly meeting of the Warrington Branch of the National Union of Clerks was held at the Lab iur Exchange on Tuesday. A report was read from the Lancashire and Cheshire District Council dealing with the formation of new branches at Damen, Macclesfield and Wigdn. Mr. A. H. Prosfield has become an honorary member, and in a letter to the local secretary wished the Union every possible success, and especially the Warrington Branch. LAWN MOWERS ground by special machinery. Send your machines now before the rush.—J. S. GARLAND 51, Bridge at., Warringtor.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Catholic Young Men's Society. This society held its annual dance at the Parr Hall on Monday evening. The attendance was not as largo as might have been expected, but the function proved highly enjoyable. Mr. M. Cunningham and Mr. E. A. Connor acted as M.C.'s. Excellent music for dancing was supplied by Mr. T. Taylor's bind, and the cater- ing of the Winnoarleigh Cafe Company gave complete satisfaction. Mr. T. Stirrup discharged the duties of dance secretary. Athletic Successes. Local athletes were well to the fore last week-end. At Belle 'Vire, Manchester, on Saturday, the third prize in the mile bicycle race at the Salford Harriers Spot ts was won by A. Siddall, Warrington AC., while in the five milca bicycle scratch (club championship) C. Berry, of Warrington, was third. At a joint meeting of the Stanley, Liverpool, Sefton and Liverpool Pembroke Harriers, J. Spencer, with an allowance of eight yards, won the chief event of the day-100 yards handicap—in fine style by a yard. A. Willcock, elf the 17 yarls mark, ran second in the 220 yards flat race and W. Wharton secured fourth prize in the Lair miles handicap. Sankey Bridges Wesley Guild. Despite the unfavourable weather which prevailed on Easter Monday, the members of the Guild were fully determined to enjoy themselves and make the bi at of the opportunity afforded by a bank holiday. The cycling club had a run down to New Brighton, and taking into consideration the strong gale that blew they cannot be accused of lack of courage seeing that 50 per cent of the members turned out. With a considerable amount of perseverance they reached their destination in due time. The return journey was much pleasanter and easier than the outward journey, the greatest difficulty being to keep the lamps burning in the wind. Another attraction was a ramble for those who are not cyclists (it was also availed of by a few that are.) The rambles are now very popular, the one in question being to Appleton, about GO members and friends partaking of the hospitality of Mrs. Parslow of Cross Farm, who kindly catered for the wants of the party and lent one of the fields for games, &amp;c. St. Mary's Catholic Young Men's Society. A whist drive, social, and dance in connection with the above society was held in the Old Post Office, Market-street, on Monday evening last bringing to a close a very succeesful social season. Upwards of 150 members and friends were present and a very pleasant and enjoyable evening was spent, whist being indulged in from 7 o'clock till 10 p.m. and dancing until 2 a.m. The following werethe prize winners at whist, which were presented by the Bev Father Swarbreck, 0 S.B. Ladies: let, Mrs Crolla ; 2nd, Miss Fell; consolation, Miss A Dean. Lady playing gent., Miss E Corney. Gentlemen : Ist, Mr F Eckersley ; 2nd, Mr J Boyle. The following contri- buted to the musical portion of the programme, Mies A Warren, Miss A Caldwell, the Rev Father L Bugging and Father Hayes, and Messrs J Caldwell and R Heneghan and the Misses Eckersley and Richardson accompanying Mr J Caldwell, the blind pianist, played for dancing. Warrington Sailing Club's Race. This popular holiday event took place on the Mersey on Saturday afternoon and, as usual, aroused great interest. Half a gale was blowing when the boats started from Widnes to cover the course to Bank Quay. Six out of the eight'boats entered lined up for the start, the Edie and Pride failing to put in an appearance. The boats were in sight at Warrington at about 3 30, with Maggie holding a good lead. Rosa Mary crept up nicely when nearing Litton's Mill, but lost her advantage at, the bend of Crosfield's Wharf. After round ng the buoy at Walton Atches, the Maggie (Captain S. Meacock) pulled away and even- tually won by about 100 yards. Rosa Mary (Captain J. Smith) was lucky to gain second place, Polly (Cap`ain A. Warbuiton), with her bowspirit broken, making a bold bid for it. Nina (Captain J Whittaker) claimed the fourth position. The last boat home was Thelma (Captain H. Warburton). She came in pretty close up, seeing that her topping-lift had given way. A. report of the disaster to the. Zinnia appears on page 7. MOTOR CTCLES.—Agent for " Douglas " &amp; "Premier.' New Models now in stock. Trials and all information free.-J. S. GARL AND, 51-55, Bridge at., Warrington.-Advt Miss Mary Gornall's Concert. We would remind our readers that the evening concert arranged by Miss Mary Gornall in aid of the Cripples' Guild will be held on Tuesday week, April 93rd, in the Bold-street schoolroom. The object is one to which the public, by buying tickets, should give their generous support. Not only will they have the pleasure of attending a concert at which some very notable lccal artistes are to perform but they will be supporting a very deserving charity. It is a public duty to aid the cripples, for by the very nature of the C 360 the sufferers are unable to do much for themselves. Those who are conversant with what is being done for cripples in other parts of the country, as by the Duchess of Sutherland in the Potteries, NNill recognise that Warrington ought not to fall behind other towns in its solicitude for the welfare of the afflicted ones here. Tickets and plans for the concert are at Mr. Pearson's, Bridge-street. For further particulars see advertisement. Miss Gornall hopes that sympathising friends, who may not be able to attend the concert, will remember the cause and send a subscription.</p>
        <p>Mare</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND ' (Aniseed, Senega, Squill, Toll] &amp;o.) For Coughs and Colds, Influenza, &amp;o.—Advt.</p>
        <p>St. Paul's Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>The annual sale of wolk in connection with St. Paul's Church will be held in the Assembly Rooms, Winmarleigh-street, on Tuesday next. It will be opened at 2.30 p.m. by Mrs. John J. Crosfield, of Frodsham. The sum of 3:300 is needed and the Vicar earnestly asks for most generous help towards this annual effort. Personal.</p>
        <p>The list of successful candidates at the inter- mediate law examinationo recently held in London, was published yesterday, and included the name of Mr. Eric J Bairstow of Queen's Park, Chester, who is 2riicled to Mr. T. S. Steel, Warrington,</p>
        <p>Liberal Bowling Competition. In the Liberal Club Bowling competition for the Peter Peacock Challenge Cup, 352 members compris- ing 27 teams have entered up to the present and the competition promises to be a most interesting one. It is expected that about 30 teams will compete.</p>
        <p>" PREMIER " CYCLES.—Speoial lines at £5 10s. or with 3 speed gear, £6 15s.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. The Citizen's Temperance League.</p>
        <p>The Warrington 'Citizen's Temperance League held their monthly meeting ma Thursday in Atkinson's Cafe, Bridge Street, Mr. J. G. Withinshaw presided over a very fair attendance. The principal business transacted was the arrangement for a great open-air Tempersnce Campaign during the summer months. Invitations are being sent out to all the religious Temperance organisati as in the di-trict seeking for their active co- reration. The prosf ects are exceedingly bright for a vigoi On i campaign. Plcturedrome</p>
        <p>The Easter attractions at the Pictured: ome were of the h'ghest order and large h uses have been the ru'e nightly. Some excellent and rare films have been shown and the whole programme maintains the high reputation which this popular pic•ure palace has achieved. Full of enterprise and Jore-thought the management procuied the film of the Grand National which proved one of the best attractions and the audience followed the race with intense interest. A high-class programme has been arranged for next week and the chief film for the early part .of the week will be " Attacked by a lion," and the premier picture from Thursday will be "The deputy aid the girl." Death of Mrs. George Airey. • .</p>
        <p>Many of our readers—will deeply reg et to hear of the death of Mrs. Mary L. Airey, wife of Mr. George Airey, Sandfield flail, Newton-le-NI/Plows, who pass, d away at Llandudno on Wednesday. The deceased lady had been in failiug health for about two years. She was well known iu Warsington and district, and was actively associated with many charitable organizations in Newton and Golborne, holding the office of Presi- dent of the Golborne Nursing Associati in. She was ul,o a generous supporter of church work. The funeral will take place at Wigan Cemetery, to-day (Saturday). It will be preceded by a choral service at St. Peter's Church, Newton, at 10 30 a.m. Warrington Relieving Officer's New _ .</p>
        <p>- Appointment. At a meeting of the Epson Board of Guardians on Wednesday, 1r Ell Cottetill, of Warrington, was appointed relieving officer and collector in the Eastern district of the Epsom Union. There were 150 applica- tions for the pisition and five candidates were selected to appear before the Board. For the past two years and a:half Mr. Cotterill has been relieving officer in No. 4 district of the Warrington Union ; prior to which he was in the service of Messrs. Gat tons, Ltd., seed specialists, at Acton Grange. He has proved himself a most capable and sympathetic official and his many friends will congratulate him upon his well- deserved promotion in the Poor Law world.</p>
        <p>The New Church. Following his series of lectures on "The Second Coming: How accomplished and when" delivered last month in the Masonic Hall, the Rev. W. H. Claxton, gave an address at Atkinson's Cafe on Monday evening, on the " Atonement " presenting the New Church view of that doctrine, as the Reconciliation of man to God and not God to man, God being in Christ reconciling the world unto himself (2 Cor. v 19). A number of questions were asked at the close of the address. These meetings will be held weekly at the Atkinson's Cafe on Wednesday evenings, at 8 p.m. throughout April. The subject of the Address Lest Wednesday, will be " Redemption and Salvation" and on April 21,</p>
        <p>" M traDieB." VACUUM CLEANERS, thoroughly reliable machines rom 375. 6d.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmongers, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Tablet to Mrs. Barbauld. We understand that the tablet to indicate the site of the residence of Anna La.titia Atkin (Mrs. Barbauld) in Dial-street (I-Toward Place), is to be unveiled on Thursday next, at three o'clock in the afternoon, ty Mrs. T. J. Ridgway, of Lymm, who is well-known as the authoress of three charming volumes of essays, "The Sweet o' the Year," "The Garden of Content" and "he Gate Beautiful." It is specially appropriate that the memory of the greatest local authoress of the eighteenth century should be honoured by a lady who has done so much to maintain the present literary reputation of the town, and it is hoped that there will be a large attendance The tablet, which contains a medallion of Miss Atkin has been designed and modelled by Mr. J. A. Allan, of the Warrington School of Art, and beautifully cast in bronze.</p>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Society. The annual meeting on Tuesday, April oth was attended by about one-third of the members notwith- standing the fact that it was held in the middle bf the holiday week. The treasurer gave a satisfactory balance sheet to the Society, and was able to announce another increase in the membership though not, of course, so marked as that of last year. The secretary was heartily congratulated on his report of 83 notable a series of meetings as the last 12 months, both summer and winter, has seen. In the election of officers and ccmmittee very few changes were needed. Mr. W. E. Brown, B.A. of Penketh, the President was re-elected, as were also the retiring secretary and treasurer. Mr. Rowlands took Mrs. Lowe's place as hon. portfolio secretary. An attractive series of summer outings was arranged, the places selected ranging from Liverpool (by boat) on the one hand to Lincoln on the other. Warrington Chess Club. The Warrington Chess Club held their final meeting of the season, at Prince's Cafe, Sankey-street, on Thursday, at which the prizes were distributed and a very enjoyable evening was passed. Mr. John New ccimbe, the president, presented the Championship Trophy to Mr Frank Dunn, this being the third time on which he has attained the position of champion, [ and, curiously, at intervals of about ten years on each occasion. Mr Dunn also won the first prize for Ithe handicap, the second prize being won by Mr John R Wood. The past season has been a very success- ful one in every way and there seems every possibility of a steady increase in the membership. The club was formed in ISSO, Mr. Dunn being the only member left of the original founders and we have to thank himself, Mr. Newcombe and Mr. J. R. Wood, who have done everything possible to keep up the good standard [of the club. Intending members can obtain full particulars from the joint secretaries, 8, King-street. Children's Ball at the Gymnasium. The Borough Gymnasium was the scene of a very successful children's ball on Thursday evening, the occasion being the benefit night of Miss Ethel Spinks. About 130 boys and girls were present and as many of theta were in character costumes, the result was a Itirticularly bright and pleasing spectacle. Dancing was engaged in from 7 to 11 p.m., excellent music being supplied by Messrs Wainwright and Burrows. A delightful feature of the evening was a series of fancy dances by pupils of Miss Spinks, the most notable being the difficult and fascinating toe dances. There were also [ a statue dance, hunt dance, hornpipe and skirt dance, [ each of which was most gracefully performed. The duties of M.C. were capably discharged by Mr S L partridge, who was assisted by the following stewards: Messrs J P Taylor, II Rushworth, II J Pearce, Walter Welsby, F Partridge, G Picton, Victor Hughes, G Houghton, R Isherwood and S Denham. The arrange- ments were in the hands of a ladies' committee con- sisting of Mesdames Asthury, A Denham, Lightburn, Taylor, F Parsonage, Partridge, Houghton, Powell, W Proud and Wilkes.</p>
        <p>1 '2</p>
