      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>April, 13, 1912.</head>
        <p>April, 13, 1912.</p>
        <p>TOWN HALL WARD.</p>
        <p>THE FORTHCOMING ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Following the death of Councillor W. Rogers a vacancy has been declared in the Town Hall Ward and the e ection will take place on Thursday,-April 25th. Nominations must be handed in not later than 5 o'clock on Wednesday next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>Mr. Lindley to Stand,</head>
        <p>Mr. Lindley to Stand,</p>
        <p>A crowded meeting of tbe Town Hall Ward Liberal Committee (vas held last evening under the chairmanship of Mr. P. O'Brien. Mr. Firth Lindley was unanimously invited to con- test the vacancy due to the death of Councillor Rogers and accepted. In November 1910, Mr. Lindley con- tested the seat against the late member and was defeated by 39 votes. The poll was Rogers (C), 41G; Lindley (L), 33/</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>END OF THE STRIKE.</head>
        <p>END OF THE STRIKE.</p>
        <p>_MINERS RESUME WORK.</p>
        <p>The great coal war, which has lasted five weeks, and cost the nation at least £15,000,000 is now over. From the Westminster Palace Hotel, before sunset on Saturday scores of telegrams were sent to the various coalfields announcing that the strike was officially at an end. This was the decision of the national conference, approving, by 440,000 to 125,000 on a membership vote, the recommendation of the Federation executive.</p>
        <p>It was glad news to the nation, and especially to thousands of homes in the areas where distress is keen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>COST OF THE WAR.</head>
        <p>COST OF THE WAR.</p>
        <p>ESTIMATED NATIONAL LOSS OF OVER £15,000,000.</p>
        <p>Many months must elapse before it is possibkto estimate, even approximately, the loss to the country of the coal-war now happily endtd. It is probable that, including the miners, close upon 2,000,000 men were out of work. Four days before the end of the strike the fellowiug was the estimate— Miners Other trades 816,450</p>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>, A Liter estimate brings the total up to 2,500,000. The Miners were, of course, out for five weeks, but the others were out for varying periods, beginning from the time when the industries in which they were engaged could tot proceed for lack of coal. In the case of the miners, it is possible to arrive at some idea of what the strike has cost them. Approximately the figures are : Working days Wages Trades. Lost. Lost. Mining Mechan;cs an d labourers Agents, travellers, clerks, &amp;c 2,720,000 ... 1,050,000</p>
        <p>Totals - 49,020,000</p>
        <p>11.870,000</p>
        <p>Other industries affected were about 30 in 'number. _</p>
        <p>This, of course, is only one aspect of the losses which have followed on the coal strike. The railways have suffered eeverely. The official returns of the 31 railway companies of the United Kingdom for the week ended March 31st, showed total receipts amount- ing to £1,392,370, a decrease of £747,000 compared with the corresponding week of last year. It is probable that in five weeks the loss in revenue is 31 millions.</p>
        <p>This added to the figures already given makes a total of £15,370,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>LANCASHIRE'S ATTITUDE.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE'S ATTITUDE.</p>
        <p>H The decision of the National Conference has been followed by a speedy resumption of work in most parts of the country, but in Lancashire there Was a strong movementlin the earlier part ( f the week in favour of remaining on strike until the scheduled minimum rates have been conceded. By Wednesday night, however, a decided reaction had manifested itself among the miners of the insurgent " district of South-east Lancashire, and there was every sign that it would fall into line with the rest of the Lanca- shire colliers and the miners of the cauntry generally and go back to work. The ballot taken at Pendlebury on Wednesday to test the decision of Tuesday's meet- ing against resuming resulted in a reversal of hat decision by a vote of three to one, and v%ithin three hours of this being announced miners were going down the pits.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>TROOPS SENT TO LANCASHIRE.</head>
        <p>TROOPS SENT TO LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>Strike Disturbances at Wigan Pits. There was a startling development. on Wednesday in connection with the aggressive attitude which the "irreconcilables " in the Wigan coalfield have dis- played towards men who have resumed work in the pits, and troops were hurriedly sent. On Wednesday night there had arrived: 300 of the 10th Lancers, 500 Fusiliers, 500 of the Suffolk Regiment. General Bethune was in command, and made Wigan his headquarters. The real secret of the trouble is in the cut districts of Wigan. Gangs of 3 ouths and young men armed with p•eks have raided several collieries and prevented colliers working. There was an attack at Golborne by a crowd from Leigh. '1 he police were in ambush and met the rioters with their batons. About forty were injured and had to be medically attended. Miners from the Pendlebury ard Leigh districts marched to those pits where work was ih progress, and demanded that the men at work should be with- drawn. The authorities annealed for troops, and the 10th Lancers stationed at Norwich were accordingly sent. When the o ders for the departure of the regiment were received the officers' point-to-point races were being held at Ketteringham, about five miles away. The races were abandoned, and later in the afternoon about 300 officers and men left in two special trains. A body of strikers visited Ellerbeck collieries, near Chorley, and after swarming over the engine room and colliery piemises demanded that the men at work below ground should be brought up. To save further trouble the request was corn?lied with. A disturbance also took place on Wednesday at the Pretotia Pit, near Bolton, the scene of the great colliety :disaster in December, 1010. So threatening was the attitude of the strikers that police reinforce- ments were sent for, and when about 100 had arrived they charged the Crowd. There were hand-to-hand encounters:sand the police used their batons. Many men suffered in consequence. Earlier in the day many railings had been broken down, and other damage was done. As a sequel to the disturbances 500 Fusiliers arrived from Lichfield on Wedneslay night at Wigan, and were sent to Leigh. Five hundred I Suffolks, who have been guarding the mine at Chirk, ; where disturbances occurred during the strike, also arrived, and were stationed at the Drill Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Quietness Restored.</head>
        <p>Quietness Restored.</p>
        <p>The presence of the strong force of military in the Wigan and Chorley districts had a quietening effect on Thursday. In the Wigan coalfield many thousands of miners were at work under the protection of the police, w bile squads of soldiers paraded the district The irtesponsibles, however, gave the soldiery a wide berth, and the miners were allowed to pursue their calling without hindrance.</p>
        <p>Slaw thirteen hundred soldiers are now in the disturbed Lancashire districts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Cheshire Lints.</head>
        <p>The Cheshire Lints.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire Lines C3mmittee will, on and from Monday nes', reinstate a number of local trains between Liverpool, Garston, and Warrington. Further additions to the restricted service will be male week by week. It is expected that the Sunday service will be resumed on April 21.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' is a good Cough Mixture. Effectiv e and Economical. Of Chemists only.—Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>POSITION IN WARRINGTON. Much Distress but a More Cheerful</head>
        <p>THE POSITION IN WARRINGTON. Much Distress but a More Cheerful</p>
        <p>Outlook.</p>
        <p>One welcome indication of the improved outlook in Warrington was that early in the week several of the tall chimneys at the works of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company, Ltd., were once more send- ing forth smoke, an outward token that work had already been resumed in several of the mills. About 400 of the workmen who had been thrown idle by the strike have been taken back and it is expected that next week about two-thirds of the total number of employees will again be at work. Messrs. Rylands Bros Ltd., have already resumed work in most of their departments and the whole of the works will be running next week. The Whitecross Company, Ltd., anticipate that a fortnight will elapse before they are able to find employment for all their bands. The position at the works of Messrs. Monks, Ball and Co., Ltd., is also very l indefinite, as the firm is entirely dependent upon a regular supply of fuel and up to the present has,not been able to obtain sufficient to warrant a start being made. Operations will, however, he resumed at the earliest possible moment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>RELIEVING DISTRESS. MORE FUNDS URGENTLY NEEDED.</head>
        <p>RELIEVING DISTRESS. MORE FUNDS URGENTLY NEEDED.</p>
        <p>It must not be thought that with the end of the strike the need for relief work has ceased. On the contrary funds are more urgently needed than ever, for more new cases have passed through the Cei.tral Relief Offices this week than last week. Poor people who have been making heroic efforts to keep the wolf from the door have been rEduced to such sore straits that they have at last been compelled to avail themselves of the fund. Some of the cases which have had to be investigated are of a really heartbreaking character and the marvel is how people have been able to exist under such poverty-stricken conditions as have been revealed.</p>
        <p>• As will be seen from cur advertisement columns, the Mayor is continuing his appeal for funds. Arart from the temporary relief given a foltuight Ago, some 900 families had been dealt with up to Thursday night through the Central Relief Offices. There would appear, however, from the large number of cases investigated during the last two or three days to be every likelihood that this number will be yet further increased. This figure does not by any means represent the total of families relieved, as, owing to the admirable arrangements made by the large works, whose employees are chiefly affected, and by the Bewsey Adult School and by other employers who have kept their works going, a large number of families have been helped other than through the Central Offices. .</p>
        <p>Special attention has been paid to the granting of relief to young children, babies, and invalidi —milk and strengthening foods, such as cod liver oil, bovril, Benger's food, &amp;c., having been given in a number of Cases.</p>
        <p>In some few cases it has been Lund that injudicious purchases have been made by the telieved and in order to avoid a repetition of this, tradesmen upon whom orders for food are given ale requested to supply only those articles mentioned on the order form As far as can be seen at present it will be necessary to continue the relief for another fortnight, and the Committee feel that even then, in the cases of mothers and babieP, it would be too early to cease. The Mayor regrets that it is necessary to make a further appeal for support, and whilst deeply glateful to those who have already helped, he hopes this intimation will convince those who have been waiting in older to see the requirements, that there is urgent need of their assistance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>PERFORMANCES AT THE THEATRE.</head>
        <p>PERFORMANCES AT THE THEATRE.</p>
        <p>The praiseworthy effort of members of those excellent amateur organizations—the Warrington Musical Society and the Warrington Dramatic Society —to assist the Mayor's fund for the relief of distress in the town aroused a degree of interest and enthusiastic support which was altogether gratifying. The audience at the Royal Court Theatre last evening when the firat of the two performances began was large and representative, every part of the theatre being comfortably filled. It would indeed have been surprising had it been otherwise, for not only had the cause itself an undubitable claim -upon generous sympathy but the programme was of such a high-class and admirably varied character as to attract on its merits. The uprising curtain disclosed an assembly of some of the best known members of the Musical Society to wh, m the first part of the entertainment was entrusted. It took the form, for the most part, of a succession of delightful excerpts fi ono leading operas, including some in which the society's operatic section has won its laurels. First of all we had the sparkling madrigal " When the budding bloom of May " from " Haddon Hall," which was tastefully sung and proved a capital foretaste of the pleasures of the evening. Then the quintet "Love is meant to make us glad," from " Merrie England " was sung with a crispness and gaiety which could hardly have been improved upon. Miss Nellie Anderton revived pleasant memories of her triumph as Queen Eliz ibeth. in "Merrie England," by a stately, inspiring render- ing of "Oh, peaeeful England," her cultured voice being used with most artistic effect, and a vigorous eccore was accorded. Mrs A Booth, Mrs E A Sanders, Mr J Rabbitt and Mr F Fieldhonse were associated in the popular quartette "Strange Adventure" from the "Yeoman of the Guard." The item went with a nice swing lut the effect was somewhat marred by a decline in pitch. Miss Sara Silcock enhanced her reputation as one of the most pleasing of our local vocalists by a piquant and finished rendering of" Valley of Laughter" (Sanderson) and the desire for a repetition was unmis- takably manifested. The next item was in a tragic vein, Mrs A Booth and Mr J Rabbitt giving the Miserere scene from "Ii Trovatore" in highly dramatic and soulful style. Each soloist rose to the occasion and the effect was heightened by the solemn chanting of the monks' chorus by the male members of the party. The quartette" A regular Royal Queen" from The Gondoliers " was sung with refreshing gusto by Mrs Booth, Miss Taylor, Mr. F. Fieldhouse and Mr. J. H. Draper. The first part of the programme closed with the quartette " Four jolly sailormen "from " Princess of Kensington "the breezy seamen being represented with quaintly humorous touches by Messrs. F. Field- house, J. Poole, G. H. Smith and W. Ray. Mr. Frank H. Crossley tactfully conducted the various concerted items and 'Mr. W. 11. Payton, Mug. Bac., accompanied with his usual taste and judgment. The second part of the programme, on which per- haps the greatest interest was focussed,was Sir Arthur Conan Doyle's one act play " Waterloo " by members of the Dramatic Society assisteoy Mr. Harold Smith, M.P. This touching little story of the ever-memorable battle was enacted in a powerful aud realistic way which held the audience spellbound. Mr. Haro!d Smith added another to his histrionic successes by a remarkably clever presentment of the tottering, withered veteran, weak and tremulous-voiced yet always taking a pride in past glories. The sudden climax, in which the corporal after re-living for ene moment his exploits in the field, falls back dead, was realized in thrilling style, and the audience applauded again and again. Miss Gladys Adams took the part of Nora Brewster, the corporal's grandniece, with befitting simplicity; Mr. Sidney Wallhead was a cheery, confident Sergeant Archie McDonald, and Ur. G. R. Williamson, as Col. James Midwinter, carried himself with dignity allied with tender solicitude for the veteran. The orchestra, under Mr. Tom Boothroyd's con- ductorship,played choice selections. The arrangements were admirably carried out by a committee with Mr. J. W. Ballen as hon. secretary. , THE PROCEEDS. The hon. secretary has kircvarded to the Mayor a cheque ter S-50 on account of the proceeds of the performances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>. HIPPODROME MATINEE.</head>
        <p>. HIPPODROME MATINEE.</p>
        <p>In aid of the Mayor's Distress Fund a special matinee was given at the Hippodrome on Saturday afternoon. The artistes who had appeared during the 1 week and several local entertainers kindly gave of their best, and the programme was nicely varied and most interesting throughout. It was anticipated that the seating accommodation would be fully occupied, but unfortunately the audience was comparatively small. The staff of the Hippodrome and the orchestra gave their services willingly, and the cost of printing, posting and advertising the event was generously borne by Mr. Macnaghten. Amongst the locals who appeared was the popular young comedian, Mr. Wilily Farrington, who highly amused the audience with his absurdities. Although the effort did not receive adequate support from the public, the management's good intentions will be appreciated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Taylor—Norcott.</p>
        <p>A quiet but interesting wedding was solemnised at Great Sankey Church on Thursday afternoon, the con- tracting parties being Mr. Wilfred Taylor, third son of Mr. Frederick Taylor, of Manchester-road, Warrington, and Miss Norcott, of Burtonwood. There were only a few friends present at the ceremony which was performed by the Rev. E. Powell, vicar of Greenfield.</p>
        <p>Sampson---Taylor. There was a pretty wedding at the Parish Church on Tuesday, when Mr. Bernard, younger son of Mr. George Samps-,n, C.C., Ingfield House, Deepcart Sheffield, was married to Miss Lucy Taylor, third daughter of Mr. Samuel Taylor, Rockside, Manchester- road, Warrington. The bride and her parents are prominently associated with the Parish Church and the ceremony aroused great interest, a large congrega- tion assembling. The Rev. Canon Willis officiated, assisted by the Rev W C Edgingstone, of Bolsterstone, Yorkshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>Horsfleld—Grees,</head>
        <p>Horsfleld—Grees,</p>
        <p>The marriage was solemnized at the Gospel Hall, Forster-street, on Easter Monday, of Mr. John H. Horsfleld, son of Mr. W. Horsfield, of Orford-avenue, and Miss Maud Green, daughter of Mr. James Green, of Marshhouse-late. Considerable interest was taken in the event, and a large congregation assembled. Mr. J. Hixon Irving, of Liverpool, conducted the service. Mr. W. Street was at the organ, and played Mendelssohn's Wedding Much and other selections. The bride, who was given away by her father, wore a beautiful dress of ivory silk, with veil and wreath of orange blossom, and carried a lovely bouquet of lilies, tulips and orange blossom. The bridesmaids were Miss Minnie Green and Miss Elsie Green who were attired in sate blue voile dresses, with sate blue and black picture hats. Their bouquets were composed of daffodils and their ornaments were gold brooches, the gifts of the bridegroom. Mr Walter Wright discharged the duties of best man. After the ceremony a reception was held at the bride's home and the happy couple left for London, the bride travelling in a grey txeed costume with black picture bat trimmed with ostlich feathers. Numerous presents were ieceived, including a set of carvers from the bride's Sunday school class at the Goscel Hull.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The Easter week programme at the Hippodrome is one of the best ever compiled for local patrons of the music-hall. Every turn has some special merit, and the infinite variety of the " bill "in an additional attraction. Quite recently a stock-whip expert has been honoured by the King, and after seeing Captain Jack Kelly. and Miss Violet Kelly demonstrate some of the marvels which are possible to the trained manipulator of these fearsome weapons, we no longer wonder at the delight expressed by His ' Majesty and other members of the Royal Family. Captain Kelly is a fine specimen of the Australian. "corn-stalk," and one finds the whip-play in hie sinewy hands a fascinating study. It seems quite easy (when you know how) to flick the ash off a 1 cigarette with a forty-foot whip, or play the National Anthem with a pair of cracking thongs. Miss Kelly is no mean assistant, and adds., much brightness to an already attractive "turn.' A"' striking "romance of the golden west" is pourtrayed Miss Jean Marvin and Mr Thos Hyde, the latter taking both the masculine roles in effective style. The sketch is dramatic throughout and provides an unexpected denouement. Some really original and humorous songs and jokes are presented by that accomplished pair of comedians, Messrs Downes and Langford, of Drury-lane fame. The Sados excel as novelty jugglers and some clever big-boot and_step dancing is presented by the Three Cape Girls. One of the smartest " diunken " comedy turns we have seen for a long time is that presented by Hunter and Bob. The dumb show of Hunter, combined with artistic tumb- ling, is irresistibly funny. Nellie Denver, Australian comedienne, earns applause in the usual song and dance. There are some excellent pictures. and the music of Mr. Widdop's orchestra never lacks lightness or melody.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>NEXT WEEK.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK.</p>
        <p>Patrons of the Hippodrome are in:f .4. another good show next week and the top liners are Shields and Rodgers, who will give remarkable exhibitions of lasso throwing. Shirley and Ransome will give a blend of Cockney humour and pathos. The Wedburns are sure to piove amusing and entertaining and the programme will also include The Rurals, in a comedy musical scene and James Dixon, the Lane 'shire baritone and, other popular leaders in the music-hall world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
