      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Easter Day. at. the Parish Church.</head>
        <p>Easter Day. at. the Parish Church.</p>
        <p>The great festival of Easter Day was marked by services of a joyous and inspiring character at the Warrington Parish Church. The altar, font, pulpit and choir stalls had been very prettily decorated with lilies, daffodils and other spring flowers. There were several early celebrations of the Holy Communion, followed by matins and a choral celebration at 10.30 the music being beautifully sung by the choir. Eyre's line setting in E flat was used for the greater part of the Eucharistic service. The Rev A J Brett preached on the words "The third day He rose again, according to the Scriptures." There was a crowded congregation at Evensong which commenced with the hymn "Jesus Christ is risen +o-clay " sung as a processional. The choir gave a finished interpretation or Nlresley's anthem "Blessed be the God and Father." The Rector preached en several aspects of the Resurrection, his text being "Whether it were lor they, so we preach and so ye have believed. . .But some man will say : How are the dead raised up, and with what body do they come ?" After the Blessing the Hallelujah Chorus was sung. Mr FM Darby, Mus. Bac. ably presided at the organ at, all the services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>"It Should Not Be."</head>
        <p>"It Should Not Be."</p>
        <p>We have passed (siys the "Buttonwood Parish Magazine") through a never-to-be-forgotten March. Our history cannot point to a time of industrial strife and unrest a time of "strike" privation comparable to that of 1912. It should not be. Wears Christians, we are brethren, we have a duty man to mitt, we are called upon to dwell together in love, yet we have bitter industrial war, mines unworked, mills idle, workshops closed down, railways and electric cars on half time or less, street lighting partially suspended difficul- ties of locomotion daily on the increase, house fires in reduced circumstances food stuffs at abnormal prices, pawnshops over-stocked,empty tills and still more empty purses, inconvenience, discomfort, shortage, want, poverty, semi-starvation on every side, in city, town, village and hamlet, especially in those centres where workers congrevate. Again, we say, it should not be ! Our terrible plight is self-inflicted ;it dishonours us as a civilised naticn, it demonstrates to a rathematical certainty the hollowness of that Christianity of which the basis principle is the Brotherhood of Man ! How Is it possible to lore as brethren, to be pitiful to be courteous when we have assiduously created an atmosphere of mistrust and suspicion in which we have lived and moved and had our being for years past ? The root evil of the industrial and sr cial tragedy is mistrust. We no longer trust one another. We view; one another with suspicion. The workman no longer trusts the employer, the employer looks upon the labourer, not as a fellow-helper with him but as a profit-making machine. The Minimum Wage Bill might as fitly be termed "the Industrial Distrust Bill" On the ODA band there is the demand for inclnsion of " the schedule " in the Bill, on the other, insistence upon safeguards." An house built upon mistrust rests upon the worst possible foundation—it cannot stand. When this great disastrous strike is over, when industrial warfare, for the time being, is at an end there can be no assurance of lasting peace until mistrust has been cast out and mutual confidence restored. We must not be blind to the final cause of our miserable plight nor to the main issue at state—the permanent restoraticn of the Christian spirit into our industrial relations and into our social life as a whole. The spirit of brotherhood is sssertine I itself ; to fight against it is to light against God. " Sirs ! ye are brethren. Why do ye wrong one to another ?" When shall we learn that money is not the chief good, that there is something better than perpetual grubbing, on the muck heap?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>Mr. D. McEachren presided over a fair attendance at the meeting of this society 03 Sunday. The speaker was Mr. U. Smiley, who spoke upon The Resurrec-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Wesley Guild National Conference.</head>
        <p>Wesley Guild National Conference.</p>
        <p>Six hundred delegates of the Wesley Guilds arrived in London on Saturday for the third National Confer- ence. They include council, secretaries, guild secre- taries and elected representatives from England, Scotland, Ireland, Wales, the Isle of Mae, and the Channel Islands. The conference meetings are being held at the Leysian Hall and Wesley's Chapel, City- road. In the afternoon there was a gathering of secretaries of district and sectional councils, when papers were read by Mr FL Jenkinson, of Birmingham ; the Rev Walter Rees, of Newcastle ; Mr Lake, of Newcastle ; and Mr G P Hargreaves, junr., vice- president of the council. The pipers generally dealt with the cases of success or failure in the working of Wesley Guilds.</p>
        <p>Departure of the Rev. Father Dowley.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April, 13, 1912.</p>
        <p>THANKS. Councillor Craik proposed a vote of thanks to the ladies of the parith for their kind services in so beautifully decorating the church on special occasions in the Church's Near. Mr Isherwood seconded and the vote was warmly passed.</p>
        <p>Mr R Done moved a similar compliment to Mr Darby (organist) and the choir. He said the musical portion of the services was much appreciated by the town in general, and it attracted people from outside the parish to worshipat the old church. Mr Hopkins seconded aud the vote was heartily agreed to.</p>
        <p>ST. PETER'S CHURCH. PARTY POLITICS PREDOMINATE.</p>
        <p>The annual Easter vestry meeting of St. Peter's Church was held in the vestry on Wednesday evening. The Rev B Percival presided and Councillor Lashford submitted the church wardens accounts which showed that there was a balance in hand of £9 2s. lld. but rgainst that there was an anticipatory expenditure of £3O for the paving of the church walks, which work the councillor pointed out, would be commenced as early as possible. That would leave a debit balance of £2O 17s. Id. The vicar congratulated the church wardens on the satisfactory balance sheet. He had some funds in hand towards the paving awl he thought they would not be quite as much behind as the balance sheet indicated. 'I here would be a deficit but he thought they would not have much difficulty in getting straight. He had great pleasure in appointing Councillor T S Steel as his warden for the year because he had always helped him (the vicar) in every way. Both he and Councillor Lashford were great helpers in the work of the parish. One of the reasons for the prosperity of the parish was that they had real harmony .existing between the workers. The number of communicants was larger than it had ever been during his nineteen years' ministry in that parish. They had also touched the record in the Easter offerings. He was very grateful to all the people who had helped in the work of the church, the officers, choir-boys and organist. They had made steady progress, which was much better than progress- ing by leaps and bounds. Councillor Steel thanked the Vicar for appointing him his warden.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr Bamford, seconded by Mr T Reddish, Councillor Lashford was appointed people's arden and Mr J Bamford end Mr J W Kay were nomicated as 1-iy representatives to tho ruri-decanal conference.</p>
        <p>Councillor Steel appointed Mr B =forth as his sides- man and Mr Reddish was appointed by Councillor Laahford as his sidesman. Messrs Webster and Clayton were re-appointed auditors. The otter sidesmen appointed by the Vicar were : Messrs J Cowden, Robert Knight,JosephJ liands,James Taylor,E Clayton, J W Ray, G J McQuillan and Thomas Ince. The vestry appointed as sidesmen Messrs George Greaves, Robert B•ownbill, J Berry, T M Wratislavv, G Browne, W Pinson and W Dickens.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Councillor Steel, secondel by Mr Bamforth, the following resolution was passed : "That this meeting learns with regret of the serious illness of Mr Wratislaw and expresses the hope that he will soon he restored t)</p>
        <p>WELSH DISESTABLISH:HE:4E.</p>
        <p>Mr Bamford 'p-r-O-p.c&gt;seci -ft:W(l -iii-inCe-a.e-Oo—nied a resolu- tion protesting against the Disestiblishment of tie Church in Wales.</p>
        <p>A SCANDALOUS QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Mr J J Hands, in supporting, said he felt it was his duty, and the bounden duty of every Churchman in Great Britain, to express their feeling on that most scandalous question being brought before the country by the present Government. Probably some of them remembered that Mr Gladstone when the question of disestablishing the church was introduced, refused to entertain it. Why ? He gave as his reason that the Church in Wales was a live and progressive church. It was wrong for the present Government to try and plunder the Church and rob it of what they had no just claim upon. He could not understand why the Prime Minister and the Chancellor of the Exchequer should lend then selves to the support of such a Bill, seeing that those two gentlemen got their early education in a school belonging to the Church, which they were now willing to plunder and rob. Why were they doing it ? Beciuse they had clergy connected with the Church who dared to go on a platform and show their disagree- ment with the Licensing Bill which was brought before the Government a few years ago. The Chancellor had said then that he would</p>
