      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>MOTHER SEIGEIS</head>
        <p>MOTHER SEIGEIS</p>
        <p>'SYRUP</p>
        <p>EASTER VESTRY MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The annual Easter vestry meeting in connection with the Warrington Parish Church, was held in Fairfield schools at noon on Thursday. The Rector (Rev Canon Willie) presided over a good attendance, which included the Revs A .1 Brett, T Vickers and J M Buckmaster, Councillor J U Craik (rector's warden), Mr E Isher- wood (people's warden), and Mr E A White (vestry clerk). THE FINANCES. Mr Isherwood presented the balance shret, which showed that the year began with an adverse balance of £lB 12s 4d Through the kindness of several friends that was wiped off, and the year had closed with an adverse balance of £3 Its 7d, which he thought was very favourable when they took into consideration several extra amounts they were compelled to spend, which would not be required in 1912. Ihe total expenditure for the year was £713 14s 3d and the income was £709 19s Bd. The offertories totalled £589 4s Bd, made up of general offertoi ins (church expenses), £3Ol 14s lid ; special offertoriea. £284 9s 9,1. The total number of coins contributed was 71,477. Mr S Taylor moved the adoption of the =counts. He said the people's warden was to be greatly compli- mented upon the manner in which he attended to his duties, lie gave every satisfaction.</p>
        <p>Mr It Antw-iss seconded and it was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ELECTIO.N OF OFFICERS-.</head>
        <p>ELECTIO.N OF OFFICERS-.</p>
        <p>The Rector said Councillor Craik had again consented to be his warden. He (Canon Willis) had often said Councillor Craik was as acceptable to the people as he was to himself and the congregation would no doubt be gratified that he VIEIs willing to continue his work. (Hear, hear.) ,1 Councillor Craik thanked the Rector for his continued confidence in him. It was a great honour to him to be proposed year after par for that office and he was only too pleased to serve the Rector and the church to the best of his ability. Mr. T. J. Hopkins proposed the re-election of Mr. E. Isherwood as people's warden. He said Mr. Isherwood did his best for the church and loved it dearly and when they got the right man in the right place it behoved them to keep him there. Councillor LOCKER seconded. He said that prior to undertaking that important office, Mr. Isherwood had taken a deep interest in Church work and his continu- ance in the work was something they had need to prize. The proposition was heartily agreed to. Mr. Isherwood, in returning thanks, said he would do all he possibly could for the good of the Church and for the parishioners. Council! or Craik nominated Councillor Locker as his sidesman and Mr. Isherwood nominated Mr. Samuel Taylor. The Rector said they deeply regretted the loss of ono of their sidesmen, the late Mr. F. G. Taylor. There were nine nominations for the eight sidesmen required and the following were declared elected by show of hands :—Messrs Joseph Hill, R W Collin, J T ‘Vhittle, R. \V Antwiss, G b' Knott, G W A Brown, R T Harding and Robert Lee. Mr R Done : I challenge those votes deliberately. Councillor Craik explained that the votes of the Rector and the wardens had been taken into account in adding up the figures. Mr Done : I withdraw the challenge, but at the sometime I am not satisfied. The sidesmen elected for the missions were : St. George's, Mr Robert Sumner ; St.Clernent's, Mr Joseph Houghton. Messrs P C Price and J T Whittle were re-elected auditors and Mr E A White was re-appointed vestry clerk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>TH.E RECTOR'S ADDRESS.</head>
        <p>TH.E RECTOR'S ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>The RECTOR, in commenting upon the financial position, said it was very satisfactory to feel that the receipts from all sources were about £7OO and the deficit was only £3. They bad about £3OO in the special repair fund and they would need all they could get for that purpose. It would be satisfactory to the parishioners to know that the church WA now insured for £30,000 and the other . parochial buildings for £20,000. He was delighted to see from the parochial statement that there was a balance in hand on nearly all the accounts. They all felt most thankful for the results of their Lenten and Eister services. They had never before had such a large number of communicants, except on one occasion. There wera nearly 1,000 this year. without any pressure whatever being put upon them to attend. He wished to thank the church- wardens particularly for their care of the church and churchyard and for the interest they were taking in the erection of the new vestry for which they would need the 000 already in hand and more. He also wanted publicly to thank the three clergy of the parish for their services during the past sear. Everybody had appreciated the wont that had been done. (Applause). The coestion of St. Clement's Mission would have to be brougsat before a special meeting of the Church Council. He was told that the mission was filled to overcrowding and that some course must be taken. The Rector concluded by thanking the congregation for their kind offering to himself on Easter Day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>HAVE MS REVENGE</head>
        <p>HAVE MS REVENGE</p>
        <p>and tried to take it by the Budget which he brought forward. He said to the Irish " support us in this and we will support you in Ireland." lie (the speaker) supported the leader of the Opposition, Mr Boner Law, when he said on Tuesday that the present Government was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>GOVERNMENT OF SALE.</head>
        <p>A GOVERNMENT OF SALE.</p>
        <p>They had sold tiie-Con-siitnt-i-o-n,-eolci—ii;eroselves, and wanted to sell Ulster,ibut Ulster was not for sale. They wanted to sell the Established Church in Wales, but it was not theirs to sell. He would say : God forbid that the Church be plundered and robbed. The VICAR said it was a matter of history that the State did not found the Church because there was the Church existing before there was a State as it was called now. If it could be just to rob the Church then he had misapprehended Christianity. Councillor STEEL agreed with the resolution and said that if the Bill was passed it would be said that what was good for Wales would be good for England. He proposed a vote of thanks to the Vicar for presiding, which was heartily carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>SI. PAUL'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>SI. PAUL'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The Vicar (11-e-V- fq—lir—ac—ec—am—p—)-Tresided over the annual vestry meeting which was held in the vestry on Thursday evening. There was a good attendance which included the Revs E E Peters and J H Bankes. Messrs H Smethurst and .1 Rigby (viardens) and W D Jeans, jun. (vestry clerk). Before the formal business began the Vicar made a feeling allusion t 3 the death of Mr F G C Bennett, a former sidesrcan, who, he said, loved the church and was a great help to the parish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>ACCOUNTS.</head>
        <p>THE ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>Mr Rigby presented the accounts which showed that the year began with a balance in hand of £4 Is 9d. The total income was £397 Os, the offertories, which amounted to £334 Is 63,including :—Clergy Fund, £7O 17s 10d; Church restoration, £l4 Gs 4d ; sick and poor, £ll 03 101 ; various charities, £ll 13s 10 ; Church Expenses, £l7O 15s lid. The expenditure amounted to £395 Oa Id, and there was now a balance in baud of £2 8s lid. On the Church Restoration Fund there had been an expenditure of £2B 4s and the balance in hand was £1 4s 3d. Mr Rigbv concluded by announcing that he could not offer himself for re-election as people's warden.</p>
        <p>J Ilot GIITON moved the adoption of the accounts which, he said, were kept aith Mr. Rigby's usual accuracy. Mr HANKINSON seconded and it was agreed to. The VICAR painted out that the number of coins collected was 3,000 less than laq year. He was afraid the hot weather In the summer affected the attendance and there were also two fewer Sundays in the year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>VICARS ADDRESS.</head>
        <p>THE VICARS ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>The VICAR, iir-th-e'courre '-o—f-i;TeWiew of the year's work, tendered his heartfelt thanks to the various voluntary workers in the parish. First of all, he thanked his two colleagues, the Revs. E E Peters and J II Banker, for their loyalty during the past year. They were worthy men and had dose excellent work— in fact no Vicar could possibly have two better colleagues. The Vicar also expressed his indebtedness to the organist and choir, district visitors and the ladies' flower guild. He then thanked Mrs Lindley and Mrs Locker who,by means of a tea and subscribing liber- ally themselves,had presented the church with a beauti- ful new altar frontal which was used forthe first time on Easter Day. Thanking the parishioners generally for the way they had rallied round him, the Vicar said it had been a year of trial but they had managed fairly well. The balance sheet was a very satisfactory one. St. Paul's was not a rich parish. They were always begging sni as long as there was a St. Paul's parish and as long as they had got a Vicar, if he want3d to keep things going he would always have to beg,because there was no other way when their endowment was so small and their needs were so greet. With regard to the spiritual side of the work, they had cause to thank God and take courage. lie was greatly cheered on Easter Day by the large number of communicants—over 800 (including the Mission). Comparing that with his first Easter Day at. St. Faure, he found there were three times as many communicants as when he took charge, and it was_most.gratifying_to him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFiCERS..</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFiCERS..</p>
        <p>The VICAR nen nottitatcd Mr 5 A Smethurst as hig warden, remarking that no man could have a better warden. Mr Smethurst had been a true friend to him end a wonderful help. There was one dark blot on their meeting, and that was that he was sorry Mr Rigby ! had made up his mind definitely to give up the warden- ship. Ile bad been a splendid people's warden and k•s_d kept the accounts beautifully. His resignation metier a break in long and faithful service. All efforts to induce Mr Rigby to reconsider his decision baying failed, Mr Albert C Binks was unani- mously elected to succeed him on the proposal of Mr J H Smethurst (who referred to the entirely harmonious relations ahich had existed between Mr Rigby end himself), seconded by Mr JAMES HOUGUTON. A resolution, placing on record appreciation of Mr Rigby's work as people's warden during the last six years, was passed on the proposition of Mr SMETHURST, seconded by Mr H F GREENOUGIi, and supported by the Rev E E PETERS and Mr E WILKES. Mr Rion? in returning thanks, said that although he was relinquishing the wardenship he did not want them to think be should sever his connection with the church, as he would probably continue to work for it in many branches. Mr Smethurst nominated Mr S A H Mackey as his sideman and Mr Binks nominated Mr T Seed. The following sidesnaen were elected by the vestry: Messrs John Hayes, W H Thompson, .1 Pennington, II L Clarke, H Flood, W Woodcock, H F Greenough, II Hankinson, J Lawton, J W Parker, J Wainwright, W Hosker, H Broadhurst, Thomas Locker, W T Flood, S Bailey, James Houghton, E White, JII J Hampson, F , Lindley, G Newburn, T Atherton, T Armstrong, Lieutenant Ingram, It G S Finney, H Collin, S Lawton, and Cank ; All Saints' Mission: Messrs Boyle, Garner, Perks, Rena right, Morris, Boston, Crank and Bigney. Mr. James Houghton was re-elected auditor.</p>
        <p>THANKS. The Rev 3 H Makes proposed a vote of thanks to</p>
        <p>the wardens, sidesmen, auditor and all church workers, including the choir. Mr Wood seconded and the vote was approved. ALL SAINT'S MISSION. Mr J H Smethurst presented the accounts for All Saints' Mission, showing an income of £5B 5s 3cl, and a balance in hand of £1 after creating the special expenditure of renewing the besting apparatus. There was a credit balance oa the Mission extension account of £22 5s 9d.</p>
        <p>The VICAR said the projected extension of the mission had not been allowed to drop—in fact they were keener than ever about it. Something must be done as soon as possible. They had met and discussed a s?.herne and if it had not been for the coal strike, they w0,,1d have appealed to outside friends to help them. An appeal would be issued as soon as things were settled and he hoped the vestry would take the matter up in earnest and see what they could do to extend the work at the mission because it was an absolute necessity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>ST. ANN'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. ANN'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The annual Easter vestry meeting in connection with St. Ann's Church was held on Tuesday evening. Ihe Vicar (the Rev. James Davies) presided and amongst others present were : The Rev J F Shirtcliffe (curate), Mr T H Corbett (vicar's warden), Mr T S Jolley (people's warden), Mr Armstrong (vestry clerk), Mr W Milner (organist and choirmaster), and Messrs T Boswell, T aylor, C Bradshaw and 8 Daniels. The Vicar, in opening the proceedings, said he was very grateful to all who had helped them in their work and especially the church officers, the wardens, sides- men, choir and the organist. The singing had been greatly improved, which was due in a great measure to Mr Milner, who had spent a great deal of time in train- ing the boys. Those who had helped in the Sunday school and the district visitors were also deserving of praise and thanks. (Hear. hear.) Thera was one thing which stared them in the face, and that was the organ renovation. Those who had seen the instrument would recognise that it wos being more than renovated, it would be practically a new organ. When it was completed it would be one of the most efficient organs in the town and it would be an instrument of which they would be proud. They would need more than the £250 which it was estimated the alterations would cost, because it was no good spoiling the work for the sake of a few pounds. The Vicar nominated as his warden, Mr Corbett, and Mr Jolley was re-elected people's warden. The sidesmen were re-eleeted en bloc. The Vicar thanked Mr Armstrong for his services as vestry clerk and also Mr Daniels the church clerk.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of the Vicar seconded by Mr. Jolley a resolution protesting against the proposed disestabl shment of the Church in Wales was carried unanimously and it was decided to send copies of the resolution to the Prime Minister, the Home Secretary, Mr Bona'. Law, M P., Mr Harald Smith, M.P., and Lord Wolmer, M. P. Mr Jolley submitted the balance-sheet, which showed a balance in hand of ..£8 us 3d after paying expenses, as compared with a balance of £1 153 3d Had previon3 year. Messrs G Armstrong and II 11 Bretherton were appointed the representitives to the Ruri-Decanal and Diocesan Conference,. HOLY TRINITY CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The annual vestry was held on Eister Monday evening the Rev E C E Carleton presiding. Among others present were the Rev J Lynn Parry (curate), and Mr W Gill (vicar's warden), Mr John Hesketh (people's warden), and Mr George F F White (vestry clerk).— The accounts were presented by Mr Gill, who stated that the wardens commenced the year with a balance of £4 6s 63 in hand. The special offertories amounted to £B9 us Id (an increase of £24 14s), and the general offertory to £167 15s (£2B Os lid less). The income from all sources was £284 16s Si. They had added to the curate fund £25, and to the renovation fund £2O. A balance was left of £1 9s lid which he thought was fairly satisfactory. (Hear, bear.) On the motion of Mr T D 'Amos, seconded by Mr E Rose, the accounts were passed.- --</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The Vicar re--"aPPCia-e-dirr-Gilit;;lis warden, and Mr John ilesketh was re-elected people's warden.—Mr Gill nominated Mr Ferguson as his sidesman and Mr ilesketh selected Mr T Welsby.—The other sidesrnen appointed by the vestry were : Messrs James Harding, James Bowcock, E D3ke, W Gandy, G Gill, J .1 Hesketh, J Hignett, T Hunt, H Milling, J Pickles, E Rose, A Walsh, 1' Emmett, L R ROWSOD, W W Leigh, Taylor, W Jeans, T Domville, W Turner and T Amos.—Mr White was unanimously re-appointed vestry clerk.and Mr H W Naylor auditor.—The wardens, choir and Vicar were warmly thanked for their services.— On the motion of the Vicar, seconded by Mr John Elesketb, the following resolution was unanimously carried :—" The parishicners of Holy Trinity Church, in Easter vestry msernbled, strongly protest against the proposal to dismember the Church by cutting off from the Province of Canterbury tbe four Welsh dioceses, and to despoil it by confiscating to secular uses endowments consecrated to the service of God ; and this vestry further urges upon all Christian people, irrespective of political party, to unite in uncom- promising resistence to any Bill embodying such proposals."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>ST. JAMES', LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>ST. JAMES', LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>The Rev H B Firth presided at the Easter vestry meeting in connection with St James' Church, Latch- ford, which was held in the schoolroom on Thursday evening. In his opening remarks he said they had every reason to congratulate themselves on the internal con- dition of the church which showed that the church- wardens recognised their duties in that respect. He had great pleasure in appointing Mr W H Stansfield as his warden for the third time. Mr Stanefield had nominated Mr Acton to be his sidesman. On the proposition of Mr Grounds, seconded by Mr Woodward, Mr J Eckersley was unanimously appointod people's warden. Mr Eckersley thanked the people for the confidence they had placed in him ant nominated Mr Hall as his sidesman.-0a the proposi- tion of Mr Eckersley, seconded by Mr Farglaer, a resolution protes,ing against the Disestablishment of the Church in Wales was heartily carried. At this juncture, the Vicar said as time went on in the parish, the people were taking a wider interest in the church and by getting a large body of people interested, the church was bound to prosper. Mr Swift had left them in June and they were sorry to loe him. He was, however, doing well at Crewe. It was a tiibute to Mr Swift that when he preached on Hospital Sunday ,they had one of the largest congregations they had ever bad in the church. Seeing that they had not such a large amount of money they were leaving the position of third curate vacant and he along with Mr Curable would endeavour to do all they could for the good of the parish. During th 3 year they had bad before them two great schemes. The most prominent was that of the building of a permanent church for St. Ililda's Mission. More than £7OO had been collected in aid of the fund and they would be glad when they got their first £l,OOO. The plans had been prepared and sent to the Diocesan Society from which they wera expect- ing a grant. Then thera was the proposed new organ. They hoped the fund would get a good send-off on the 12th May. lie thanked them for the generous offerings at Christmas and Easter. They ought to be encoursg ad by the large congregations. The number of communi- cants at Easter was larger than ever before, and they were making distinct progress. Mr Starafield and Mr Fargher were elected lay repre- sentatives to the ruri-decanal conference. The follow- ing were elected sidemen : Messrs Astley, Eckersley, Harding, Heath, Grounds, Hodge, Woodward and Todd.</p>
        <p>_ &gt; The danger of simple injuries is increased at I cleaning-down time be- cause of the myriads of disease germs that are disturbed with te dust</p>
        <p>and which quickly poke their way into any exposed wound, and set up a bad skin-trouble. Zam-Buk, being prepared by a secret process and possessing antiseptic powers that no cheap ointment has, is the only balm equal to destroying this menace. Dress any cut, scratch, or bruise with Zam-Buk, and the way is at once closed to disease•breeding germs. while perfect healing is assured.</p>
