        <p>1911. Mr Thomas Jackson moved, and Mr J M Crog seconded the adoption of: the accounts vvhich agreed. The Vicar re-appointed Mr Oreerag arden. On the motion of Mr T 11 Westb! _</p>
        <p>seconded by Mr Jackson, Mr Les was re-apPol people's warden.—The following were appointed 8 men : Messrs W Richardson, A Down, T R Westb] W Mclntyre, T Sbarrocks, L G Davies, J Parkes, Willett, J Melbourne, 111 H Brady, Thomas Jacked Worrell, J Swinton, S McDowell and J ltichso</p>
        <p>Jun. MezST3 F Pearson and W Mclntyre "v`i re-appointed the lay repr,-sentatives.—The Vicar;Et posed a vote of thanks to the organist and choirro tri (Mr F Rose), the officers of the church and the visitors.--Mr Greenall seconded, and the motiell, carried.—Thanks were also accorded to Mrs Dowo'd had kindly provided new surplices for the choir,oo, Mr A Down for his gift of new books to the choir bri; The Vicar announced that he expected the nevicvb Ito he with them in June. It would be ar4 lief (the Vicar) and the parish when he came.—The thanked the congregation for their handsome offti'd on Easter Day. He added that all present WoLoi pleased to learn that they had a record number 0v municants, viz., NI. GRAPPENHALL CHURCH. t The annual vestry meeting was held on Tuescisi $ Rect. )r p•esiding. The accounts presented bid churchwarden, Mr H S Leyland, were adoptee di passed. The Rector nominated Mr Henry Stsd° I Leyland as churchwarden, and the vestry appoint Oswald Lofthouse as the other warden. The 0:-$ nominated Mr Reginald W Wilde as sideman, 513%! vestry appointed as the other sidesmen—Messrs croft, Brierley, D%mmelow, Evans,Leigh,Loma.ol°l.l Peacock, F Smith, N Smith, and 15 instenleY•lb vestry appointed Messrs 0 Lofthouse and F lay representatives. A hearty vote of thanks was to Mr Arthur Down for his services as organist " afternoon services and on many other occasions.</p>
        <p>WALTON CHURCH. The vestry meeting was held on Monday, ther,V! (the Rev L J T Harwell) in the c air. Mr (people's warden) handed to the Vicar a checlu_t £8 4s. Id. as the Easter offering from the congree, The appointment of wardens was then proceed 1c Sir Gillert Greerall, Bart., C.V.0., and Mr C° continuing in office. The accounts, showing Red! balance in hand, were next submitted and fass resolution against the disestahlishment and Mead meat of the Church in Wales was agreed to, 10 meeting concluc'el with votes of thanks to the 0115 and choir for their services, to Mrs Harwell 01.:( efficient assistance at the organ on various (;,•cas., and to the Chairman.</p>
        <p>PADGATE. ClOrci The annual vestry meeting took place in the 611 House on Thursday evening. The Vicar (Refoct Dickinson, M.A.) cccupied the chair, and Mr well was appointed vestry clerk. The accounts past year were presented by the people's What 55, J Critcbley. The financial statement showed tbaeolt income for the year had been £137 5s id. The elcP4Jtv, ture had exceeded this amount by £ll 17,84501 a against a deficit at the beginning of the year of _t‘ The income for the Wcolston Mission had amours-9F £l2 ha 9d and the expenditure had totalled which leit the satisfactory credit balance of as compared with £l5 9s 9d last year. The Vicar re-appointed Mr H Houghton fredb; warden, while Mr J Critchley wis again re-clecedlo people's warden. The vicar's sidesmen apn_ei°„tuitabel, Messrs R Denham, P .3 Dutton, E Gorton u ' E W C London, P Royston and A 0 Hilditch•_, r peoples' sidesmen chosen are Messrs J Atkinsoaa Jump, W S Howard, P Croft, J Antrobus, 5e131,(1 Houghton and W Putter. Messrs It Ryder goo Stafford were appointed sidesmen for Woolston aaoto The auditors who were re-elected are Messrs P.P0.0 and F Gidman. Messrs G Mather and W Jo°`w appointed ruri-decanal representatives. e'fb,,•4 In a short speech the Vicar thanked all thesferP had !assisted in the work of the year, and with regret to the removal from the districti:r George Hatton. A vote of thanks to the Vicar P. 550, cmiduct of the business as chairman was P unanimously. •</p>
        <p>ORFORD. The fourth Easter vestry meeting for the P3r: Orford, was held in tha vestry of Sr Marg 013 All Hallo e, Orford, on Easter Tuesdal*„ Vicar, the Rev G Vale Owen, presided over attendance. The minutes of the last meetingsv read and approved. The treasurer, hir C brought forward his balance sheet, which gu,nice, deficit of £4 10s. Last year there was a ba".10 hand and in the batik of £4 18s. The offertotled church expenses amounted to £49 4.. 6d., file&amp; expenses to £5B 12s. 6d. Offertories had been to- 01 sick and general fund, £lO ss. 7d., Sunday 7s. 3c1., choir fund, 1:1 7s. 2i, special offertorill'a 18. 81., ont.ide charitable objects Er; 163. 7tl.,bi. 11.1 the total expenses for the year t 3 102 104. Brandreth proposed that the accounts be P'sse Spencer seconded, and it was carried. ; tOl3 The Vicar again appointed Mr 3 Sd his warden. Ile said they had always worked amicably together, and he hoped they would to do SG for many years to come. Mr Swinton and Mr Br.thdreth seconded that Mr C ea, re-elected people's warden. This wag unanimously. On the proposition of Mr seconded by Mr Brandretb, the six sidesrcgoßeir re-elected en bloc : Messrs II Williams, W Povey, L Cross, E Brownbill and R Monks. Messrs L Cross and W Houghton were apirip'';f decanal representatives, on the proposition , Povey, seconded by Mr J Swinton. ormoo In a short review of the past T Vicar said he would like to thank the 0 P.tbljE and churobworkers, not for their servingleasel was done for God, but for their personal to him. He said that on the Bishop of LiTe,tbyi recommendation, they had made up their al' ji they would pay off the church debt by Ellgteolire was now practically accomplished, and be ger, 61 as a plain instancs of the efficacy of PraYvrair. 1,00 number of communicints for the past year je compared with 1,300 for the previous year- eider:4 this as satisfactory. It was proposed 00,,,..'tabilitt that a resolution protesting against Welsh Dig"—eitbr ,h, ment and Disendownieut, be sent to Mr A5O ralt, McKenna, and Mr Boner Law. A vote of 551,0. was passed with Mr Hallwood in his deep sor</p>
        <p>)r.rdo ;'°°_,rl</p>
        <p>St. Mary's Cricket Club This club held its annual general meet'°g, Guild Room. Buttermarkeb-street, on Suncl3i. following officers were elected :—Presiden"optsil Father Wilson; chairman, Mr S J nice ; e retof Mr R Price; vice-captain, Mr E Moffattßeeps S and treasurer, Mr Jas Dolan' committee, -Writ Price, It Price, R Heneghan, J Duffy E J Dolan. -.see</p>
        <p>C" , I , iI cc &amp; KN , Ruis Bl,„# whil. tit \\.rs , .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Fl NANC A.</head>
        <p>Fl NANC A.</p>
        <p>Mr Eckersley submitted the accounts which showed that at the beginning of the year there was a deficit of £1 IN 9J, which had been wiped off, and the balance in hand was at the present time £1 as 7d. Ihe sum of £25 bad be,n expended on wood blocks and for that account £l2 93 had been subscribed. The upon was adopted and comments were made on the satisfactory state of affairs.</p>
        <p>ST. HILDA'S MISSION CHURCH. The two mission wardens elected for St. Hilda's were Mr Rathbone (vicar's warden),and Mr James Cartwright (people's warden). The two sidesmen were Messrs J Reynolds and A Livingstone. Mr Rathbone submitted the balance sheet which showed that there was a balance in hand of £3 153 at the beginning of the year and at the present time there was a credit balance of £1 45, The report was adopted ST. BARNABAV VESTRY. The Easter vestry meeting was held on Thursday evening the Vicar(Rev J HG Holliogworth) presiding.— The accounts were presented by Mr C Skinner (people's warden). They showed that the year began with a balance in hand of RI 14s Bd. The income amounted to £147 133 3d, the church collections being as follows :—For church expenses, 466 43 6d ; for special objects £4B 4s Bd. After devoting £29 to the curacy fund,..£s to the church painting and decoration fund and £5 to the school fund, there was a credit balane3 of £1 83 4d.—The Vicar nominated Mr J E Morgan as his warden. Mr C Skinner retired from th 3 office of people's warden and Mr W Boyle was elected to succeed him. A vote of thanks was passed to Mr skinner for his work as warden during the past four years, The following sidesmen were elected :—Messrs P Bennett, W E Cotterill, B Dimelow, T Evans, jun., %V Hind, TA Jones, F Kimberley, H V Mayhew, W ' Minards, T Oxley, W Turner and E Wakefield. A resolu- tion protesting against the proposed Disestablishment and Disendowment of the Church in Wales was adopted. Votes of thanks were passed to the officers for their past services. The Vicar,. in a short address on the work of the parish, said the number of communicants on Easter Day constituted a record. In spite of the shortage of work, the collec- tions during Lent had been higher than in previous years. During the coal strike acute distress had pre- vailed in the parish and he expressed cordial thanks to those who had helped him to relieve the sufferers especially mentioning the gifts of Mr. Peter Peacock' and Messrs Peter Walker and Sons Ltd (through Mr Hutton and Mr Mercer).</p>
        <p>ST. THOMAS'S, STOCKTON HEATII. The annual vestry meeting was held at noon on Tuesday. The Vicar (the Rev, James Collins) presided, and others present included Mr Henry Greenell (vicar's warden and vestry clerk) and Mr T J Lee (parishioners' warden). Mr Lee presented the accounts which showed that the year was commenced with a balance in hand of £7 18s 'id. The general offertory, including the harvest offertory, amounted to £230 17s Id. There wal a balance in hand of £8 183 Oi ; against £7 18s 7d for</p>
      </div>
