        <p>school had so increased its Ifter being twice drawn out at a telescope, it was still too -tanagers decided that it should and a new one erected. They nlinent architect, Mr. Clarke, n.eed to adopt the plan he had Plan .was laid before the who made various objections, as too good, and another that too wide and that a school 18 the best fitted for any school managers replied that there Public building in the village 8 desirable that the new build: over-ornamented, should be in .cive the village taste, and that not been found a difficulty in )1. As the Privy Council were 11° Dart of the expense their e not insisted upon, although °f eases they had insisted on ilug of one measure. le school, having by Mrs. th lost a friend, it was decided 8,,,,eh00l should at once be pro- ▪be contract was made with a wooden tabernacle erected u church in the playground ~.lackburne conveyed as a gift a little over a year the B,ned. It has been twice (thrice) `ja substantially what it was 10. Special sermons marked reachers:Archdeacon Emery, I:.tLY ; Canon Raines, vicar, ;u3r divines. Mr. Livingstone, rr,d, was selected by Mr. yelamere, representative of i_Cheshire families, to be his '4aPlain, and he had to preach la before Her Majesty's Judges Bitting in Court beside Mr. "11 a prisoner was on trial for thedeath of a neighbour's • °secuting counsel said would -1;•• er in a human subject, the r Is voice, whispered to the e Properly suicide." of being a High Sheriff's Chap- If3en fall to the lot of a curate. la, his being H.S. Chaplain ga"11 more directly under the Le"itcoP, who soon gave him the at?' the Weaver Churches at ,0 in 1868 he was presented by Aiti;e more valuable vicarage bur tile mastership of the school go* "vingstone advised George 0 11 of the village shoemaker at ( gh°. t° Chester College. George he It advice, obtained a Ist rfo,arve year became school- Where he remained 26 wife' corn s failing health co - resign rovvafter Mr. Livingstone, was who came recommended