      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>1111\■.-__</head>
        <p>1111\■.-__</p>
        <p>sand, flower patterns took the place of cindered roads and pavements. Mr. Owen who had never seen the like before thought Orford people were not indifferent to the happiness of their friends. Mr. Owen preached well; but his congre- gation had reason to complain that he was too often absent, and the pulpit was so often filled by one or other of the neighbouring clergy that the people began to think his absence would be laid at their door as if they and not he were the cause. A member of the congregation who had beard his frequent absences ascribed to his wife's health, asked him one Sunday morning how she was, and he replied very dolorously that she was far from well, that be hoped he might find her living at his return. His hearer condoled with him but very unnecessarily as it turned out; for, next day, as he was going down Bold-street he saw Mr. and Mrs. Owen walking down the opposite side both con- vulsed with laughter, so he concluded Mrs. Owen's fits of illness were feigned in order to keep her husband home. Before Mr. Owen had been curate many months he was made Vicar of St. Peter's, Oldham, the Parish Church of that town, and which had a large school of 1,500 Sunday scholars, and a numerous and influential congregation. Here he continued until his death on 28th June, 1883. _ _ _</p>
        <p>His successor was the Rev. Archibald Davy, M.A., who came to live at Orford. He I was the son of Dr. Davy a well-known man of science,. and nephew of Sir Humphrey Davy, inventor of the davy lamp. Mr. Davy was a thoughtful reader and rather retiring man. At the end of two years he gave up his curacy to travel, afterwards becoming domestic chaplain to Lord de Tabley, at I Tabby. His successor was the Rev. Arthur Wright M.A., late scholar of Clare College, Cambridge, and son of Rev. James Wright,l Latchford, who was anxious that his son who had a school at Birkenhead should employ his Sundays at Orford. Unlike Mr. Owen Mr. Wright never preached a written sermon but gave every Sunday two extempore ones in which with no repetition and an abundant low of language he preached the truth in love. He retired from the curacy because he could not well manage it with his school and was succeeded by the Rev. Henry Siddall, M.A. late scholar of Clare Hall, Cambridge, and second master of Boteler Free Grammar School. He spent his half holidays and Sundays in Orford work. In May 1869 he accepted the living of St. Ann's,Warrington. Next came the Rev. William Page Owen, M.A., who had been a Tutor or Fellow of Radley College, near Cuddeston, Oxfordshire, who like Mr. Siddall was also master at the Grammar School. He also lived at Orford, and his gentle and amiable manners made him many friends, and he did not neglect the temporal wants of his flock any more than the spiritual. It was to his taste that his flock were indebted in carrying out their wishes in the memorial—to be seen on the west wall of the school—to him (Mr. Beamont's son) who had taught the young in Orford School since he was seven years old, and who attained the honour of teaching men in Trinity, the largest college in the world, and who was called early to that eternal rest for which his whole life had been a preparation. Promotion came quickly to Mr. Oldham who in 1870 was made vicar of St. Thomas's Church, Ashton-in-Makerfield, which he only enjoyed a few months before he was called away by death. In April 1870 the Rev. Offley Henry Casy, M.A., of Christ's College, Oxford, having engaged Mr. Edward John Willcocks to be second master of Boteler Grammar School of which be had been some time headmaster, and Mr. Willcocks intending to apply for Holy Orders at the next ordination, Mr. easy offered to take the curacy until Mr. Willcocks should be ordained. Mr. Casy was an especial' I favourite with the choir. In September 1870 Mr. Willcocks, M.A., of ICatherine College, Cambridge, entered on the duties of his office at Orford and fulfilled them without any neglect or complaint and ihe was rarely if ever absent. He held the office until May 1878 when he retired to Warrington, continuing at the Grammar School as second master until he was appointed Headmaster. On Mr. Willcocks' retirement, some diffi- culty was found in filling the vacancy, until a friend at Southport recommended the Rev. John Ward, MA., of Queen's College, Cam- bridge, who had just given up the curacy of Trinity Church, Southport. Mr. Ward remained until May 1884, when he gave way to the Rev. J. Phillips, M.A., of Trinity College, Cambridge. Later history falls within the memory of your readers and need not be given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>TEACHERS AND DENOMINATIONALISM.</head>
        <p>TEACHERS AND DENOMINATIONALISM.</p>
        <p>The National Union of Teachers' Conference opened on Mcnday at Hull. The president, Mr. Dent made a strong speech against the denominational system. There should, he said, be DO dual system. Every public Primary school thould be under complete popular control Why was the present dull system tolerated? The answer he bad to give was humiliating. The reason was —To provide for the continued denominational religious instruction of children whose parents in the very vast majority of cases bad not asked for it, were not keen on it, and would view its discontinuance in the schools with indifference. He had bad a long experience in the admission of children to provided schools ; he had admitted thousands, but he had never heard a parent complain of having to enter children in such a school and he had never heard an expression of regret at their statutory inability to provide definite denominational religious instruction. The truth was that enthusiasts had built an inverted pyramid of fiction on a pin point of fact. "Let those in authority recognise that what the parents are anxious about," said Mr Bentliff, "is not denomi- natioral religious instruction but the retention of the Bible in the schools. lam voicing the opinion of the members of this great Union in saying here and now that the Bible must be kept in the schools. If that is done, if we are allowed to teach the truths therein con- tained, truths which are accepted by every denomina- tion which founds its creed on the Bible, then, speaking for myself and I hope for the very great majority of teachers in this country, I say that, so far as the day schools are concerned, denominational instruction may go." There had been several attempts to end the dual system, and in each an honest effort had been made to preserve the religious instruction. Let the sectarians responsible for the failure of those efforts beware lest in sheer despair Parliament removed the block they had set up by removing the Bible from the schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>PLUS AND MINOS.</head>
        <p>PLUS AND MINOS.</p>
        <p>That little word plus means so much. Add another little word to it and it means very much more. The other word is—Currant. Anything in the way of food—no matter what—plus Currants, becomes most valuable, because the Currant is that which provides the delicious flavour and the nutriment. Take, for instance, Currant Bread—it can never be dry bread, for the Currants keep it always moist, always savoury, always nutritious. Bread plus Currants becomes bread and pure grape sugar, it changes from the uninterest- ing to the highly attractive. It is always savoury and highly palatable. Therefore, see that your bread is always bread plus Currants What a gloomy side is presented by the minus aspect! "Minus Currants" is a truly awful thought! It would mean no Christmas pudding, no mirce ties, no Eccles cakcs, in fact none of the dishes which make life worth living can be made without Currants. Wise folk make a point of eating Currants every day. It ' does not matter how or when, so long as they eat I them, because Currants are so perfect a food and those who have learnt thcir value by experience will tot be I without the delicious little fruit. A day without Currants is worse than minus—it is a blank. See that all your days are of the plus varie:y— for then you will enjoy robust health, the greatest of all earthly blessings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WARRINGTON YACHT RACE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON YACHT RACE.</p>
        <p>A HOLIDAY DISASTER.</p>
        <p>TWO WARRINGTO</p>
        <p>A tragedy marred the Warrington Sailing Club's Easter boat race, uhich took place on the Mersey on Saturday afternoon. One of the contesting yachts capsized, and two of the occupants—Walter Warburton (40), range fitter, 7, Fairclough's-avenue and James Edward Crooks (38), wire worker, 41, Lord Nelson-strect, lost their lives.</p>
        <p>Six boats took part in the race' which was from Stockton's Yard, Widnes, to Bank Quay, Warrington, and the Zinnia, owned and captained by Mr. Warburton, was one of them. Mr. Warburton was accompanied in the boat by his two sons, Walter, aged fourteen and John llenry, aged sixteen, and Mr. Crooks. An exceptionally strong wind was blowing but all went well until two miles had been covered. V\ ben near Sullivan's Point the boom of the Zinnia smashed and the craft capsized, all the occupants being thrown into the water. . _ .</p>
        <p>A fishing vessel, the Red Rose, happened to be near and vi hen the seriousness of the position was realized,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MEN DROWNED.</head>
        <p>MEN DROWNED.</p>
        <p>went quickly to the rescue. Mr. Warburton and his two sous were got out of the water, apparently dead They were taken to the Ferry Inn, Penketh. where artificial respiration was applied but Mr. Warburton succumbed from exhaustion. It was at first reported that one of the sons had also been drowned, but happily both revived and are now little the worse for their immersion. Crooks was the only member of the party who was not wearing a life bet. Mr. Warburton and his sons made heroic efforts to keep him afloat tut when they were compelled to relax their hold he went down. A few hours later his bdy was washed to the side of the river by the ebb tide. Mr. Warburton had taken part in the Warrington boat races for many years, and was an experienced yachtsman. It is supposed that the boat capsized owing to Mr. Crooks clinging to the outer end of the b, om after it had broken. .</p>
        <p>The oarr boats finished the course in ignorance of the tragedy which had overtaken the Zinnia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>ILL-FATED ZINNIA, Photographed at her moorings, Warrington Bridge, on Wednesday. Inset, a snapshot of the Zinnia on the Mersey at Liverpool,</head>
        <p>THE ILL-FATED ZINNIA, Photographed at her moorings, Warrington Bridge, on Wednesday. Inset, a snapshot of the Zinnia on the Mersey at Liverpool,</p>
        <p>THE INQUEST. A grap,lio story of the tragedy was unfolded at the inquest which Vl as conducted at the Warrington Police Station on Tuesday morning by Mr S Brighouse,County C( caner. Ellen Warburton, widow of Walter Warburton,said she last saw deceased alive at 10 o'clock on Friday morning when he and his two sons left home to go for a sail in their boat to Widnes, to take part in the race the following day. At six o'clock on Saturday evening she was informed of the a:cident end went to Fiddler's Ferry to see her husband, but was not allowed to do so. Sarah Ann Crooks, widow of Jame] Edward Crooks, also gave evidence of identification. Henry Stacey, flit man, 36, St. Mary's-road Widnes, stated that at 3 o'clock on Saturday afternoon he heard that a yacht bad capsized in the Mersey opposite Brook's works, Widnes, and that a man had been drowned. Witness got assistance and went in search, with the result that at five o'clock he found the body of Crooks on a sand bank opposite Brock's works. He placed the body in a boat and conveyed it to Widnes. John Henry Warburton, 16 years of age, stated that i on Saturday his father was a competitor n the annual sailing match on the river Mersey between Widnes and Warrington. He was the captain; of the sailing boat Zinnia which was the scratch boat in the race. His crew consisted of witness, his brother Walter, aged I fourteen, and James Crooks. About three o'clock they got the order to start from Widnes and proceeded on the course to Bank Quay, all the other boats having gone at intervals of several minutes. At 3.15 when they had sailed shout two miles and were opposite the end of Sullivan's works, they were going well in a strong wind when the boom broke about 2 feet 6 inches from the mast. Crooks held the boom with his bleach and witness's father told him to let it go but he refused. His father told him a second time, remarking "My lads are here." A gut of wind then came and cap- sized the boat and they were all thrown into the water. With the exception of Crooks they were all wearing life-belts, and they assisted to keep Crooks afloat. Witness's father got his arms round Crooks's body. After having been about half an hour in the water, bath appeared exhausted and seemed to be getting WlCollseioll9. About that time the Red Rose came on the scene and the occupants threw witness a rope which he secured. He was dragged on board bud placed in the cabin. Soon afterwards his father was rescued and witness he .rd him talking and murmuring in the cock- pit. His brother Walter was swimming about the stern of the Red Rose when he last saw him. Witness was taken to the Ferry Inn, Penketh. The Coroner : How was it that Crooks had not a life-belt on? Witness: We did not know be was going with us. The Coroner : Have you sailed in this boat before? Witness : Yes, sir. Several times. Further questioned witness said the Zinnia was an 18ft. racing boat. A fair wind was blowing at the time of the accident. The Coroner: Did Crooks know anything about sailing ? Witness : Oh, yes. He had been down a time or two. In his answers to the Coroner, witness displayed a</p>
        <p>good knowledge of the structure and management of tho yael.t. Henry Warburton, brother of the deceased and commodore of the Sailing Club, said the deceased had had the Zinnia atout 18 months. Sae was a half-decked boat If when V-e boont had smashed, the main sail bad been allowed to swing out, it would have caught the wiud and the vessel would have righted itself.lnatead of that Crooks bad apparently held the boom, with the result that a gust of wind hurled it against the top mast and the boat ,apsized. A chemical labourer named Sutton, 08 James- street, Widnes, said he was at the Ferry Inn, Penketb, when Dr. Murray was sent for and pronounced Walter Waiburton to be dead. he Coroner, addressing the jury, said he used to sail that class of boat when he was younger, and therefore he knew something about it, and could quite understand how the accident happened. He had no right to dictate to the jury, but from the experience he gained when be was sailing—and he was sailing for a good many years—it was quite a simple matter. If Crooks, who was not very well versed in seamanship, had not got hold of the boom and stuck to it, the-sail would have swung out, Warburton would have put down the tiller and every- thing would have been right. It was, however, some- thing that anybody might do on the spur of the moment, even if be were an experienced man. If the deceased Warburton was as competent as his brother who had given evidence, he knew something about sailing. A verdict of 'Accidental death" was returned.</p>
        <p>MOTOR CAR ACCIDENT AT CADISHEAD. An alarming motor car accident, attended by some fortunate escapes, took place at Cadishead on Monday. At about one o'clock in the afternoon a motor car belonging to the Provincial Motpr Cab Co., Ltd., of Liverpool, vas travelling alcng Liverpool-road on the way to Manchester, NS hen the driver, George Shallot, 13, Tiber-street, Sefton Park, saw a girl crossing the road. Owing to the strong and gusty wind the girl did not hear the horn, and in order to avoid her the driver turned the car into the hedge, knocking down a large poplar tree and demolishing the gate and stumps. The car was overturned and the occupants, seven in number, bad narrow escapes. The girl, Florenoe Ellen Kell, who lives with her uncle, Mr J H Oatenby, of 245, Liverpool-road was knocked down, hut not dangerously injured, thanks to the presence of mind of the driver, in promptly swerving. Of the occupants, Mr 13 Allanson, of 21, Herrich-street, Stanley, suffers from a broken rib and Mr H Wolfhall has a fractured knee. The driver received a severe sprain in the left leg. The car was badly damaged.</p>
        <p>Three seamen, members of a party of six who were in a boat, were drowned at Queenborough on Saturday night. The boat upset on the river Swale, and the three men sank before assistance arrived. Two coloured seamen were rescued and a third saved himself by swimming to a barge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>GREAT INSURANCE ACT.</head>
        <p>THE GREAT INSURANCE ACT.</p>
        <p>MATERNITY BENEFIT AND SANATORIUM BENEFIT.</p>
        <p>BY DR. C. ADDISON, M.P.</p>
        <p>MATERNITY BENEFIT.</p>
        <p>What is the maternity benefit? It means, first, that the wife of every man insured under the Act will receive benefit to the value of 34., to be distributed by the society of which the man is a member, on the birth of a child. This benefit may take the form either of nursing, medical attendance, food, necessaries, or money, according as the neces- sities of the case may require and as the society may arrange. The maternity benefit also. means that where the wife is herself a worker and insured under the Act she will receive in addition to the 30s. benefit coming through her husband a sum of 7s. 6d. a week for four weeks or 30s. sick-pay. That is to say, where the husband only is insured the benefit is value 305.; where the wife also is insured it is worth L'3. he first week that the benefits of the Act come into operation 18,000 women will receive this benefit, and so on for every week of the year, this being in round numbers about the average number of births per week taking place amongst those who are insured under the Act. If, as will probably prove to be the case, this benefit begins on January 15th, 1913, it will mean that on the 16th some 2,600 women will receive this assistance, and so on for every day. Whatever the opponents of the Act may tell people at street corners, we may be fairly certain that 2,600 house- holds per day will be grateful to the Chan- cellor of the Exchequer for the Insurance Act. What is the object of this benefit? In order to answer this question it is necessary to study the statistics of infant mortality. In them we find that during the first six weeks of infant life the death rate and the waste is greater almost than at any other period. There are, of course, a great many ,causes contributing to this result—in the nourishment of the child, improper feeding, lack of clean- liness, infection of different kinds, evil home surroundinge, ignorance and many more. The disastrous results of these conditions, as we should expect, are more effective in those dis- tricts where the mothers are employed and have to leave their infants to the care of others. Nearly every reader will know of a case. The mother io a charwoman or an office cleaner, or works in a factory. She goes on with her employment until the last moment, and, with the extra home expenses, she has to go back to work as soon as she can after her confinement so as to get the few shillings that she earns at her work. Often enough she goes back to her work far too soon, and per- manently ruins her own health. At the same time she has to leave her child to the care of older children, or to be looked after by a " minder " or by friendly neighbours. The child has to go without proper motherly care and feeding. Often enough it is fed on im- properly prepared and unsuitable artificial foods, given with all geod intentions but in ignorance, whereas what it needs only is the care and sustenance of its mother. It is within the truth to say that we liese in this country every year, from impropet feeding, at least 50,000 children under the age of twelve months. But the mischief does not cease with those that die, for multitudes of them which survive are weakened in their bodies. Under the Insurance Act such a woman worker as we have been thinking of will not only receive a benefit of 30e. to secure for her proper attendance and nourishment in the time of her need, but she will also be secure in 7s. 6d. a week sick-pay for four weeks, so as to enable her to stay at home to look after her infant as she would wish to do, to get her strength back again, and to feed the child properly, with the opportunity of caring for it, herself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>SANATORIUM BENEFIT.</head>
        <p>SANATORIUM BENEFIT.</p>
        <p>The sanatorium benefit means that every person who is suffering from tuberculosis may be recommended by the Insurance Committee for special treatment. But the Act further pro. vides that the dependants, the wives and chil- dren, of insured persons may also come within the scope of this benefit. There is, as a matter of fact, little doubt but that the whole scheme of sanatorium benefit will be extended to dependants at the same time as to insured persons, and, indeed, it would be wasteful and short-sighted were it otherwise. The sum of about £882,000 a-year is pro- vided under the Act for the treatment of in- sured persons (or their dependants) for tuber- culosis, and under the Budget of 1911 the sum of £.1,500,000 is available for the provision of dispensaries, 6anatoria, shelters, country homes, or any other form of special institu- tions which may be considered to be desirable or necessary for the treatment of .persons suffering either from consumption or any other form of tuberculosis. If the cost exceeds the money provided in the Insur- ance Act, half the extra cost will be shared with the local authorities by the Im- perial Exchequer. We may take it fairly well for granted that the Insurance Act means the beginning of a national scheme for the treatment of a disease which causes more poverty and distress than any other. It should also be borne in mind that the number of those who die from consumption is speci- ally great between the ages of twenty-five and fifty; that is to say, in those years particu- larly when the man is the breadwinner and the woman the mainstay of the home. At the present time a man of fifty with this disease, knowing what is in store for his family, goes on working until he can work no more. If he has a club, he draws his sick- pay to the utmost penny. Mostly, however, he has no club, and his wife and children have to keep the home together as best they can. The feeding of the children (that is to say, their resistance to the disease), and the kind of home their diminished income can provide get worse and worse, and the suffer- ing of the patient in multitudes of cases is attended with the steady depreciation of the well-being of his children and the spread of the disease to other members of his house. hold. Early detection, early treatmE.M4, pro. per feeding, exercise, and good surroundings amongst those affected are vital to the success of any properly- directed attempt to stamp out this disease. At the present time some 40,000 persons die from consumption in this country every year, and 10,000 to 15,000 more of other forms of tuberculosis. People dying with consumption form a large proportion of those occupying our workhouse infirmary beds, at a cost to the ratepayers of about 30s. a week each. rive shillings out of every of the sick-pay of most friendly societies goes to those who have fallen victims to what has been justly described as the "White Man's Scourge." For that army of cases in the ad- vanced and incurable stages that will exist on the commencement of the Insurance Act little can be done except to make their last days easier and of greater comfort, and to prevent in some measure the spread of the infection from them to other members of their family. One of the first steps must be to make provision available throughout the whole country, so far as medical knowledge and skill can do it, whereby the disease can be discovered at the earliest possible moment, and whereby not only the workers but their wives and children may be guarded, as far as possible, from its inroads in the early stages —those stages in which material benefit may be obtained, and by which not only the in- sured worker may expect to prolong his work- ing years and often to obtain the mastery of the disease.</p>
      </div>
