      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>SOME HOWLERS.</head>
        <p>SOME HOWLERS.</p>
        <p>- . When schoolboys or girls make funny mistakes older people call them "howlers." I think many bowlers are invented,but some very good ones are found from time to time in examination papers. I am going to give you a few to laugh over. The Young Pretender was so called because it was pretended that he was born in a frying-pan. _</p>
        <p>-Grace- Darling was a light housekeeper's daughter. Lord Rayleigh was the first man to see the Invisible Armada.</p>
        <p>The South of the U.S.A. grows oranges, figs, melons, and a great quantity of preserved fruits, especially tinned meats. Milton married a young girl who ran back to her parents, so he wrote a sonnet on divorce.</p>
        <p>The saddest thing King John did was to lose his crown in the laundry. The father of Henry IV.was John O'Groats. The American War was started because the people would persist in sending their parcels through the post without stamps. Prince William was drowned in a butt of Malmsey wine; he never laughed again. Richard 11. is said to have been murdered by some historians ; his real fate is uncertain. There are five continents, a, e, i, o, and u. The tides are caused by the sun drawing the water out and the moon drawing it in again. _ _</p>
        <p>-A circle is a line which meets its other end without ending. _</p>
        <p>A man has ;miles to travel; he goes a miles by train, b miles by boat, and c miles he walks ; the rest he cycles. How far does he cycIe?—DEFGHIJKLMNOPQR S T_U V W miles.</p>
        <p>If the air contains more than 100 per cent. of carbonic acid it is very injurious to health. Algebraical symbols are used when you don't know what you are talking about. An angle is a triangle with only two sides. A circle is a straight lino drawn as round as possible with a dot in the middle. The mechanical advantage of a long pump handle is that you can have someone to help you pump. St. Andrew is the patent saint of Scotland The patent saint of England is Union Jack. An old soldier is called a vegetarian. A centipede is a French measure of length. The Home Office is where Home Rule is made.</p>
        <p>A bishop without a diocese is called a suffragette.</p>
        <p>In the houses of the poor the drains are in a fearful state, and quite unfit for human habitation.</p>
        <p>LITTLE THINGS. A crumb will feed a tiny bird, A thought prevent an angry word, A feed brings forth full many a flower, A drop of rain foretells a shower. A straw the wild wind's course reveals, A kind weld oft an old grudge heals, A beacon-light saves many a life, A slight rill often kindle a strife. A humble band may alms bestow, A godly life make heaven below, A little child conkunds the wise, A lark sings nearest to the skies. A sunbeam makes the earth rejoice, A flower will teach, without a voice, A brave man's arm will right the wrong A simple prayer will make us strong. THIS WEEK'S STORY.</p>
        <p>" What this word F ather ?" asked Denis. "Phenomenon," said father. "And what's a phenomenon ?" "That, sonny, is what you would be if you never bothered your father with questions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>ALWAYS WELCOME HERE.</head>
        <p>ALWAYS WELCOME HERE.</p>
        <p>What could ho more welcome here than open-hearted statements like the following ? Week after week local men and women speak out in this earnest way,and their personal experiences ring with encouragement. Mrs. It. Whelan, of 8, Witt-road,Widnes, rays : " For nearly five weeks I suffered with cruel pains In my back. The shooting-pains under the shoulder-blades were so severe, that at times I was almost afraid to draw my breath in a propur manner. Day and night I had these pains,and I did not vern able to relieve them in any way. I could not lay on my left side. In the day time I felt tired and languid and out of sorts, as WAS only natural I suppose, seeing all the rain I was obliged to endure. There was a thick sandy sediment in the kidney excretions, and I also suffered at times from palpitation of the heart. At last I heard of Doan's backache kidney pills, and made up my mind to give them a thorough trial. I had only taken a few doses of the pills before I found relief in my back. This encouraged me, and I kept on with the medicine. until I was completely cured. "I have a high opinion of Doan's pills, and I feel very grateful for all that they have done for me. (Signed) (Mrs.) 11. WHELAN." 2e. J. a box, six boxes 13s. 93. Of all dealers. Ask for Doan's backache kidney pills, like Mrs. Whelan had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>CANADA'S IMMIGRATION PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>CANADA'S IMMIGRATION PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>The immigration problem of Canada, in the opinion of the "Tcronto News," is one of the most momentous I that has ever confronted a nation which desires to preserve the high ideal of citizenship and perpetuato the best traditions of the race /rom which it has sprung. The problem in Canada is two-fold, that of receiving, distributing and absorbing the volume of the immigra- tion which annually seeks its shores and that of assimilating and moulding to the national standard the large foreign element among the newcomers. In tea years Canada's population of five and a third millions has bad to cope with en emigration of nearly two millions, more than half of whom were aliens. The problem a assimilation is best appreciated by the analysis of the emigration for 1911 which was 350,374, comprised of British, 144,086 ; United States, 131,340 ; Continental European, 63,376; and miszellanoous nationalities, 11.,582. The magnitude of the task in such circumstances is aßparest. Itudyurd Kipling's advice to " pump in the Britisher " is one of the obvious necessities. The British element in Canada must be so strengthened as to be enabled to assimilate instead of being fused with the alien races pressing into her country. Undoubtedly the first step in this process is to Canadianize the newcomer befare he can can be expected to appreciate and absorb tells idea of his wider imperial citizenship. Experience shows that American settlers quickly submit to Canadian influences from the outset. They become n ituralisei and associate themselves readily with the British element in moulding into the best typo of Canadian citizenship the less tractable material represented by the Continent il Euri-psan and other foreign races. The American settler, therefore, on the whole, is from a national point of view distinctly a consolidating and not a disruptive or disintegrating force, and therefore of real value in Canada's growing period.</p>
      </div>
