      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 13, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 13, 1912.</p>
        <p>OUR 56iwAi'l576.kV.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.] :</p>
        <p>A BID FOR A BRIDE</p>
        <p>BY BLANCHE EARDLEY, Author of "Kitty Bell—Actress," "The Lady Killer," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XX. tIAMOND CUT DIAMOND.</p>
        <p>A few minutes later Clifford Hawke was in the library, where the visitor had been shown. As he entered the room the man standing by the window could not help ad- miring his tall, fine figure and deep, inscru- tablo eyes. There was about him that inde- finable "something" that spells personality and magnetism. He came forward slowly, the card in his hand. "You wish to see me on business?" he said politely. "Is it important?" Mr. Scafe hesitated slightly. "Well, sir," he said slowly, "these things always are important to the police. It is about the body of a man who was found burnt some tithe ago in that fire in Pimlico. It was sup- posed to have been the body of one of your clerks, a_man called •Paul Steinway."</p>
        <p>Clifford Hawke nodded. "Yes. He was in my employ some little time. What about it?" "That it has since been found out that more than the two men lodged in that house, and only one was burnt," the detective replied, "a man in the front room. The other went away and never returned to see the house where he had stayed; but it appears that the man who was burnt was a new lodger, who appeared to have come for a night only." Clifford llawke's eyes narrowed with an ugly thought. Paul Steinway not dead! It must be a mistake—a foolish idea of the police. Of course Steinway was dead, or he would not have dropped so easily out of his life, or have relinquished his idea of ven- geance against the man he hated. He looked up and found the detective watching" him with an intensity that the occasion did not seem to demand.</p>
        <p>"I have heard nothing of this," he said slowly. "If I do have any communication from Steinway I will let you know. Do you want him particularly?" he added, with a grim smile touching his lips. "No, sir; only I called because he was in your employ before the fire. I thought he might have had some reason for disappear- ing, and that you might have known it. Of course," he went on carelessly, "people disap- pear for strange reasons nowadays, and are only discovered by a fluke. I heard of a man the other day who left his wife nearly twenty years ago after trying to drawn her in a little fiAing village, and an idle conversation has almost disclosed his identity." Clifford Hawke smiled.</p>
        <p>"Ah ! How interesting! I always envy you chaps your work," he went on, "it is full of movement and sudeitn surprises that keep it always fresh." "Yes," the detective smlled drily, "it is very interesting at times, but there are occa- sions when even we are nonplussed hi some cases. For instance, there is this assault upon that yonng artist, Mr. Jasper Tennant. Though we've tried every way we know to get a clue we can't, except that once he remem- bered being followed by a Chinaman. Now a case like that makes one wild."</p>
        <p>Clifford Hawke turned to a table and took up a box of cigars. "Try one of these. They are a special brand." The detective helped himself. During their brief conversation he had been obliged to admit that the financier was too cool a custo- mer to be surprised into a self-betrayal, and his admiration for his opponent rose. The suspicion he had had since his talk with the old fisherman, and then Juspar Tennant's ex- clamation about the scar on the wrist of the man he hated, had not gained a point sines his interview. True, he had glanced at the sleeve links on the financier's sleeves, and they were mother-of-pearl and gold. Only the ugly scar on his wrist gave the slightest colour to his new theory; and yet he knew that because a man had a scar on his wrist it did not follow that he could be the man he wanted. He would have to go very carefully where the big financier was concerned, for a slip would be fatal—if it were a slip. "I suppose you've been a big traveller?" he said, as Clifford Hawke chatted pleasantly on commonplace topics. "Yes, I've done my share of knocking about," he laughed. 'Most countries know me pretty well; but a time comes when one gets tired of other places. and wants the old country—a kind of nostalgia creeps over one." Mr. Scafe nodded. "Yes, that's always the way. Well," he added, "I won't keep you any longer, Mr. Hawke, I have a lot of work to do. I got a clue yesterday as to the Chinaman who fol- lowed Mr. Tennant." In spite a his iron will the muscles round Clifford Hawke's mouth quivered slightly. "That's good news," he said. "I read of the affair in the paper the other day, and though Mr. Tennant's hardly a friend of mine, I sympathised most sincerely with him; so did my wife," he added, as the door opened suddenly, and Stella came into the room. She started at the sight of the detec- tive, and glanced apologetically at her hus- band. "I am sorry. I thought you were alone," she said in a low voice. "'Am I interrupt- ing you?" "No," he answered slowly, a curious light flashing into his eyes as they gazed at her pale, proud face. "This is Mr. Scafe, a de- tective from Scotland Yard. He was telling me about his search for the man who nearly lost 'Pennant his life."</p>
        <p>A wave of colour swept the girl's face, and she turned to Mr. Scafe with a look that told him what he wanted to know. _</p>
        <p>"I hope you will find the man," she said quickly, "it was a dastardly trick; no punishment is had enough for it!" Then she smiled as she saw the expression on the detective's face. "I ought not to be expressing myself so plainly, but Mr. Ten- nant is a great friend of mine, and I was very upset when this happened!" A few seconds later Mr. Scafe was walking towards Hyde Park-corner. His face was very thoughtful and grave, and once or twice he smiled grimly. "That is the cleverest man in the world," he muttered. "I have no evidence, not a gesture, nor a word, and though be has a :slight scar on his wrist and seems to hate Mr. I Tennant, that in nothing to go on. I must I try some other means of finding out what puzzles me. But that that man has a secret in his life I know, whether it concerns my job or not. I have as yet only the slightest thread, but one day I'll get a little nearer the truth; in the meantime it won't be a bad idea to have Mr. Clifford Hawke watched, he will be worth it."</p>
        <p>As he looked at her cold, white face and read the scorn in her blue eyes a sudden rage sprang up in his heart. He saw that for once he had met his match in the pliable girl he had married by a trick, and for a moment he felt a great longing to crush that spirit as he had crushed another woman's in the past. "1 don't wish you to stay with Lady Mary !" he said harshly, "she probably has that chap Tennant staying with her, and I don't intend my wife to meet a man who haa made love to her in the past! Do you Under- stand?" She flung back her head with a scornful little laugh. "I understand that your reason is an in- sult, and one that I will not degrade my womanhood by obeying." " Tnen you defy sue! " he said slowly, watching with angry sullen eyes the face of the only woman he had ever loved, and whom his own conduct was only driving further from him. "I refuse to obey you when your objections to my seeing my oldest friend are couched in such terms !" Stella said coldly. "I will certainly go to stay a few days with Lady Mary." " And probably see that posing artist chap?" he sneered savagely. A vivid flush stained her sweet face, but</p>
        <p>scith the soft voice know.?</p>
        <p>"I am quite well, thank you," he saki curtly. "You may go. 1 suppose you know what to do till—till Mrs. Hawke sends for your' She nodded.</p>
        <p>"Yes, thank you. But are you sure I can do nothing for you? You do look ill, really." He smiled grimly. It was a unique ex- perience for him to be pitied, and by a street beggar whom his wife and that "artist chap" had picked up. He looked at her more elovely. He had always felt a curious anti- pathy for her—she reminded him of a frag- ment in his past that he wae always trying to forget, but never succeeded in doing so. Sud- denly he caught his breath sharply and stepped forward. Round the thin girlish neck was an old gold locket, battered and shapeless, with two initials intertwined on it.</p>
        <p>"That locket looks old!" he said slowly; "where did you get it from?" Lottie raised her hand and touched it ten- derly. "It was my mother's," she said gently. "She put it round my neck when she was dy- ing, and—" But she paused suddenly. The great fin ancier had given a curious little cry, and stumbling forward had fallen in a dead faint at her feet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>CHAPTER XXI. AN OLD LOCKET.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXI. AN OLD LOCKET.</p>
        <p>When they were alone again Stella turned to her husband, lie haa not spoken since that one speech that had sent the blood to her cheeks in tell-tale confusion. "Clifford," she said quietly, "I have had a note from Lady Mary asking me to go and stay with her for a few days; do you mind if I go?" 'Mind!" he said with a savage laugh, "what use would it be if I did? Have you an' regard for my wishes or ray opinion, that you should ask that question! You keep a dog that I object to, and also a beggar girl because, I suppose, they are 'both links with that artist chap, Jaspar Tennant, who made love to you behind my back." "You have no right to speak like that," Stella replied proudly. "I don't recognise your authority in things that concern only myself. / am your wife, and so far as I can I obey you, but I don't allow anyone to die. tate my movements to me, especially if they are innocent domestic details that do uot in- terest YOU .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>He rose, and went to the telephone, then paused, and replaced the receiver. "No, I will call. Safer. Li Yen is sure to be in his hovel later on. In the meantime I will--"</p>
        <p>The door opened suddenly, and Lottie's slim figure stood on the threshold. She cast a nervous glance at him and hesitated. "Well, what is it?" he said curtly. She held out her hand, and he saw a letter in it.</p>
        <p>"Mrs. Hawke asked me to bring you this," she said quietly. "She has gone out." He snatched the letter from her and tore it open. It was brief and to the point..</p>
        <p>"I am going now to stay with Lady Mary. It will be a prolonged visit, and unless you like no one need know that it is anything more. Later on, when my plans are definite, I will send for 'Lottie, and you shall be rid of everything personally connected with me. Till then, may I ask you to give her shelter? She will not get in your way, and I have at present nowhere to take her.—STELLA." That was all, but as he read it Clifford Hawke bit his lip till the blood came. She had gone—left him—and they had been mar- ried only a couple of months. He, had be- trayed himself, and this was the result of his own folly. He glanced up and caught Lottie's grave wistful glance fixed upon him. "What-' are you staring at?" he said harsh- ly. "Are those the manners my wife taught you?" The girl flushed hotly. "I am sorry," she said quietly, 'but yoa looked ill, Mr. Hawke, and I thought I might be able to bring you something. He looked at her more closely. What did • 4.3,, nari What did this rneak-Avpd ciri</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>WOMEN UNDER CANVAS.</head>
        <p>WOMEN UNDER CANVAS.</p>
        <p>A corps of women, camping side by side wit'i men and performing all the duties of military camp-life, was one of the novel features of the Territorial Easter training. The thirty women who took part in the camp were members of the Women's Sick</p>
        <p>and Wounded Convoy Corps. Their work embraced improvising stretchers, carrying them over hedger and ditches, attending to the injured, signalling, fetching, and striking their own tents, and keeping a night watch. Our picture shows the Corp on the march.</p>
