      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>ber eyes met nts so traniuy unu mearti his own fell before them. . _</p>
        <p>"If I meet Mr. Tennant I shall certainly speak to him," she said quietly, "he is a friend of mine."</p>
        <p>"You mean he is your lover—that he has been in love with you all the time, and'you with him," he cried savagely, his self-control slipping from him for the moment as the smouldering hate bubbled up against the Man who had won the love he had failed to buy. "I knew it all along, and I made up my mind to keep you from him, and I did! I married you white he—" He paused suddenly, his heat freezing back into ice beneath the accus- ing glance in her eyes. "While he was kept away from me through your vile agency!" Stella cried in a tone of horror. "Oh, I know now that what I had dreaded and feared was a terrible truth! That you—the man I had married—could ac- tually stoop to try and kill another man who had done you no wrong!" He shrank from the loathing that he read in her eyes. "All's fair in love and war! " lie said sul- lenly. "Besides, I have not admitted that I ant responsible for Tennant's accident! You made up that on your own initiative." "You can't deny it! " she said in a low voice. "You know that I am right, and oh! how I hate you for it! How I regret that I ever let myself believe that he could be silent all that time when he knew that I was wait- ing for a signal that would change my whole life! " She paused, and the lace on her bosom rose and fell beneath her hurried breathing. - For a moment they gazed at each other, reading the secrets that each had concealed from the other for so long. Then Sella said slowly, "I will go -now. There is nothing more to be said. After what has passed between us we can only meet: as strangers now. Because I am still your wife I cannot betray my knowledge of what you are, but I cannot live with you again—that would be impossible." His face went pale with rage. "Then you forget that I can insist upon your returning to me? " "You can, I suppose," she replied Coldly, "but if you do I shall be compelled to give my reason for leaving you, and you know bent whether you would like the rest of the world to be taken into my confidence." The next moment she had turned away, and as the door closed softly behind her slim, graceful figure, Clifford Hawke, financier and cold, unscrupulous man of the world, realised that for once in his life of varied experiences he had been beaten by a woman, and that woman a slim slip of a girl whom he had won by a coward's trick. He sat down at his desk and gazed vacantly before him for a moment. His keen rapier- like brain had lost its grip of financial matters for the time being, and he pushed aside im- patiently a letter that his secretary had laid on the desk for him to read.</p>
        <p>"Confound these people:7. he muttered, "I can't attend to business now." Then he glanced again at the letter, and the frown on his face deepened. "So that chap Smith i,, making trouble for me, is he? " he muttered. "Since he came into the market with his mysterious buying of shares he has shown an unusual activity in my direction."</p>
        <p>He pushed back his chair and sat with his bead in his hand, the letter staring up at him like a menace. Since he had married all had one wrong with him. The woman he loved had drifted further and further from him. He had married her knowing she did not love him, but he had hoped that she might do so in time. Now that hope had fallen through; they would never be anything else but stran- gers! Then for some time past this mysterious man Smith had been shadowing his career. Everything he had touched lately had gone down: and for the first time in his life he had almost lost his nerve.</p>
        <p>"Who is this man?" he muttered, "every- one I have asked seems to know nothing about him. I must find him; I like to get my enemies into the open, and then I'll let Mr. Smith have a taste of what I can do." Then his mind switched off to another idea. The visit of the detective had in no way thrown dust in his eyes. "That chap had a motive for calling," he said with a grim smile. "He did it very cleverly, but I saw through it. He suspects me of something, but I wonder what tack he is on ; there are several he approached. but I don't know how much was suspicion and how much bluff Surely Paul Steinway MUEt be aprul !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>CHAPTER XXII. AN UNEXPECTED MEETING.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXII. AN UNEXPECTED MEETING.</p>
        <p>"Mr. Smith" or Paul Steinway—put down the cigarette he was smoking, and gave a laugh of triumph. "There, ..I think I have Mr. Clifford Hawke</p>
        <p>pretty well fixed up," he muttered; "in a very short time now I shall have my revenge! My enemy, who would gladly have seen me burnt to death, and probably knows more about the affair than anyone else, will come to the end of his little run, and `Mr. Smith ' will have accomplished his object in life." He rose and looked out of the window. He had taken a flat in the Albany—Clifford Hawke's old place—and smiled grimly as he thought how be had prospered since that day when he had first called on the rising man of finance. He had passed under many bridges since then, and now held the helm in his own hand. Presently he took a letter from his pocket, and read it for the third time that morning. "Dear Mr. Smith," it began. "Just a line to tell you that my cousin's husband is now alone. His wife has left him—though the affair is not supposed to be known--so the doubts you had about spoiling her life with him need not be considered now. I advise you to act at once. He is a desperate man, and, given time, will find a way out of every- thing.—Yours, I. F." He read the letter carefully, and then folded it and put it away. "So she advises my acting at once," lie mused. "I wonder why Miss. Frant is so eager to have me do this! Is it disappointed love for the man or hate of her cousin? She is a strange character, quite unlike that of little Dottie Bond. I feel somehow that that little girl would not like me to work out a plan of vengeance against anyone. She is as sweet and gentle as the other fine lady is hard and bitter." He began to fill his pipe, and presently was making notes in a small pocket-book. So in- tent was he on this that when he had finished he was startled to find that it was almost time to keep an appointment in the City. He glanced at himself in the mirror over the mantelpiece. No one would take him for the thin, half-starved man who a few</p>
        <p>- months ago had called on the financier Clif- ford Hawke to try and obtain a billet in his office. Ho had grown a beard and moustache, and his figure had filled out with good living. "No one would know me," he muttered,. "I shall escape recognition, except when I meet Clifford Hawke." A few moments later he was walking swiftly down Piccadilly, and, as he crossed the road by Regent-street, he collided sud- denly with a man, who turned and stared at him. "I beg your pardon," he said hastily; then he gave a cry, and clutched the other man's shoulder. "Cardew !" he said hoarsely. "Then you were not burnt, after all The face of the man whom he called "Car- dew" flushed with surprised joy. "Good Heavens! it's old Steinway ! Why, man, the papers said you were burnt in the fire—and you thought I was, What a comedy of errors :"</p>
        <p>- uome to the Criterion and have a drink," Steinway said, "and tell me what you've been doing. If you only knew the hours I've spent regretting that I sent you back to sleep at that place !" When they were served with their drinks, Steinway said again— " Now fire away. How have you been ex- isting since I last saw you?" Cardew drew a deep breath, and raised his glass to his old friend. "By raking up the past, and finding out the truth about Clifford Hawke," he said slowly. Steinway leant forward. "What is it?"</p>
        <p>"That I hold in my possession the key to the mystery concerning his past in Austra- lia," Cardew answered, "the mystery that helped him to become a rich man when we were poor ones."</p>
        <p>"And the mystery . was 7" Cardow drained his glass, and pushed it from him.</p>
        <p>"Murder !" lie said quietly. (To be Continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Showing Ycur Accomplishments.—America usually leads the war in the discovery ef new secial diversions, but Lady Darnley has provided an exception to the rule by her idea of afternoon teas for the encourage- ment of talents and In.bbies. You invite your friends for the afternoon, it being understood that they will give Ps far as possible a practical exhibit of any art or handiwork in which they may excel. It may be sing- ing, instrumental music, artistic metal work, artiflcial flower-n aking,art with the brush or pencil ,hat trimming, or in the innumeral-le directions in which cleverness with the needle nay be di.played. It effords an oppor- tunity for showing what you can do, but it is useful as well as intereBt:o.7, in enabling you t 3 measure your talents against those of other people. I rely Darnley has some idea of forming a Talents ". Club, but the idea might well be taken i p by the private hostess wah a view to enccureging young people to turn their talen's to accomt in any way that may he ireful to therm Ives, and for helping deserving ol jects. Walls may have Ears.—M. a scientific in,entions can be put to evil me, and the dictogrnph is no exception to the rule. This is a little instrument which may be bidden away in some odd corner of a room, but which Is capable of focusing all the sound waves so that they may he heard in the next room, or along a wire for any distance. Fa es-droppers have, in this I .test device a medium which would have made the heart of Paul Pry oveifl)w with joy, and it is not surprising to find that it has teen trade u:e of by servants, in order to overhear c nversations not intended for their ears. There will be an end to all privacy if the dictograph becomes a thing of everyday use, and one shuchlers et the thought of this mechanical lengthen'ns of eaves-d opp'ng. The only security against it would be the deaf and dumb alphabet when we Lai anything to say we should Lot care for any but the intended hearer to beceme acquainted with. Present Day Shonpirg.—The modern shopkeeper al hough compelled to go in for I eboratl disp'ays, and working exhibits, does not regard it to his advrntage to worry his customers to buy, and except et sale times he is not anxious to sell people things they do not want. Possibly this reform is largely due to the more tris'nesslike shopping habits of the up-to-date woman. Formerly a customer was appreciated not so much for whit she came to buy, as for what she could be persuaded to purchase once she got into the shop.lhcse methods have no N .been very largely abandoned, and it is a reform which adds very much to the °unfelt and convenience of shopping expeditions. At the same time, it is recognised ti a- it does not pvy to go too far to the ether extreme and it is proposed to s art a college in London for the special training of shop girl assistants. ShepLeepra, espectilly in the drapery trade are finding it increasingly difficult to obtain properly-trained assistants, and the prosperity of an estabPshecent depends nr,t a little tn. on them being cspable, well-mannered and obliging even the ugh it is not desired to make a fine rit of selling goods which the customers at That had no intention of buying. The Battle of the Tube and Panier Mode s —Gradu- ally the fashions of late years have reduced the figures of mes t well-dressed women to an attractive slender- ness, they are lab to abandon. The realer dress, which the I ading houses of London and Paris hive decided is again to come into its own, requires, if it is to he seen at its best, a fulness of figure, which would charge all this, but the slim outlive lois found el much favour with wemen of all types, that there is s nuething like a revc It against the tyranny of inin-n ado fashion-I. The campaign was directed in the first instance so as to gradually lead the slender figure in the direction of full( r s irts, frills, reed panier effects, but not a few Society women have flatly declined to adopt thernselvt $ to the small waist, and natural accomprniments of this Victorian mode. The beadquer.ers of fashion predict a speedy collapse of the revolt, but whatever may be seen at the more exclusive functions of fashion, it Is improbable that full pastier modes will become general this season. .</p>
        <p>The Suffragists and their Headgear.—The threat of the Suffrsgists to disperse with headgear is not causing milliners much anxiety. Few they say, so deeply con- cerned in the affairs of the nation have time to think a great teal about millinery, and the loss of the Suffragists' custom will not spell ruin to them. So far as the peaceful ones are concerned, the craze for going hatless has practically died out. It has been discovered that the node n spells dusty hair, headaches, eye strain, and last, but by no means least—wrinkles. A hat is cheaper, and far less troublesome it has teen found, than constant shampooing, headache-powder., glasses, and face creams. Further it seems at length to be realized, that a hatless girl or woman in the streets, fiords an appearance greatly lacking in refirement. The Evening Ilead-dress.—From an ivtistio point of view, the foshiocaLle nightcap—ell frilly net, costly lace, and dainty ribbons—is greatly to be nreferred to the tip-to-tate evenicg headdress. lhis latter creation weuld give a mere casual observer the impression that a battle had taken place, and that the wemenkind had been badly wounded about the head. Fashionable heads are more swathed than ever in the evening, and to many are added Lunches of silver grar,s, or eemi-metallic spine-like threads, which lie horizontally above the left ear, like a lance at last. Altogether, an assembly of modish women in full dress presents quite a warlike scene. On the Wearing of Veils.—Veils are never cut of favcur, although they may remain out of fashion for a season or two. Our dresses have been veiled but net our faces, of late. However, veils are returning to popularity. again, probably because they are so becoming --when properly adjusted. The putting on of a veil easily, neatly, and becomingly, is a gift vshich some women receive at birth seemingly; the rest, strive as they will are unable to acquire the art—and it is nothing le s—perhaps because they do not possess the patience necessary to success in this apparently simple matter. These women should abjure the veil, for its effect is often grotesqua in the extreme, and quite fatal to en otherwise becoming toilette. True there are now many aids to the proper adjustment of veil., which those gifted with the knack despise, but even these are sorry substitutes for the real thing. The White Veil.— White veils are to be very popular this summer. The fine white varieties are becoming, but so very fragile that they will probably only last a day or two. The thicker ceilings wash, but they com- pletely hide the wearer's face, although to see through them is an easy matter. Coloured veils are very fashionable and will continue so throughout the summer. ihin white veils with colonred threads, and spots to match the dresses worn with them, will also be seen, FO will black and white ceilings in endless designs and combinations.</p>
        <p>Antique Jewellery.—Quite a notiPeable feature of the ornameots worn at their Majesties' recent Courts, was the quaint jewellery in antique settings that adorned the dresses of many of the great ladies. From this circumstance, it is predicted that old-world pieces which have been hidden of years, will see the light of day again this season. The long chains of smloth beads are once more to be worn, especially iu the even- ing. These chains in rock crystal are very effective, as are also those of amber, and lapis lazuli.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>PRESCRIPiION THAT IS ABSOLUTELY FREE. SOUNDS STRANGE BUT ITS TRUE.</head>
        <p>A PRESCRIPiION THAT IS ABSOLUTELY FREE. SOUNDS STRANGE BUT ITS TRUE.</p>
        <p>How interesting are the conversations one often hears in public conveyances. The following discussion is related as between two workmen in a tramcar in a Midland town " I see Elenry's back to work." "That can't be, as I went to see him only on Satur- day last, and his pains were dreadful. The doctor says he's g,)t Sciatica or Neuritis or something like that, in its „worst form, and may be in bed for months."</p>
        <p>" Then the doctor is wrong, as I saw him as I came from the works, and he told me he never felt better." " Beeipg is believing, but I can't believe it till I see him. What did he do ? "</p>
        <p>" He told me that it was a free preFeription that he cut from a paper' and as my missus is bad with Rheumatism I asked him for it. Here it is: Go to the nearest chunist's shop and get 60 grains of Kephaldol in tablet form. Take two table:a at once, and then one every hour toll pain is gone. Ile says that's what cured him, and if it don't cure the missus he'll stand me a drink on Saturday night."</p>
        <p>The Marchioness of Linlithgow gave birth to twin sons on Sunday, at Hopetown House. All are doing well.</p>
        <p>• Five thousand football enthusiasts marched from Pontypool to Newport (Mon.), on Monday morning, a distance of eight miles, to witness a Rugby match at Newport. They were accompanied by three bands.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN 01 JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN 01 JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>TOO SAD. e. According to the story in last week's Comer," to knock any brains out of Bobl was impossible. After all such men have • There's another nice little tale to the effE4, patient suffering from severe pain in the head brains removed for special surgical treatmeut doctor placed the grey matter in solution, and owner to call round in a few days to have it Meanwhile, ovvirg to carelessness at the slug' brains were thrown out and irrecoverably lot: the unfortunate patient would get along witht-,0 was a puzzle to many, but he had the good 1r elected on the Town Council, so it was all beg the reader to bear in mind that thii is I tell the tale as 'twas told t) me. WHEN LOBSTER IS NOT _LOBSTER.</p>
        <p>Moreover, I should be the last to thus re our own Council ; the case is esaPritil "present company excepted." By-the-bye report of the public analyst, discussed at meeting of that body, served to recall a cow had with a fish hawker in another town a SO I ventured to question him with reference alleged " potted lobster " exposed for sale on and he candidly confcs.ed that there was any lobster about it. Be said it was st?Pl wholesaler precisely in the fotrn in which, being artifically co'ourcd and of the flavour or but consisting almost wholly of bake or myself, in the circumstances, I prefir to as ord. A COFFEE TRICK.</p>
        <p>- The Mayor mentioned that some coffee sBnll be n proved to consist largely of chicory, reminded me that it is iather a commcn pr. tradesmen to whom application is n vide. fo• , t) ask the customer if rure cAree is desirtd; myself deliberately tf sted it by asking coffee," and even then I I are been called ill wl ether I wanted it pure f r mixad. dt mand is in such prccise and definite te such a que,tion is—l had almost written 104 and an insult to one's interigerce, but, i mildly, it is superfluous. In one place 0. miles from Warrington, I knew a dealer wb coffLe at throe prices ; but whatever a custr , for and paid for came out of one and the stywol I knew another who was twice heavily firiw., local P&amp;ice Court for selling margarine as. he got thr( ugh such encrmous quantiticl stantial pr( fus that he evidently cmeidered wl.ile to continue the risk. CONCERNING BUTTER.</p>
