      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Stockton Heath,</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath,</p>
        <p>MOTOR CAUSES OBSTRUCTION.—The Daresbury Petty Sessior s were held at the Hall Cafe, Stockton Heath, on Tuesday morning. Councillor H Roberts was in the chair and the other justices present were :—Messrs G Dakin, J Hepherd,F W Monks, T Baxter and L Greening —Arnold Stubbs, Morningside, Winsford, was charged with having on the 16th IN arch, at Stockton Heath, left a motor car, of which he was in charge, in London- road, for an unreasonable time to the obstruction of the highway.—The defendant did not appear.—P.C. Garner said the car was standing outside the Mulberry Tree Hotel and was in such a position that vehicles could only pass it with difficulty. The defendant was in the hotel, and when spoken to said he was unaware that the car was an obstruction.—The Clerk (Mr Greenall) said the defendant was neither present nor had he sent a letter to say why he was absent.—A fine of lOs and costs was inflicted.</p>
        <p>INDEPENDENT METHODIST CHMICII.—The anniversary of the Bible Class was observed, as in previous years, on Good Friday and proved a very happy occasion. Tea was served to about 180 visitors in the schoolroom and in the evening a meeting vv)s held in the Chapel. Mr. R. Ilenshall presided, and inspiring addresses were given by Rev W Reid and Mr R B Wood. The musical dal tof the programme was much enjoyed. The choir of Brick-street Mission under the conductorship of Mr William Barton rendered four anthems and the quartette party of the choir gave " God is a spirit" with admirable effect. Solos were given by Miss Harriet Howell and Mr Clayton and Mr Clayton's qu3rtette also gave two selections with great taste.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Lymm.</head>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>LYAIM PARtsit Cfluncit.—The offertories at the Parish Church on Sunday and Monday last were for the Rector's Easter offering and amounted to £lOO 18s. Bd. BROOM EDGE PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHURCH-- In connection with the Broom Edge Primitive Methodist Church a public tea and meeting was held on Good Friday. Being a holiday there was a large number of friends who Came from other churches in the Circuit, necessitating a " Second sitting " at the tea provided by the members. After tea was over and the room prepared a service of song entitled The dying Saviour and the gipsy girl ' was rendered by an augmented choir, Mr I-1 Clare being the soloist. Mt W T Moss read the narrative and Mr C Moss supplied lantern illustdttions. The Rev R W B Whiteway pre- sided over the gathering.</p>
        <p>HOLIDAY VISITORS.—Lymra, though hardly figuring as a health resort is usually well patronised at holiday times by people seeking enjoyment. This Easter owing to the restricted train service and absence of excursion fares and also on account of the inclemency of the weather there were fewer visitors to our village than has been known for some years, much to the loss of the caterers. Friday appeared to be the busiest day, especially towards 3 p.m. when a large number of young enthusiasts, both pedestrian and cyclists, arrived from Warrington, and much to their delight the sun shortly afterwards gave them a welcome visit. The rest of the holidays were spent under rough conditions culminating on Monday in a strong gale which executed considerable damage to the roofs and glass houses in the locality. Many of the younger folk of Lyrnm made their way to the Drill Hall on Monday evening to participate in the dance which was held there. Tonks' Band was in attendance and discoursed sweet strains from 7.30 to 12 o'clock. Mr A Clare ably executed the duties of M.C.</p>
        <p>FREE CHURCH SERVICE.—The Free Churches com- memorated Eastertide by holding a united service in the Primitive Methodist Church on Good Friday morn- ing, when the Rev R W B Whiteway officiated. A considerable number of people were present and enjoyed the homely service. Mr Whiteway chose for his text "And the veil of the temple was rent in twain." He spoke of the epoch making day on which the veil was rent and showed how that the rending was symbolical of the removal of everything that stood in the way of man coming before the presence of the Divine. Dwell- ing upon the value of this tearing the veil to humanity, he pointed out the blessings that Christianity had given the world in the past and would continue to give if men would only accept Christ as the Way, the f ruth, and the Life. At the close a collection on behalf of the Free Church Distress Fund for Lymm and district was taken up. It was announced that a special committee bad been formed and that they would appeal for further help if thought necessary. Mr Harry Holt presided at the organ with his usual skill. WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>A pretty, though quiet wedding, took pl Ice on Saturday morning last at the Primitive Methodist Church. The contracting parties were Ernest, third son of Mr and Mrs W T Moss, of Newfield Villa, and Eliz daeth, daughter of Mr and Mrs Leab, of Cuerry-lane. The Rev UW B Whiteway conducted the ceremony. The bride, who was given away by her father, was attended by her sister as bridesmaid ; Mr C Moss, brother of the bridegroom, acted as hest man. The bride as charmingly at tired in a cream costume with hat to match. After receiving much of the usual good humoured wedding attention of the crowd they went off by car en route for Blackpool where they spend their honeymoon. The bridal pair were the recipients of many pretty, useful, and expensive presents. Much interest has been aroused amongst the local Primitive Methodists by the wedding as both bride and bridegroom are connected with their Church, and Mr. Ernest Moss is also the hon. secretary of the Sunday School. • Another Easter wedding was that of Mr. William Myatt and Miss EdiA, Maria Sutton, which took place on Wednesday tellUlson at the Congregational Church. Ihe bride, who was given away by her brother, was becomingly dressed in blue. The Rev RW B Whiteway performed the ceremony.</p>
        <p>Penketh. DEATH OF MRS. SHA.W.—We regret to announce the death of Mrs. Shaw, wife of Mr. W. W. Shaw of Willow Bank, Penketh (assistant headmaster at the Council School), which took place, on Sunday evening, rather unexpectedly. Mrs. Shaw was a native of Wilsden, near Bradford, Yorkshire, the last six years having been spent in Penketh as here she had made a large circle of friends by whom her death is deeply regretted. Special prayers had been made on her behalf at the services last Sunday, but just before the close of the evening service the minister (Rev H Middlewood) said ' he had just been told that their sister had passed away. He said he was sure that they would unite in their deepest sympathy with Mr Shaw and family in their Fad loss. Mr Clatworthy played the Dead March in " Saul " at the close of the service.—The funeral took place on Wednesday afternoon at the Sankey Cemetery. Services were held at the Wesleyan Church, when the Revs J Gunnell, H Middlewood and the Vicar (Rev J It Jones) took part and also at the graveside. The chief mourners were : Mr W W Shaw, husband ; Miss Shaw, daughter ; Mr Hubert and Sidney Shaw, sons ; Miss Wigglesworth sister ;Mr and Mrs Wright, sister to Mrs Shaw, Mrs Kirk and Mr Cowley. Amongst others were :—Mr and Mrs Robinson (Widnes), Mrs Haslam (Great Sankey), Mrs J Atherton, Mr and Mrs T Bell, Mrs Colin Cook, Mrs Dunabin, Mrs Faulkner, Mr end Mrs ',oder), Mrs Gunnell, Mr and Mrs Marshall, Mr and Mrs Moores, Mrs Peers, Mr and Mrs Stephenson, and Mr and Mrs Watson. Wreaths were sent from Mr Shaw and Family, Sister Mary, Mr and Mrs Wright (Bradford), Mr and Mrs Cowley (Ilkley), Mrs Doeds, Penketh Labour Party, Mr and Mrs Robinson, Mr and Mrs Stephenson, Mr and Mrs Marsden (Southport). Flowers were sent by Miss Davies (Frodsham), Mrs Parker and Mrs Davies. The bearers were Messrs 0 Dale, F Davies, W Tucker, J Hughes, M Ray and Eddy Thomas (Widnes).</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL.—In connection with the above school the annual tea party and prize distribution was held on Good Friday in the Wesleyan Sunday school. This event which is looked forward to with great pleasure, commenced by the echolars and friends sitting down to the usual well provided tea which was under the capable management of the Sunday school teachers, some of the senior scholars teing willing helpers. After tea was over a splendid enter- tainment was given, presided over by the Bev II Middlewood. The following scholars taking part Ronald Davies, Ilstrry Faulkner, Reggie tioxley, George Molyneux and Albert Owen, Florrie Bates and ' Florrie Carrington. Action songs and choruses were also given by the scholars. Mr Frank Davies (secretary of the Sunday school) gave an interesting rep rt of the year's work. He pointed out that at the end of the year 1911 the following scholars were on the books : Girls 119, boys 136, making a total 'of 255. The collec- tions towards the upkeep of the Children's Home were £2 10s., a decrease on last year of 9s. Speaking on the several removals which bad taken place during the year he remarked that they much appreci- ated the services of their late minister,</p>
        <p>the Rev F W Kernick, who presided at their annual meeting last Good Friday. But as Methodism removed one good minister it generally sent another to take his place, and this was the case with regard to the Rev H Middlewood. (Applause.) The lady teachers who had removed from the district were Miss Diggory. Miss Irwin and Miss Wilkinson. The following officials gave their reports: Mr H Davies, treasurer; Mr H Carman, sick club; and Mr T Drmsbin, band of hope. Mrs Dodds kindly gave the prizes to the scholars who had made the required number of marks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Stretton.</head>
        <p>Stretton.</p>
        <p>PARJEDIAL TEA AND CONCERT.—This popular annual gathering took place on Thursday evening. About 150 sat down to a splendid tea in the Stretton schoolroom, the tables being beautifully decorated. Afterwards a first-class concert was held before a very large audience. The artistes included Mr Dutton whose impersonations from Dickens were greatly appreciated. The flute solos by the Rev H V Pigot and whistling solos by Mr Willett quite delighted the audience. A pianoforte solo by Miss Ditchfleld and pianoforte duet by Miss Ditchfield and Mr Bostock were rendered in a skilful manner. The gramaphone selections by Mrs Bowers were most pleasing to all. Miss Truslove, who has a beautiful voice, charmed the gathering with her songs "When the heart is young " and "The Jewel Song " from " Faust," which secured her two encores, respond- ing with " Little Irish Girl " and " Coming through the Rye." The Stockton Heath Mandolin, Banjo and Guitar Band, under the direction of Mr C Whitley, bad a splendid reception for their selections "Skipping rope dance " and Gold diggers' march " for which they were deservedly encored. Mr Isherwood's humorous songs created great merriment. The accom- paniments were played by Mr Taylor who fulfilled his duties in a most capable manner. The Rev the Hon C F Cross thanked all those who had taken part and helped to make both tea and concert a success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>OCTOGENARIAN'S FATAL BURNS.</p>
        <p>Alice Hilton, aged 80. of 41, College-street, St. Helens, died early on Sunday morning from burns received a week ago. Mrs. Hilton had been confined to her room for the past twelve months owing to having broken her right leg. On Saturday, the 30th ult., after having had her tea she heard a knack on the floor of the bedroom, and when her son-in-la's went up he found that her hair and shawl were on fire. She explained that she was placing a teacup on the mantelpiece when her shawl caught fire. CHILD KILLED BY A TRAMCAR. A shocking tramway accident occurred about seven o'clock on Saturday night in Robins-lane. Sutton, St. Helens, when a boy of four years, named Robert Bate, the son of a moulder, of 13, Oxley-street, was run over and killed. The tramcar, in charge of a driver named Frederick Webster, was proceeding from St. Helens Junction towards the town when the child ran out of Oxley-street on to the tramlines after his hoop. Before the driver could pull up the child was knocked down and went under the car. A crowd of men who were close by immediately set to work to lift the car and rescue the child. They got a number cf planks, and their efforts were so vigorous that they lifted the car right over on its side. It toppled over and smashed one of the windows. %\hen the child was picked up he was quite dead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>GOOD FRIDAY PROCESSION.</head>
        <p>GOOD FRIDAY PROCESSION.</p>
        <p>The procession organised by the Bishop of Southwark, which took place on Good Friday, was a most impressive sight. Over 3,000 clergy and laymen taking part. There were three divisions, which assembled at the Elephant and Castle, and then pro.</p>
        <p>ceeded to the Cathedral for a short service. Hymns were sung by the processionists as they walked alDng the route. Our picture shows the procession passing flown the Borough High Street.</p>
