      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL. FINANCIAL ECHO OF THE STRIKE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Widnes Town Council wag held on Tuesday, the Mayor (Alderman F Neil) presiding. On the recommendation of the Highway Committee, it was decided to go on .vith the making of Ditten- road, and the Finance Committee were instructed to make application to the Local Government Board for sanction to borrow £2,600, the estimated cost of the work. It was also decided to make Fairfield-road, and the chairman (Councillor Harapson) said, as the road abutted on the park, one-half of the cost would be borne by the Counci.4 In the minutes of the Gas and Water Committee it was mentioned that the estimated cost of the strike to the gas and water department was £2,700. The General Purposes Committee reported that the attempt to have a separate county court district for Widnes had not been successful. Alderman Owens, proposing the minutes of the Finance Committee, referred to the increase of 2d. in the rates, and said the increased amount to be met was equal to a 30 rate; but a penny now produced £623 instead of £6ll.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians, was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr Spruce, presiding. The Clerk to the Cuckfleld Union (Sussex) forwarded a copy of the following resolution. That the Board of Education be requested to take necessary measures on an early date to bring about the rendering obligatory. Under the Elementary Education (Defective Children) Act, 189, in so far as such Act authorises the provision by Education Authorities for the education of such children ; that the members of Parliament represent- ing parishes in this union be asked to mit port this request and that all Boards of Guardians in England and Wales be invited to join this Board in making it and to invite their respective members ,of Parliament to give their support to the movement. The Board decided to support the resolution.</p>
        <p>_ . ASSISTANT OVERSEER. The Chairman of the Kingswood Parish Meeting wrote to the effect that Mr Herbert Boyer, of Manley, bad been elected assistant overseer in place of Mr T C J Davies, resigned. NEW COLLECTOR. , . .</p>
        <p>Mr Morris submitted the report of the committee selected to go into the matter of the appointment of a collector for the Guardians and stated that they had come to the unanimous decision that the work ought to be done in the clerks office and they recommended that Mr Warburton, of Mr Ashton's office, be appointed as collector. Mr Warburton thanked the Guardians for the con- fidence they had placed in him and said ha would do his best in the work he had been chosen to do.</p>
        <p>TIIANKS TO THE CHAIRMAN. A hearty vote of thanks to Mr Spruce for his able conduct in the chair throughout the year concluded the meeting.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL..</p>
        <p>- - The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council was held at Dutton Tuesday, Mr. Wright occupying the chair. DUNHAM BILL. The Cheshire Rural Council wrote to the effect that they were anxious to provide a water supply for the village (If Dunham Hill and they would be glad if the Runcorn Council would ascertain on what terms the supply could be given from the Helsby Waterworks to about 50 houses. The matter was reff rred to the Parochial Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>ROAD MATTERS.</head>
        <p>ROAD MATTERS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk of the 171-eIS-hy-Va-riSh--eatincil again called the attention of the Rural Council to the condition of the road known as the Gullet, flelsby, leading through Higher Alvaney, the road to Robin Hood Lane. The Parish Council were strongly of the opinion that if the Rural Council could not see their way to put the road in good order, they would take out a disused surface water drain which wis bare and broken and make good the surface by so doing. This matter was left with the Hikhways Executive as was also the condition of the Five Crosses Top-road. WESTON SANITARY MATTERS. At the last meeting of the Weston Parochial Corn- mittee a letter was read from the Local Government Board stating that the local icqulry would be held on the subject of the Council's applicAion for sanction to borrow the sum of £2,400 for the conversion of privies into water closets and other sanitary improvements in the parish. The letter continued that the Bmrd would not be prepared to allow longer than seven years for that portion of the loan which would have to be repaid by the Owners before sanctioning the borrowing of such part of the sum required for other sanitary work uncon- nected with the conversion, the Board would require to be satisfied that the District Council bad tiken pro- ceedings in a sufficient numbsr of cases to justify the raising cf a loan to carry out the works in general of the owners. It was resolved "that so much of the application to the Local Government Board as relates to the loan for other sanitary work unconnected with the conversion of the privies, be withdraw 1, and that the application be so amended as to ask for £1,664 for the conversion of the privies." The minutes were approved. TII H; cnAlnat. At the conclusion of the meeting Mr Linaker, in moving a vote of thanks to Mr Wright, who had served his term of three years as chairman of the Council, said be was sure they would agree with him when he said that they were all thankful to Mr Wrght for the able manner in which he had fulfilled the duties of chairman</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>RELIEVING DISTIcESS AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Fairhurst and his staff of agents con- tinued their efforts for the relief of the distress in the Earlestown district occasioned by the coal strike, last week. On Good Xciday morning 400 children received a hot cross bun aarone pint of new milk each, and on Saturday morninr -another 120 pints of new milk were distribuhed to the most deserving cases. It is hoped that the settlement of the miners dispute will relieve the auxiety which has been so prevalent in this district. This seconi gift of milk from Mr Homer, uuddington, Cheshire, makes a total of 90 gallons of milk which he has given. This is a typical example of the generosity which has met the appeals of the workers, and which has considerably assisted poor people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>SHOP ASSISTANTS.</head>
        <p>SHOP ASSISTANTS.</p>
        <p>MR. SEDDON AND THE NEWTON DIVISION.</p>
        <p>The annual delegate meeting of the National Amalgamated Union of Shop Assistants, Warehouse- men, and Clerks was resumed on Monday in London. Mr. J. Donald, of Aberdeen, president, was in the chair. The conference discussed at length in private the question of Parliamentary representation. It was agreed that the Union should continue the Parlia- mentary candidature of Mr. J. A. Seddon in the Newton division of Lancashire. The committee, who had con- sidered the matter pointed out, however, that the continuance of the Union's political activities would depend upon adequate voluntary contributions for Parliamentary purposes being given. If these were not forthcoming the committee would be compelled to reconsider the whole question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>WORD OF A WOMAN</head>
        <p>THE WORD OF A WOMAN</p>
        <p>Women will take another woman's word. They know that a woman would not trifle with their sufferings. Thus it comes about that hundreds of thousands of women have given their trust to Mrs. Lydia E. Pinkham and enabled her to bestow the greatest benefit that woman can receive. This is no exaggeration, for it is not only from bodily pain that Mrs. Pinkham has been enabled to rescue her suffer- ing sisters, but from the ordeal of medical examination and mutilating operations. Mrs. Pinkham first began to interest heraelf in women's ailments when a vegetable compound which she prepared made a num- ber of her immediate neighbours well, but the fame of her cures spread so far that sire was obliged, for humanity's sake, to prepare it in large quantities and allow chemists to sell it. Mrs. Lydia E. Pinkham 's Vegetable Compound can now be obtained anywhere, or will be sent by her representatives, post paid, for 2s. Pd. or 4s. 6d. (very large size). Her book of private counsel to women will be sent (to women only) free of charge by writing to The Lady Manager, c/o Lydia E. Finkham Medicine Co., 42a, Hanipatead Road, London, N.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>NEW AMERICAN NATIONAL HYMN.</head>
        <p>A NEW AMERICAN NATIONAL HYMN.</p>
        <p>Mrs. G. Jowett, formerly of Warrington, but now living in Oklahoma City, U.S.A. sends us a contribu- tion which she thinks will interest local politicians. It iB called : A NEW AMERICAN NATIONAL HYMN. My country tis of thee, Land of small liberty, Of thee I sing Land of race suicide, Land of the law defied, Every town and country side A grafter's ring. My native country, thee, Of the divorce court free, Thy name why love? • Why love thy tariff bills. That rob the man who tills, While Wall Streets coffer fills The top above? Let music swell the breeze, Praising monopolies, A mournful song. Let every mucker rake, Let all the trusts partake, Rise, price of ham and steak. Oh, Lord, how long. Our Senate, 'tis to thee Friend of plutociacy Te thee I sing. Long may our land be bright With Standard Oil's great light Ye trusts with all your might Tax everything. z</p>
        <p>4 Mr. 3. E. Berry, Eeeretary of the Birmingham Trades Council, was killed in a motor cycle accident in aat city on Sunday.</p>
