        <p>4, Pool Road, Smethwick, March 26th, 1911. Dear Sir,—l must give you praise and congratula- tion upon your wonderful remedy ; Eade's Gout and Rheumatic Pills. I have SUFFERED FOR THE LAST NINE- TEEN YEARS UN lOLD PAIN and agony and have taken dozens of bottles of doctors' medicine, but NOTHING HAS GIVEN ME SO Mum RELIEF AS YOUR PILLS! • - •</p>
        <p>I shall advise all sufferers that I know to try them: I know it will he for their own good. You may make what use you like of this letter for the truth requires no study. I remain, yours faithfully, CHAS. FLE rcirEa. EADE'S GOUT PILLS Are perfectly safe in their action, eliminate all injurious matter, and are highly restorative. Sold everywhere in Bottles, Is. Id. and 2s. 9d. or sent post free for stamps or P.O. by the propriet s,,ORGE BADE, 232, Goswell road, London. EUMArrIC PILLS</p>
        <p>I-1</p>
        <p>DEATH OF AN OLD WARRINGTON LADY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Interesting Career.</head>
        <p>Interesting Career.</p>
        <p>The death has occurred, at the ripe old age of ST years, of Mrs. Dobson, of Liverpool-road, Bank Quay, who was born in Bank-street, on April 14th, 1525. She was a granddaughter of Joshua Phillips, well known and deservedly honoured in his day as a pioneer of the Quaker Methodist Church. He followed the calling of chairmaker and had his home in Bank-street. Among the possessions of the late Mrs. Dobson were some chairs made by her grand- father, and they were treasured as riches of a good and remarkable man. The brother of Joshua Phillips was Peter Phillips, also a chai maker, whose home was in Ship Yard, and who is famous as the guiding spirit of the Quaker Methodist denomination. She was a remarkable woman in many ways, and her phenomenal memory and love of music and poetry drew even the stranger into conversation with her. Her father, John Broadhurst, master fustian cutter, of Hopwood-street, was a fine tenor singer, and was one of the first members of the Musical Society. Her brother was also a gifted musician, and, with the late Herr Praeger, for many years a well-known figure in War- rington, accompanied Jenny Lind on her first tour to the United States. She often sp ke with a vividness and clearness which was surprising of the outbreak of cholera in Wart ington, in 1833, ia hen pitch barrels at Market-gate and other points were burnt and of incidents connected with the reign of William IV., and the crowning of the late Queen Victoria. She well remembered the old watch who did duty for police, who called the hour and state of the weather at night ; " " Leigh driving through from Sankey in a cart rigged up like a ship, and wearing ruffle shirt, knee breeches and three cornered hat,and pigtail. The launch of the Taylenr and Startled Fawn ; the Tayleur being a boat of 4,000 tons, which was wrecked shortly after (IT the coast of Ire- land ; the old Warrington Bridge, which was made of wood ; the building of the present bridge ; and the erection of Walton Arches, which at that time was supposed to be a great under! taking. In those days Arpley Fields was a very pretty spot, in which the salmon fisher loved to roam. She had a great deal of interesting information to impart with reference to the annual regatta, which was I looked forward to for months by young and old ; the gay colours of the contestants being very much in evidence. She spent many happy days at the old Church-street Fair, and the Georgian Fair held at Bank Quay, which could only boast of about forty houses. She was a great reader of good English literature and poetry and her never-failing kindness, straightforwardness and generosity endeared her to everyone with whom she came into contact. Her grasp of the burning political and social questions was surprising in one of her age, in fact she retained all her facilities unimpaired up to the last.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORICENGNIEN'S ASSOCIATION (N.A. CENTRE). During the Easter holidays several sneers journeyed to the Gowy and Actoa Bridge. Others were waiting for better conditions day by day, the most favourable day being Sunday. But owing to the railway curtailing their service there was no train from Chestsr on Sunday evening, so those who wale waiting for the change in the weather were greatly disappointed to miss the best during the holidays.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>GOWY.</head>
        <p>THE GOWY.</p>
        <p>The members who visited the Gowy complained of the bitterness of the gale on Saturday and Monday, angling being out of the question, especially on Mon- day. Those who tried were compelled to st nod at least ilve yards away from the troams. Pike, trout and eela were creeled but not of great quantity. Those who did the best were Messrs Mcflale, Singleton, Henshave and Hewitt. Henshaw's trout turned the scale at 83Z.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>ACTON BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>ACTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Several merabers visited the length, but as far as the sport went with them the results were nil. We advised members the other week to wait for warmer weather before trying the mails. One member reports seeing roach. As regards the flat fish in this length, there is ne doubt they will be tried for later on in the seasan. . .</p>
        <p>-- Chester was not visited and the same applies to the Shropshire Union canal. Owing to the Easter holidsys club night was post- poned aid will be held on Monday next, April 15th. AS.</p>
        <p>GOLF.</p>
      </div>
