      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>BEECHAM'S PILLS.</head>
        <p>BEECHAM'S PILLS.</p>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Messrs R T Fairclough (in the chair), F Monks, C S Burgess and T Pemberton. THEFT OF A BicycLE.—John Moorcroft, 10, Clegg- straet, was charged with having stolen a bicycle valued £5 on the 31st March.—The Chief Constable statad that Henry Appleton, a revitter in the employ of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company, Ltd., left his bicycle in the firm's stable on the date in question. About an hour and a half afterwards he discovered and the bicycle was missing and the work men were watched as they left work, and Moorcroft was pointed out to the prosecutor. Moorcroft then appfared to have gone borne, and he subsequently returned the cycle, which had been altered in almost every detail, to its place in the stables. The cycle had been re-enamelled and the saddle and handle bars were changed, and it would appear that the prisoner had spent the whole of one afterno( n altering it. When taken to the police station he said he did not intend to steal the machine, but he was afraid to take it back at first. There was a second charge against the prisoner for stealing a spirit level and a hammer from the works during the last six months.—Moorcroft, who pleaded guilty to both charges, was sent to prison for two months in the first case and a month in the second— three months in all.</p>
        <p>MOND. \y.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Smethurst 13olton and Alessrs RoLerts, C S</p>
        <p>Burgess, Wr Furness and G Pilling. A,SAIII.T ON POLICE.—John Burn=, 28, King-street, crane driver, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in King-street, and also with assaulting Sergeant Jonn Stuart. He pleaded guilty. The cfficer stated that at 4.30 on Saturday afternoon he received a complaint that the prisoner bad stolen some ice-cream and assaulted the dealer. He went to his house and found the rrisoner sitting in a drunken condition by the fire. He refused to give any account of the ice-cream incident and when asked for his name be flew into a passion and the officer turned to leave to avoid a disturbance but the prisoner ifollowed him to the door and assaulted him. In self-defence he (witness) closed with him and caused an injury to the prisoner's eye.— Burns, who appeared with his head swathed in bandages was fined ss. and nests.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr C White in the chair), Alder- man Smethurst, Conncillor C Jackson Holmes and</p>
        <p>Mr W 'l' VVillia;ns.</p>
        <p>MOTHER AND SON.—THEFT FROM A BUILDER.— Margaret Livingstone, 7, Manley-street, was charged with stealing or receiving a quantity of timber, value Gs Gd, the property of Charles Longshaw.—The Chief Constable stated that the prosecutor was a retired builder of 91, Folly-lane, and kept a cutting shop at the rear cf Cartwright-street and Bostock-street. The wood was stolen from the cutting shop on Satur- day by one of the prisoner's children, who got into the shop and did a considerable amount of damage and wrecked two water closets, of which the wood was part. Ile estimated amouat of damage done was 265., includ- ing thirteen knives. From information received the police made inquiries and Detective Reddington searched the prisoner's house and found the wood in a cupboard. The prisoner said the wood had been there a long time but there was some new mortar on it, and further questioned she said she did know it was there and did not know it was stolen. The boy had been interviewed and had admitted that the woman knew about the wood. They had not charged the boy on account of his tender age be being only nine years old. —Prisoner was ordered to pay 20s. including costs or in default 14 da3s imprisonment.</p>
        <p>TUURSDAY.—Before Messrs A J Jolley (in the chair), W Roberts, E Woods and Dr Naden. A MEAN THEFT.—Joseph Wild, Cheshire Cheese Yard was charged with stealing by means of a trick the sum of 10d.,the money of Mr CE Hawthorn,district represen- tative of the" Liverpool Echo." The Chief Constable stated that on Tuesday evening two boys were employed by Mr Hawthorn to sell newspapers and the prisoner stopped them and said he had been sent by the office boy to collect the money. He got eightpence from one and twopence from the other. He had tried to obtain money from a third lad but was unsuccessful. Prisoner said be would not have done it if his father had not been out of work.—The Chief Constab!e described prisoner as a lazy, skulking rascal.—Wild was sent to prison for one month. ASSAULT ON OLD WON AN.—Edward Hill, 30, Jockey- street was charged on the 7th mat, with assaulting Beatrice Gornley, his mother-in-law, who is 74 years of age.—Prosecutrix stated that on Sunday night prisoner vas thrashing his wife and she.witness took him to task about it. tie struck her and knocked her over a table and also hit her on the head. Prisoner who said prosecutris had no right to interfere between him and his wife, was sent to prison for two months.— Hill was further charged by his wife with being an habitual drunkard. Mrs. Hill said be came home every night in the week so drunk that he could not walk.—Prisoner : I have not been drinking lately. I have no money to buy coal never mind drink. —Continuing, Mrs Hill said he struck her when she went to bed at night. They had been married eight years and had three children. She had had as little as 2id per day from him.—The magistrates granted a separation order with an allowance of 7s 61 per week. iNGENIOUS LAVATORY THEFT.—Thomas Jones, of no settled address.was charged on remand with stealing from a cash box in the public lavatory in Palmyra- square, the sum of twopence, the property of the Warrington Corporation. He was also charged with stealing between the 25th March and April Ist, the sum of Is 7d from the same souroe.—The Chief Constable stated that for some weeks prior to the apprehen- sion of the prisoner complaints had been received in regard to the shortage in the takings from the lavatory. Various marked coins had been placed in the box and these had been taken. It was found necgssary I to have a systematic watch kept on the lavatory with the result that on Wednesday Detective Metcalfe found the prisoner on the premises attempting to open the box. Other charges were preferred against the prisoner and he admitted them. He had a bunch of keys in his possession which be had used to open the boxes.—Prisoner who said he was very sorry and was hard up at the time, was committed to prison for two months.</p>
        <p>--, ,- Flin the 0 1 4 ~._ Oill !ITS -i_ • , di" .. ~</p>
