      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <p>Evelyn-street School Headmastership. The Teachers' Case. Sir,—As the School Management Committee has recommended the appointment of a non Warringtonian for the headship of Evelyn-street Council School thereby causing great disappointment and consider- able unrest amongst the local teachers, I should like to set forth the teachers grievance as concisely as possible, and leave the pubic to judge from the facts of the case, whether their own teachers are being treated with that consideration which they are so often told they deserve. A letter has recently been rent to the Secretaries of Teachers' Associations, asking the conditions of pro- motions under their authorities with the follwiog result : (I.) Authorities restricting headships to their own teachers by (a) Definite rule to that effect, or (b) Preference given (and alwa3s kept). or (c) Advertising vacancies, hut always giving preference. (d) Never advertising. West Ham, London County, Bristol, East Ham Ipswich, Middlesex, Hull, Middlesborough,Manchester, Grimsby, Leam ington, Sheffield, Rot herb am ,Le icester,. Newcastle, Southampton, Leeds. Stoke, Wood Green, Leyton Erich, Portsmouth, Heading, Birmingham, Derby, Swindon, Shipley, Halifax, Bradford, Liver- pool, Nottingham, Bedford, south Shields, Coventry, Croydon, Gateshead, Blackpool. Gloucester, Tyne- mouth, Widnes, Sunderland. Stockport, Aston Manor, Rom for d, Workington, Enfield, Northampton, Maccles- field, Huddersfield, Salford, Brighten, Crewe, Notts County, Rochdale, Bootle, Colchester,</p>
        <p>Oldham. 11. Authorities giving preference to their own t3achers : Gloucestershire, Exeter, Bolton, Darlington, Southend, Eccles, Wilts, Norwich, West Bromwich and Wednesbury, Acton, Surrey, Eastbourne. Carlisle, Cheshwick, Southport, Accrington, Staffordshire, Hereford. 111. Of the autLori!ies which were declared " open " the majority were authorities over counties, health resorts,aud county towns, having administration almost entirely over small echools only. The advertisement columns of the " Schoolmaster " have been carefully scrutinised and an analysis made of all the advertisements for head teachers for a period of five successive weeks. The table prepared showsl Irony clearly that almost all the " good " headships are restlicted to teachers in the locality where they arise, and only relatively poor posts are advertised, the salary of the Lead teachers being no bettor and often not as good as that of a senior assistant in a large town. The position being realised amongst the local teachers is that to become a Wartington assistant, is to become an assistant for ever. The solution is evident. Why not institute a scale of promotion like other large towns? Surely there is a headmaster of one of the smaller schools capable of taking charge of the new school. Why not promote him and let another take his place? By continuing this policy all along the line, many teachers would benefit by one new school, and a largo number would gradually be becoming fitted for the final position as head of a new council school. There is one other matter I desire to place before you. Having decided that a Warrington teacher shall not have the headship of Evelyn-street school, the local authority rules that no Warrington teacher, who has been doing gcod work in the town, shall have a position in the boys' department even as an assistant If any Euch teacher should apply, it has to be treated as a "new appointment" and not as a "transfer." Consequently that teacher has to start at the minimum provided by the scale, possibly sacrificing £3O or £4O per year. Why should this condition obtain? I have made inquiries throughout Lancashire and have been unable to find any other authority with any such rule. These authorities transfer teachers without loss of salary. Surely it is only common justice that one of your own teachers who has been working for years under adverse conditions in one of the older buildings of the town should have an opportunity of being able to go to a new school without being penalised to such an extent. Fin ther- more, every one knows the difficulties which have to be overcome during the first few years in a new school. Again every town has its own peculiar educational requirement. Would it not be more beneficial, and more in accordance with the educational interests of the town to transfer some of the older assistants, than to flcod the school with a head teacher for outside, and a staff of strange, young and inexperienced teachers ?—Yours etc , GEORGE NEW BURN, Hon. Sec., Warrington Certificated Class Teachers Association. April 12th, 1012.</p>
