      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Tariff Reformers who Shirk the</head>
        <p>Tariff Reformers who Shirk the</p>
        <p>Issue.</p>
        <p>Sir.—The point emphasized in Mr. Parker's Free Trade letter of last week is one felt by many Free Traders. I refer to his complaint that Tariff Reformers make no serious attempt to meet Free Trade arguments. Tariff Reformers pirouette round the Dumping Exhibition argument that the import of foreign made golds, such as clocks, which could he made here at greater cost, involve a reduction of the amount of work, which is presumed to be the same as welfare. That is the sum total of Mr. Harold Smith's arguments on the Tariff question, since Warrington has known him as a politician, and has on two or three occasions been challenged by local gentlemen, through the press, to state his position on Tariff Reform more specifically, but although be has acknowledged that he is familiar with their arguments, he Eti 1 states his views in copybook formulas and stock phrases, that do not even tcuch the fringe of the question. He never gets any further, nor does he examine the arguments, which Free Traders believe, prove the Tariff Reform inferences to he inconclusive because based on false conclusions. And so we go on year by year and get no forrader. Dumping Exhibitions are organized, thousands of letters written and speeches made, but the opposing parties seldom if ever get to grips. Rightly or wrongly Free Traders think that Tariff Reformers thirk the issue and believe that they can never make any substantial advance until they abandon their narrow outlook. Free Traders hold that what a nation wants is , more, cheaper, and better commodities to minister to individual comfort, that the more of these it has the larger the share of each individual and that work is only the means to procure these, and if it fails in this. work is an irksome burden. The Paris Committees ita,..184S are said to have to'd the employees et the National Work Shop for whom they had undertaken to find work, to bottle the Seine. What Tariff Reformers seem to U 9 to aim at only differs in degree from this absurdity. They want us to do for ourselves what others are willing to do for us better and cheaper, and in exchange for something that we can make better and cheaper than they can. A protective tax would attract men from the industries in which they can produce more to those in which they produce less, but it would also compel those who continue in the old industries to pay for the support of these who enter the new. Why will not Tariff Reformers face the difficulties ?-ICo.irs G.T. Latchford Apt il 10th, 1012.</p>
        <p>Dlsendowment. Sir,—Your correspondent, :qr. Westbrook has asked me a number of questions through your columns which I only regret were not asked at the meeting wire ray remarks were made, but bricfly I will try</p>
        <p>- and reply. I based the question" Would the Church trouble over the question of disestablishment if it were not one of disendowment also," on the statements that one continually hears that the Church of England is not " Established " any more than any other denomination and that on the other hand those who are supporting "Disestablishment " are guilty of robbery and spolia- tion and would not support it were it not that they elpected to gain by the Disendowment of the Church. Personally, my objection is to any religions sect, of any sort whatever, being considered the " State " church, on a good many grounds, not the least being that the control of any section of the Church of Christ by a secular body, who have a right to say what doctrines shall or shall not be included,is not good for the religious life of that Church.</p>
        <p>The question of Disendowment is another matter altogether. I stated that Nonconformists, as such will not benefit by such Disendowment any more than any and every citizen, with or without religious belief, and including Church of England members equally berefitting ; as the Welsh Bill, I believe, provided that funds received from the Endowmeots should be used</p>
        <p>in certain ways by the various Town Councils. It is, perhaps, a matter on which Mr. Westbrook and I would disagree, as to whether the endowments of a Church holding Roman Catholic doctrines, and prior to the Reformation, could consistently he considered ,as endowments of the Church of England and her teachings as they should be according t her Articles to-day. As far as my own opinion is concerned, I should not cavil at the endowments remaining as they are, and I think many Nonconformis's vv, uld agree with me. It is not and never has bee i the desire of those seek- ing Disostablishment to lay hands on what ths Church considers as her prop.rty for the endowment cf Nonconfcrmity, and such charges are grossly unfair. Doubtless, as Mr. Westbrook states, there are also numbers of Norcmformist endowments included in Trust Deeds of various kinds, but I do not like the system 'which tire down gifts for the propagation of doctrines ages after the givers are dead, in spite of ever increasing light and knowledge. I am sorry that what I intended as an olive branch has been grasped at as a nettle.—Yours sincerely, J. JARMAN.</p>
        <p>78, Manchester-road, Warrington, April 4, 1912. Mr. Townroe's Explanation.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln fairness to the many good Liberals, who have shown me much kindness in Warrington, may I refer to a sentence, wrenched from its context by a Manchester newspaper, an commented on by you in your issue of the tith inst. As quoted, it gives the impression that I violently attacked Liberalism. I have too much respect for the traditions of Gladstone, and other great and noble men of your party, to indulge in reckless abuse. The sentence in question belonged to an argument respecting certain aspects of the i.resent policy of the Government—aspects which I showed by quotations would have met with Mr. Gladstone's disapproval, and which lately have been disowned by many prominent Liberals. you, I sir, have always treated me kindly and jut-tly, and you will be the first to recognise that an abridged report of a speech often misrepresents an argument. This letter is not an attempt to enter into political controversy, but simply to make clear to you and other Wallington friends, that I spoke and always hope to speak, not in a spirit of hitter partisanship, but as one who, while he strongly attacks part of a policy, yet sincerely , believes that there are problems, both of defence and social organisation, which can only be solved by the moderate men of the great patties, working in co-operation for the good of the community.—Yours &amp;c., B. S. TOW NROE.</p>
        <p>Nottiogham, April Bth, h)l2.</p>
      </div>
