      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April, 13, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April, 13, 1912.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHURCH LADS BRIGADE.-FIELD DAY.</p>
        <p>LIEUT.-COLONEL APPLETO</p>
        <p>On Monday the 2nd. (Warrington) Battalion, Liverpool Regiment, and the 3rd. (AltriPcham) Ballalion Chester Regiment held a mimic 6gbt in the neighbourhood of High Legh Hall, through the kind- ness of Colonel Legh and his agent the Hon. J. E Cross. The scheme was that Lancashire and Cheshire were two s.parate States and on April 2nd the Cestrians had declared war in Lancashire with the result that the latter State were rapidly preparing to check the enemy's advance. To this end the Wallington Battalion C.L.B. mobilized at the Cheshire side of the Swing Bridge at Latchford on Eas'er Monday and advanced on High Legh commanded by Lieut.-Colonel Appleton. When about a mile from the Hall the advance guard</p>
        <p>.. • . ,</p>
        <p>retreated towares Wigan, and, though they were warmly pursued by the victorious army, they entered the town of Wigan, and Lund quarters there, %%toile the puliamentary foice3 were obliged to pitch their tents in the fields. The next morning the Duke c tinned his retreat to Warrington, but being still pursued his tro Ts made a resolute stand at a pass near Winwick, which they maintained for many hours The courage and discipline of the force under Crom- well, at length overcame hie resistance. "By the blessing of God" says Cromwell's dispatch " our men charged home upon them, and beat them from their standing, where we killed about 1,000 of them, and took about 2,000 prisoners, and prosecuted them home to Warrington town, where they passed the bridge. In our good old town General Bailey, to whom the Duke had confided the command of th:s division of his army, sued for a capitulation to which Cromwell consented, on the bard condition that he should sur- render himself and all his officer 3 and soldiers prisoners of war, with all his arms, ammunition and horses upon quarter for life. This condition was either not faithfully fulfilled, or it was liable t some exception for a number of the combatan•s were hanged in a field near the scene of the fight, the field being after- wards called " Gallows' Croft." A large stone was also fixed near the p'ace as a monument of the victory." I wonder if any of your readers, Mr. Editor, can locate" Gallow's Croft:" Trusting this short account of the " Battle of the Pass of Winerick " may prove interesting to your readers and encourage a more widespread study of our local biery. —I remain, yours &amp;c., EDWARD STEEL. " Etona," Marsh House-lane, \Warrington.</p>
        <p>Another Bridge. Sir,—Don't you think the present time is an opportune one for the widening of the "Black Bear" Bridge? The stone-work, &amp;c., from the old town- bridge could be transferred to the "Black Bear" Bridge, and well utilized there; thus a much needed improvement would be effected with a great saving ta the ratepayers.—Yours LATCHFORDIAN. Aptil 7th, 1912.</p>
