      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Battle of Win wick. A Chapter of Local History.</head>
        <p>The Battle of Win wick. A Chapter of Local History.</p>
        <p>Sir, The more one reads of the history of Warring- ton and district the more interesting it becomes .I have during the past week been read ng an old history of Winvgick and an acc unt is there given of the stand made by the Scots in 1618 against the victorious army of Oliver Cromwell who, with great loss on both sides, beat them from the ground. Trusting that it may prove as interesting to your readers as it has proved to myself I here summarize the account. " Although the King was a prisoner, and his cause for the present utterly hopeless, another attempt was made from the north, about the middle of the year 1618 to light up the ember 3 of civil war. General Langdale had assembled a considerable force in the Northern counties, near the Scotch kontier,and an army was collected in Scotland, to he placed under the command of Duke Hamilton, which was intended to penetrate into England, and to retrieve the fortunes of the Stuarts. The approach of this force produced a deep sensation in Lancashire and repeated meetings of the county were called to provide the necessary force to secure the persons and property of the inhabitants, as well as t.,) resist the invaders. All eyrs were now turned on this part of the Kingdom and reports were made from Lancashire to the Parliament almost every sitting, indicating the approach of the enemy, and the St ,to of preparation in which the county stood for their reception. A committee was appointed in Parliament, under the designation of "The Committee of Lancashire" and by this body it was ordered that four colonels of foot and two of horse, with their regiment, then in readiness in the northern part of the country should jL in Major-General Lambert. Of this Lancashire force Colonel Ashton was appointed commander-in-chief and at length it was announced that Duke Hamilton had entered England at the head of 17,000 troops, and that it was his purpose to march from Cumberland along the Western coast, while General Langdale advanced in a parallel direction from Northumberland, keeping to the East, but so arranging their.plan of operation, as to be able to form a speedy junction. when, by the nature of the service it might be required The force under General Lambert was found wholly insufficient to nicest this torrent from the North, aud Oliver Cromwell was ordered by Parliament to march into Lancashire, to reeiat their further progress. These orders he promptly obeyed, and advancing from orkshire on ths 16th of August to Hodder Bridge, he was joined by Colonel Ashton with the Lancashire force, and crossed the Ribble. In the meanwhile an Irish force under General Monroe had joined the Scotch troops. Oa the morning of the 17th Cromwell marched towards Preston. Here the two armies met in the evening, and after a sanguinary battle of 4 hours, fought in the fields, Duke Hamilton's troops began to give way and were charged through the streets at the point of the bayonet. On the night of the 18th the Duke, at the head of the wreck of his army,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>IN COMMAND OF THE WARRINGTO.</head>
        <p>IN COMMAND OF THE WARRINGTO.</p>
        <p>of the Lancashire lads, under Captain F, rd, came into contact with what rapraseiredthe advance guar I of the Cheshire force, and after reinforcements were sent the Cheshire advance was obliged to retire. The Chester a ivanc3 guard was represented by Cyclist Sergeant Lowe and six Territorial cyclists of the 4th South Lancashire Regiment (who were well supplied with blank ammunition), t •g?.ther with the High Legh Boy Scouts. After dinner, which was engaged on the road side,the Batt di m accompanied by the Boy Scouts, who were now to fight with the Lancashire force, marched to High Legh Hall and had parade service in the private chapel, the Rev C H Edwards officiating and the choir consisting of Boy Scouts, the organist bAng drawn from their ranks. At O active opara-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>WARRINGTON BATHS EXTENSION. Laying of the Foundation Stone.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BATHS EXTENSION. Laying of the Foundation Stone.</p>
        <p>An interesting little ceremony took place on Thursday afternoon when, in the presence of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee, Alderman Bolton (chairman) laid the foundation stone of the extensitm of the Corporation baths in Legh-street For some unexplained reason, efforts were made to keep the event as secret as possible and even those members of the Town Council who do not happen to be members of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths committee, were not invited to attend. The Mayor (Councillir Dr. Joseph) presided and formally invited Alderman Bulton to lay the atone. Before performing the cexemony the alderman was the recipient of a handsome silver trowel presented by the Borough Surveyor (Mr. T. Lcmgdin) on behalf of the builders (Messrs. J. Dolan and Son) and himself. The stone, which is of Dalbeattie granite, bears the inscription in raised letters : " Warrington Baths extension. Alderman William Bolton, J.P., chairman, 19l?." _ _</p>
        <p>The Ma.yo-r-w- aa thanked for presiding, on the pro- position of Alderman SNIETIIUR`nT, seconded by Alder- man BoLTN.</p>
        <p>In responding, His Womsuir said he sincerely hoped the extension might he of real g od to the rising generation. Whatever differences they might have with r, gard to the mode of education as at present carried on—and he must say he did not altogether approve of the manner in which education was conducted—there could be no doubt that the lessons taught at the baths would be of great value. There were two things which he bad always thought every man, woman and child should learn—one was how to swim and the other was how to gargle. They were both connected with fluids. (Laughter.) lie did not mean gargling in the sense that some people terhaps understood it, but gargling in cases of throat affections. It might seem a singular mixture to ally swimming with gargling, but if they thought of it there was something in the suggestion. (Hear, hear). When the extension is completed, Warrington will be able to boast of one of the largest and most efficiently equipped puhl:c baths in the Kingdom. The additions will comprise :—Men's swimming bath (second class), ladies' swimming bath (first class), and sixteen new private baths. The new men's bath will be 77ft. Sin. long by 35ft. wide with a depth of from 3-t. at one end to 51t. at the other. Forty-nine dressing boxes, two wash baths and a douche bath will be provided. The ladies' bath will be :Art. long by 35ft. wide and will also be from 3 to 51t. in depth. There will be two wash baths and a douche bath in this section of the building. The ladies' swimming bath , already in use will be retained as a ladies' second class bath. The men's first class plunge !)eith and the boys' plunge bath will be utilized for their present purposes, so that, all classes of bathers will be adequately catered for. With the provision of sixteen new slipper baths for men, the accommodation for private bathing will be re-arranged as follows f-2.2 slipper baths, 2 slipper and vapour baths, and one upright bath for men, 5 slipper baths and one slipper and vapour bath for ladies. The sides of the plunge baths will be of brick work in cement, faced with tiles, and the ponds will be made water tight with natural 'asphalt. The whole of the floors will be in terrazo. The dress- ing boxes will be formed of re-inforced terrtzo and cast iron frames, the absence of woodwork increasing the durability of the structures and facilitating clean- liness. It is proposed to fix new machinery in the existing laundry and a new Lancashire boiler in the present boiler house,coupled up to the existing Cornish toiler and connected with caloriaers in a subway for supplying hot water to the slipper baths and connected with the swimming ponds for the purpose of heating the water. The present entrance hall will be divided by the ticket office and a screen, so as to give a separate entrance for each sex. The estimated crst of the extension is £8,317. The plans have been prepared by the Borough Surveyor (Mr. T. Longdin) and the contract is in the hands of Messrs. J. Dolan and Son. It will not be possible to carry out the work for the coming bathing season but it is expected that everything will be completed for next year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>',BATTALION ON MONDAY.</head>
        <p>',BATTALION ON MONDAY.</p>
        <p>tiona were resumed and the Warrington Battalion tried their hand at holding up their Cheshire enemies. Daring the conflict the signallers were of great use, as were also the cyclists connected with the C.L.8., and the Ambulance squtds showed that their training was not in vain. The honours of the daywere divided. Prior to the homeward march Lieutenant-Colonel Appleton thanked Colonel Legh and his agent, the lion J E Cross, for their kindness in allowing the brigade to 1140 the lark. Tne Hon J E Cross, in respinding on behalf of the Coloael and himself, said it gave them great pleasure to entert tin the C.L.B. Although Colonel Legh was unab'e to see them that time be knew that he would be pleased to entei tain them again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>HOME RULE COMING.</head>
        <p>HOME RULE COMING.</p>
        <p>MR. ASQUITH INTRODUCES HISTORIC MEASURE.</p>
        <p>The long-cherished aspirations of the great majority of Irishmen for a system of self- government are at last within measurable distance of realization.</p>
        <p>In a memorable speech lasting almost exactly two hours, the Premier introduced in a crowded House of Commons on Thurs- day the third Home Rule Bill. The chief features of the measure are as follows : 'lrish Parliament to consist of:— Irish Senate 4O Members Irish House of Commons 104 Members</p>
        <p>Senate to be nominated by the Imperial Government for six years, afterwards by the Irish executive. Ulster will have 59 Members in the Irish Commons, Leinster 41, Munster 37, Connaught 25, the Universities 2. In case of dispute both Houses to sit and vote together. Irish Parliament cannot make laws affect ing</p>
        <p>Crown, Peace or War, Army, Treaties, Navy, Treason, &amp;c. Cannot deal with Land Purchase, or (at present) Old Age Pensbns, State Insurance, the R.1.C., or the P.O. Savings Bank. After six years, however, the constabulary will be automatically transferred to care of Irish Parliament; P.O. Savings Bank may be transferred at the wish of the Irish Parlia- ment after ten years; central of pensions and insurance may be demanded at end of one year.</p>
        <p>An imaginary " peril " of Laws passed by Irish Parliament will be prevented by Veto powers possessed by (1) Irish Appeal Court; (2) Lord Lieutenant; (3) Judicial Committee of Privy.Council,and (4) Imperial Parliament.</p>
        <p>. . No religion can be endowed by the Irish Parliament,no ieligious tests can be imposed and there can be no interference with the validity of mixed marriages.</p>
        <p>The imperial Government will continue to collect the taxes for the United Kingdom and will hand over to Ireland the cost of all Irish services and also £500,000 a year to be reduced annually till_it_reaches £200,000. _</p>
        <p>Ireland will control the postal service, and has the power to reduce or discontinue for Ireland any Imperial tax. Taxes so reduced will mean an equivalent diminution in the sum transferred from Westminster.</p>
        <p>Ireland may add also to certain taxes.</p>
        <p>Growth in receipts, in revenue other than taxes imposed by the Irish Parliament, will go to diminish and extinguish the grant from Westminster. Thus Ireland will get the full benefit of her economies, but if she is extravagant she will have to find the money. Irish M.P.'s at Westminster (now 103) will be reduced to 42.</p>
        <p>Mr. Asquith emphasised the facts that this was "only the first step in a larger and more comprehensive scheme" of devolution, that the supremacy of the Imperial Parliament was amply secured, and that no minority had anything to fear. "We cannot, and will not," he exclaimed in a significant passage, "admit the right of a relatively small minority, when every care is taken to safeguard their special interests, to veto the verdict of the vast body of their countrymen."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>YACHTING FATALITY. FUNERAL OF THE VICTIMS.</head>
        <p>YACHTING FATALITY. FUNERAL OF THE VICTIMS.</p>
        <p>Touching scenes were witnessed at the funeral of the victims of the Warrington yachting fatality at the Cemetery on Thursday afternoon. At the interment of Walter Warburton, the late captain of the Zinnia the chief mourners were : Mrs Warburton (widow), John, Walter, Charles, Sydney, Arthur, and William (sons), ellie (daughter), and Sister Lena. Others present were: MN It Warburton and sons, Robert, Ernest and Alfred, Mrs H Warburton and sons Henry, and William and daughter, Minnie, Mr and Mrs John Whittaker, Mr and Mrs W Charnock, Mr and Mrs G Baxter, Mr W M Clew and William, Messrs T and A Boscow, Messrs A Warburton, 4, Smith and C Schule representatives Warrington Sail: ing Club, Messrs T Bennett, W Knowles and W H Smalley, representing Fiddlers Ferry Sailing Club, Messrs C T Cox, W Patterson and W Warburton representatives Richmonds Gas Stove and Meter Co. Ltd. and Mr A Rustage of the yacht Zone. In addition to wreaths from the family floral tributes were sent by :—Mr and Mrs H Davies, Mr and Mrs W Horton, Mr and Mrs G Baxter, Mr and Mrs John Whittaker, Mr and Mrs 0 Baxter, Mr and Mrs A Mulhall, Mr R Whittaker, Mr and Mrs W Charnock, Mrs J Deane, :Mr and Mrs H Mickey, Mr and Mrs W M Clew, Mr and Mrs H Davies, Misses E and M Warburton, Warrington Sailing Club, Fiddlers' Ferry Sailing Club,Tranmere Sailing Club,Richmonds and Co. The last sad rites were performed by the Rev T Vickers, who also officiated at the funeral of James Edward Crooks the other victim of the fatality.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>DEATH OF COUNCILLOR W. ROGERS.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF COUNCILLOR W. ROGERS.</p>
        <p>Deep and widespread regret is felt at the death of Councillor William Rogers who passed away at his residence, the Feathers Hotel, Bridge-street, on Sunday afternoon. The end was not unexpected, for Mr. Rogers had for a long time been suffering from a serious heart affection and complications developed so rapidly that when Dr. Reginald Smith, his medical adviser, called into consultation Dr. IReynolds, the well-known specialist, of Man- chester, the case was f und to be hopeless Mr. Rogers had borne his trying illness with great fortitude and the sympathy of his fellow-townsmen found expression in the many inquiries as to his con- dition which were received each day from all sections of the community. Mr. Rogers had had a very active and useful career, and took a prominent part in political and municipal life. He was born at Grossendale, Liverpool, in 1854 and was in his 59th year. In early life he served for a short spell in the Army and afterwards joined the Lancashire County C ,ostabulary, being stationed successively at Atherton and Penketh. He was a zealous and cooscientious officer and established him- self in the E s teem of the inhabitants of those townships. Nearly thirty years ago Mr. Rogers came to War- rington and took up the business of a licensed victuoller. He had been licencee of the Feathers Hotel for the past seven years. He entered municipal life as an Independent candidate in 1895, when he defeated the late Mr. S. J. Beaman in Bewsey Ward by 33 votes. In 1898 he was opposed by Mr. Thomas Malkin and the first poll ended in a tie, Mr. Rogers being successful on a second vote. He continued as a representative of the ward until 1905, when he was defeated by Councilor Skelland by 132 votes. In 1907 Mr Rogers contested the Town Hall Ward in the Conservative intere,t and obtained a majority of 130 over Mr 0 T Wilding. In 1910 he retained the seat, polling 30 votes more than Mr Firth Lindley. Mr. Rogers was an active member of the Paving and Sewerage, Cemetery, Markets, Gas, Water, Electricity and Tramways and Bridges Committees. Mr. Rogers was a staunch Conservative and took a keen interest in local political warfare, although he never held office in the party. He was a Churchman, and as a boy was a chorister at Grassendale Church. Since coming to Warrington he had identified himself with the Parish Church, and for some years acted as a sidesman.</p>
        <p>An institution particularly dear to his heart was the Aged People's Treat Committee, of which he was a vice-president, and nothing gave him greater pleasure than to contribute to the enjoyment of the old folk. It is significant of his kind-heartedness that during his last illness he constantly asked about the condition of the poor people who were suffering through the coal sttike• In 1893 when a similar dispute in the Linea- shire coalfields caused great distress in the borough, Mr. Rogers devoted himself whole-heartedly to relief work in the Bewsey disttict. Mr. Rogers was a Freemason, being associated with the "Gilbert Greenall " Lodge. He was also a member of the Executive Council of the Warrington Licensed Victuallers' Association. Much sympathy has been extended to Mrs. Rogers and family in their sad bereavement. Amongst those sending letters and telegrams were Sir Gilbert and Lady (Arsenal], Mr. Harold Smith, MP. and Mr. and Mts. W. Peter Rylands.</p>
      </div>
