      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>An impressive demonstration of respect was witnessed at the funeral which took place at the Warrington Ceire;ery on Wednesday atternoon. Representatives of public bodies and many organisations with which the late Mr Rogers was identified, were present in large numbers and the streets were lined with sympathising onlookers. The cortege was healed by a detachment of the Borough polies, under the direction of Inspector Foster, and next came members of various Masonic lodges. Following the hearse were two open carriages filled with beautiful floral tributes. The fltst part of the service was held in the Parish Church, and here the Mayor and members of the , Corporation were in waiting. The procession was met at the west door by the choir and clergy who preceded it up the centre aisle, while the opening sentences of the burial office were chanted. The Easter hymn "Jesus lives ! no longer now can thy terrors death app II us" was sung and it was followed by Psalm xxxix, and the solemn funeral hymn " Now the labourer's task is o'er." The clergy taking part in the 'service were the Rect. r (Rev. Canon Willis), Rev II V Pigot (Rector of Grappenhall), and the Rev ti Percival (vicar of St. Peter's) As the coffin was borne out of the church, Mr. F. AL Derby, whci,was at the organ, played Chopin's Funeral M arch. the griveside the last rites were performed by the Rector. The chief mourners were :—First coach, Mr Harry Rogers and George Rogers (sons), Lieu erant fourth and Mr T B Allen (sons-in-law), Mr Ackroyd Rogers (brother) ; second conch, Mrs Baines, Miss Edith Rogers (daughter.), Miss Mitchell, Mrs Hill (of Grassendale) ; third coach, Mr William Hill (brother- in-law), Mr John Lee, Dr Smith, Nurse Haynes ; h witch, Mr Hill, Mr John Matthews brothers-in-law), Mr W Jones (nephew), Mr C Davenport (cousin), Coun- cillor George Archer ; fifih coach, Mesri J Hatton, Mercer, Humphreys (Osvvestry) and Travis. The representatives of public bodies and organiza- tions were as follows :</p>
        <p>The Corporation : The Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph), the Deputy Mayor (Alderman Forshaw), Alder- men A Bennett, John Evans, James Evans,W Pierpoiot, J Smethurst and D Tinnion : Councillors IV H &amp;Mien, T Birtles, J Broadhurst, T Chantler, E Cunnington, J Dickenson, J Halliwell, P. Ilenshall,C Jackson Holmes, Dr McKee, J Sankey, J Skelland, 'l' S Steel, 0 Toole ; Messrs A T flallaway (deputy town clerk), W H Wood- cock (assistant trrmurer), T Longdin (borough surveyor), A M Ker (assistant surveyor), M Nicholls (chief constable), F' V L Mathias electrical r ngineer), Dr J C Hibbert (medical orticer), J Gray water eoginef r), F Taylor (secretary of the gas department), W S Howard (cashier of the gas department), W 1' Flood (inspector of nuisances), and F Turner (superin- tendent of the Fire Brigade). Councillor 11 Roberts r..te regretting his inability to attend. The Parish Church : Councillor J 0 Craik and Mr E Isherwood (wardens), Councillor J T Locker and Mr S Taylor (sidesmenl. Freemasons: Gilbert Greene!! L,dge, 1250, Bros W Price, W.M.. W H J Jenkins,P.P.G.R., P.M., Edward Rose, P.P.D.G.C.R. (secretary), J Lee, P.P.G.M. (Cheshire), 11 F Greenhougb, P.P.0.5.8., A F 0 Potter, P.M , Thomas Silo wan, P.Z., C Monn- W Bainbridge, Thos. Healing, P.M., W Hodgkinson, P.M., W 11 Ainsworth, S.D., Joseph Bailey, J W G Craddock, P.M., P.S., C Bird, J Melbourne, and F J Dell, ty ler ; Lodges 148 and 249, Bros Thomas D Amos, P.M., P.P.G.0., and John Muskett ; Lodge 3287, Bros vtilliam Chalmers, George Newburo, A.S' J Waldron, 1.P.M.,J H Heywood, J.W.; Blair Lodge 875, Bro John Brown; Rock Lodge 1289, Bros Arthur Denham, T Part ridge, James Earle and W Cherry; Lodge 2155, Bro Frederick T Bennie, ; Volunteer Lodge 2)05, Bro J W Plinston; Lodge 1713, Bro J Stoprord; Lo (go 2325, Bro F A Jenkins; Lodge 1102, Bro Isaiah Goldstone; Lodge 2963, Bro 11 Smith, S.W.; Lodge, 1381, Bro Thomas Cooper. Warrington Conservative Association : Messrs W Peter Itylands' E A White, George H Smith, Herbert Hayes, Joseph Hill, It Sumner, Joseph Hatton, George Allen, and It Done. Junior Conservative Association : Mr E James Hall. Conservative Club, Sankey-street : Messrs H Collin, and T Mason.</p>
        <p>—Junior COD-fervative Club : Mr W L Williams. Aged People's Trent Committee : Mr W Maddock. Licensed Victnsllers' Asrociation : Messrs R Shack- lady, Robert Walker, N Hankicson, S Wakefield (repre- sented by Mr Davies), J Jennings, H Ashton, J E Shaw, H Williams, and E Lockett (secretary). Beer, Wine and Spirit Sellers' Association : Messrs Peter Lawless (chairman), T SAngster (vice-chairman), W Longmore (treasurer), W J Shaw (secretary), Boscow,</p>
